aemorrhage, eclampsia\ndelirium delirium new agitation with fluctuating attention altered awareness pain retention constipation infection inattention plus altered awareness/arousal are the key separators. usually precipitated by acute illness surgery injury retention constipation pain or medicines. sepsis hypoxia hypoglycaemia stroke intracranial bleed alcohol/benzo withdrawal urinary retention faecal impaction. sepsis hypoxia stroke withdrawal haemorrhage eclampsia why it fits new agitation with fluctuating attention altered awareness pain retention constipation infection new agitation with fluctuating attention altered awareness pain retention constipation infection subjective bodily torment and urge to move dominate. mood or psychosis may be absent. fluctuating attention confusion sepsis haemorrhage pre-eclampsia/eclampsia metabolic derangement psychotic or manic syndrome with abrupt change from baseline in the postpartum period. cognitive impairment fluctuating attention fear pain aggression is often fear- or belief-driven rather than predatory. acute change fluctuating course inattention inattention plus altered awareness/arousal are the key separators. usually precipitated by acute illness surgery injury retention constipation pain or medicines. must-not-miss sepsis hypoxia hypoglycaemia stroke intracranial bleed alcohol/benzo withdrawal urinary retention faecal impaction. sepsis hypoxia stroke withdrawal head injury medication toxicity. haemorrhage eclampsia bedside question inattention plus altered awareness/arousal are the key separators. usually precipitated by acute illness surgery injury retention constipation pain or medicines. subjective bodily torment and urge to move dominate. mood or psychosis may be absent. psychotic or manic syndrome with abrupt change from baseline in the postpartum period. aggression is often fear- or belief-driven rather than predatory. inattention plus altered awareness/arousal are the key separators. usually precipitated by acute illness surgery injury retention constipation pain or medicines. inattention and fluctuation dominate. psychotic content is often fragmented or changeable. usually chronic internally generated but experienced as alien embedded in broader psychotic syndrome. formal thought disorder plus broader psychotic syndrome and functional deterioration. acute and fluctuating with inattention delirium medical/toxic cause . immediate action in older or medically unwell patients assume delirium until proved otherwise cannabis and stimulants can closely mimic primary psychosis reassess once partially cleared escalate early to medical/neurology review if red flags are present consider mri eeg csf and autoimmune testing when the syndrome is atypical clarify positive symptoms negative symptoms cognition and functional trajectory violence in psychosis is often defensive from perceived threat mixed irritability is easy to underestimate one of the commonest reversible drivers of acute violence agitation in dementia is frequently delirium pain or environment-driven recognising the interpersonal trigger can prevent the next escalation do vitals bgl sats attention testing collateral and medication/substance review early use 4at in most adult settings and icu tools where appropriate quiet hypoactive delirium is commonly missed investigations neuroimaging ct/mri eeg lumbar puncture / csf studies blood glucose vitals and oxygen saturation neuroimaging ct/mri eeg lumbar puncture / csf studies mimics / overlap primary psychosis dementia severe depression catatonia intoxication post-ictal state stroke medication overdose stimulant intoxication primary psychosis dementia severe depression catatonia intoxication post-ictal state stroke medication overdose akathisia inner restlessness pacing shifting inability to sit still after antipsychotic/antiemetic/antidepressant bipolar mania restless overactivity with decreased need for sleep pressured speech irritability impulsivity acute psychosis fearful suspicious internally preoccupied command-driven pacing/guarding/aggression stimulant intoxication after methamphetamine cocaine synthetic stimulants heavy caffeine or stimulant crash panic shaking pacing fleeing startle breathlessness terror after trigger/conflict/reminder catatonic excitement severe purposeless excitement impulsive/bizarre overactivity odd postures switching between excitement/shutdown postpartum psychosis rapid-onset confusion bizarre beliefs hallucinations marked mood lability agitation severe insomnia days-weeks after birth postnatal depression low mood guilt anhedonia anxiety poor bonding hopelessness impaired functioning after birth psychomotor agitation akathisia bipolar mania / mixed state acute psychosis clinical hinge subjective bodily torment and urge to move dominate. mood or psychosis may be absent. perinatal acute psychiatry postpartum psychosis postnatal depression perinatal ocd / intrusive infant-harm thoughts clinical hinge psychotic or manic syndrome with abrupt change from baseline in the postpartum period. blood glucose vitals and oxygen saturation neuroimaging ct/mri eeg lumbar puncture / csf studies violence in psychosis is often defensive from perceived threat mixed irritability is easy to underestimate one of the commonest reversible drivers of acute violence agitation in dementia is frequently delirium pain or environment-driven recognising the interpersonal trigger can prevent the next escalation do vitals bgl sats attention testing collateral and medication/substance review early use 4at in most adult settings and icu tools where appropriate quiet hypoactive delirium is commonly missed treat precipitants and use non-drug care first reserve antipsychotics for severe distress or immediate risk do not stop at behavioural labels in older or medically unwell patients assume delirium until proved otherwise cannabis and stimulants can closely mimic primary psychosis reassess once partially cleared escalate early to medical/n