unctional trajectory do not just ask any voices clarify form content controllability conviction prominent visual or tactile hallucinations widen the toxic/organic differential acutely unwell or older patients with new hallucinations need a delirium lens first think lewy body dementia early when visual hallucinations and fluctuation coexist acute change in an older person is delirium until proved otherwise lewy body disease is a classic trap in late-life psychiatry the medication list is one of the highest-yield late-life investigations even modest recent changes or cumulative burden can precipitate delirium","sourceRole":"clinical_reference","access":"public","validationStatus":"unverified","sourceStatus":"review_due","sourceLineage":[],"publicationVersion":"59d4ba2f7f6ea28494d965575a44514bd60607ee9d2349aa005a215335518eda","contentHash":"1ee36c8cc8fe21ee2585e1b8805a7f7f37a71f727366ab4acdba04f48ae79efe"},"renderPayload":{"slug":"delirium","title":"Delirium","status":"emergent","subtitle":"New agitation with fluctuating attention, altered awareness, pain, retention, constipation, infection","clinicalHinge":"Inattention plus altered awareness/arousal are the key separators. Usually precipitated by acute illness, surgery, injury, retention, constipation, pain, or medicines.","safetySnapshot":{"summary":"Sepsis, hypoxia, hypoglycaemia, stroke, intracranial bleed, alcohol/benzo withdrawal, urinary retention, faecal impaction.","tags":["Sepsis","hypoxia","stroke","withdrawal","haemorrhage","eclampsia"]},"sections":[{"id":"why-it-fits","title":"Why it fits","summary":"New agitation with fluctuating attention, altered awareness, pain, retention, constipation, infection","items":["New agitation with fluctuating attention, altered awareness, pain, retention, constipation, infection","Subjective bodily torment and urge to move dominate. Mood or psychosis may be absent.","Fluctuating attention, confusion, sepsis, haemorrhage, pre-eclampsia/eclampsia, metabolic derangement","Psychotic or manic syndrome with abrupt change from baseline in the postpartum period.","Cognitive impairment, fluctuating attention, fear, pain","Aggression is often fear- or belief-driven rather than predatory.","Acute change, fluctuating course, inattention","Inattention plus altered awareness/arousal are the key separators. Usually precipitated by acute illness, surgery, injury, retention, constipation, pain, or medicines."],"tone":"fit"},{"id":"must-not-miss","title":"Must-not-miss","summary":"Sepsis, hypoxia, hypoglycaemia, stroke, intracranial bleed, alcohol/benzo withdrawal, urinary retention, faecal impaction.","items":["Sepsis","hypoxia","stroke","withdrawal","head injury","medication toxicity.","haemorrhage","eclampsia"],"tone":"warning"},{"id":"bedside-question","title":"Bedside question","summary":"Inattention plus altered awareness/arousal are the key separators. Usually precipitated by acute illness, surgery, injury, retention, constipation, pain, or medicines.","items":["Subjective bodily torment and urge to move dominate. Mood or psychosis may be absent.","Psychotic or manic syndrome with abrupt change from baseline in the postpartum period.","Aggression is often fear- or belief-driven rather than predatory.","Inattention plus altered awareness/arousal are the key separators. Usually precipitated by acute illness, surgery, injury, retention, constipation, pain, or medicines.","Inattention and fluctuation dominate. Psychotic content is often fragmented or changeable.","Usually chronic, internally generated but experienced as alien, embedded in broader psychotic syndrome.","Formal thought disorder plus broader psychotic syndrome and functional deterioration.","Acute and fluctuating with inattention = delirium (medical/toxic cause)."],"tone":"question"},{"id":"immediate-action","title":"Immediate action","summary":"In older or medically unwell patients, assume delirium until proved otherwise Cannabis and stimulants can closely mimic primary psychosis Reassess once partially cleared Escalate early to medical/neurology review if red flags are present Consider MRI, EEG, CSF, and autoimmune testing when the syndrome is atypical Clarify positive symptoms, negative symptoms, cognition, and functional trajectory","items":["Violence in psychosis is often defensive from perceived threat","Mixed irritability is easy to underestimate","One of the commonest reversible drivers of acute violence","Agitation in dementia is frequently delirium, pain, or environment-driven","Recognising the interpersonal trigger can prevent the next escalation","Do vitals, BGL, sats, attention testing, collateral, and medication/substance review early","Use 4AT in most adult settings, and ICU tools where appropriate","Quiet hypoactive delirium is commonly missed"],"tone":"action"},{"id":"investigations","title":"Investigations","summary":"Neuroimaging (CT/MRI), EEG, Lumbar puncture / CSF studies","items":["Blood glucose","Vitals and oxygen saturation","Neuroimaging (CT/MRI)","EEG","Lumbar puncture / CSF studies"],"tone":"test"},{"id":"mimics-overlap","title":"Mimics / overlap","summary":"Primary psychosis, dementia, severe depression, catatonia, intoxication, post-ictal state, stroke, medication overdose, stimulant intoxication","items":["Primary psychosis","dementia","severe depression","catatonia","intoxication","post-ictal state","stroke","medication overdose"],"tone":"overlap"}],"related":[{"id":"akathisia","label":"Akathisia","likelihood":"possible","note":"Inner restlessness, pacing, shifting, inability to sit still after antipsychotic/antiemetic/antidepressant"},{"id":"bipolar-mania-mixed-state","label":"Bipolar mania","likelihood":"possible","note":"Restless overactivity with decreased need for sleep, pressured speech, irritability, impulsivity"},{"id":"acute-psychosis","label":"Acute psychosis","likelihood":"possible","note":"Fearful, suspicious, internally preoccupied, command-driven pacing/guarding/aggression"},{"id":"stimulant-intoxication-withdrawal","label":"Stimulant intoxication","likelihood":"possible","note":"After methamphetamine, cocaine, synthetic stimulants, heavy caffeine, or stimulant crash"},{"id":"panic-trauma-hyperarousal-dissociation","label":"Panic","likelihood":"possible","note":"Shaking, pacing, fleeing, startle, breathlessness, terror after trigger/conflict/reminder"},{"id":"catatonic-excitement","label":"Catatonic excitement","likelihood":"possible","note":"Severe purposeless excitement, impulsive/bizarre overactivity, odd postures, switching between excitement/shutdown"},{"id":"postpartum-psychosis","label":"Postpartum psychosis","likelihood":"possible","note":"Rapid-onset confusion, bizarre beliefs, hallucinations, marked mood lability, agitation, severe insomnia days-weeks after birth"},{"id":"postnatal-depression","label":"Postnatal depression","likelihood":"possible","note":"Low mood, guilt, anhedonia, anxiety, poor bonding, hopelessness, impaired functioning after birth"}],"currentPresentation":["Psychomotor Agitation","Akathisia, Bipolar mania / mixed state, Acute psychosis","CLINICAL HINGE: Subjective bodily torment and urge to move dominate. Mood or psychosis may be absent.","Perinatal Acute Psychiatry","Postpartum psychosis, Postnatal depression, Perinatal OCD / intrusive infant-harm thoughts","CLINICAL HINGE: Psychotic or manic syndrome with abrupt change from baseline in the postpartum period."],"investigations":["Blood glucose","Vitals and oxygen saturation","Neuroimaging (CT/MRI)","EEG","Lumbar puncture / CSF studies"],"immediateActions":["Violence in psychosis is often defensive from perceived threat","Mixed irritability is easy to underestimate","One of the commonest reversible drivers of acute violence","Agitation in dementia is frequently delirium, pain, or environment-driven","Recognising the interpersonal trigger can prevent the next escalation","Do vitals, BGL, sats, attention testing, collateral, and medication/substance review early","Use 4AT in most adult settings, and ICU tools where appropriate","Quiet hypoactive delirium is commonly missed","Treat precipitants and use non-drug care first","Reserve antipsychotics for severe distress or immediate risk","Do not stop at behavioural labels","In older or medically unwell patients, assume delirium until proved otherwise","Cannabis and stimulants can closely mimic primary psychosis","Reassess once partially cleared","Escalate early to medical/neurology review if red flags are present","Consider MRI, EEG, CSF, and autoimmune testing when the syndrome is atypical","Clarify positive symptoms, negative symptoms, cognition, and functional trajectory","Do not just ask \"any voices\" — clarify form, content, controllability, conviction","Prominent visual or tactile hallucinations widen the toxic/organic differential","Acutely unwell or older patients with new hallucinations need a delirium lens first","Think Lewy body dementia early when visual hallucinations and fluctuation coexist","Acute change in an older person is delirium until proved otherwise","Lewy body disease is a classic trap in late-life psychiatry","The medication list is one of the highest-yield late-life investigations","Even modest recent changes or cumulative burden can precipitate delirium"]}},{"logicalId":"differentials:diagnosis:delusional-disorder","logicalDocumentId":"098c9606-75da-5c96-a160-196da83708ab","logicalChunkId":"f63eaf83-5233-5ab9-9789-08db2cdfb9fa","normalizedText":"Differential diagnosis: Delusional disorder\nSingle/circumscribed non-bizarre delusion, preserved function outside it\nClinical hinge: Often fixed, systematised, accompanied by hallucinations, thought disorder, or negative symptoms.\nTags: Jealous/persecutory/erotomanic violence risk, stalking, treatment refusal., Stalking, violence, severe treatment refusal.\ndelusional disorder delusional-disorder single/circumscribed non-bizarre delusion preserved function outside it often fixed systematised accompanied by hallucinations thought