m","notes":["Official title and availability checked 17 July 2026","Archive guidance remains a prototype reference aid and requires local clinical/legal governance review","Use the current approved PDF or PSOLIS form; do not modify approved form content"]},"tags":["Form 13","Electroconvulsive therapy","Mental Health Act 2014"],"catchments":["Western Australia"],"catalogueLabel":"Form 13","navigatorQuery":"Form 13 Statistics about ECT Electroconvulsive therapy The official register lists this form, but it must be obtained from the OCP. Check the register and local governance before proceeding.","source":{"label":"Office of the Chief Psychiatrist WA — approved MHA 2014 forms","status":"Source checked","url":"https://www.chiefpsychiatrist.wa.gov.au/laws-and-rights/legislation/mental-health-act-2014-forms/","reviewed":"Official register checked 17 July 2026","notes":["Contact OCP monitoring to obtain or submit this form","PSOLIS is the preferred completion method where available"]},"catalogPayload":{"id":"form-13","form":"13","name":"Statistics about ECT","category":"Electroconvulsive therapy","purpose":"The official register lists this form, but it must be obtained from the OCP. Check the register and local governance before proceeding.","maker":"Only an appropriately authorised person under the Mental Health Act 2014 and the approved form instructions.","involved":"Confirm the required recipients, support persons, records and local PSOLIS workflow on the current approved form.","threshold":"Confirm the statutory criteria and completion instructions on the current approved form before use.","clock":"Confirm any time limit, expiry or review point on the current approved form and local policy.","destination":"Confirm any required place or destination on the approved form.","authorises":"Only the action or record expressly described by Form 13 and the Mental Health Act 2014.","doesNotAuthorise":"No action beyond the current Act, approved form wording and the maker’s lawful authority.","before":[],"parallel":[],"after":[],"copies":"Confirm notices, copies, handover and filing requirements on the approved form.","documentationStem":"","traps":[],"safetyPearl":"Open the current official source and confirm authority, timing, notices and filing requirements before completion.","sourceNote":"Official title and availability checked against the Office of the Chief Psychiatrist register.","aliases":[],"searchTerms":[],"riskLevel":"medium","indexedTerms":[],"legalNote":"Use only the current approved form or PSOLIS pathway. This catalogue is a reference aid, not legal advice, and does not replace the Act, form instructions or local governance.","practicePearls":["Treat the approved form and current legislation as authoritative."],"preUseChecks":["Open the current official form or PSOLIS workflow before use.","Confirm the maker is appropriately authorised and all statutory criteria are met.","Confirm identifiers, date, time, signatures, notices, copies and filing requirements."],"actSections":[{"section":"201","title":"Statistics about ECT","summary":"Where a mental health service performs electroconvulsive therapy, the person in charge must report to the Chief Psychiatrist in the approved form as soon as practicable after each month ends. The report covers the number of people whose ECT course was completed or discontinued that month, broken down by children, voluntary patients, involuntary patients and supervised persons (both those detained at an authorised hospital under the CLMI Act and those treated as a condition of a community supervision, interim, extended, leave-of-absence or interim-disposition order), with the child counts for each. It must also state the number of treatments in each course, how many were emergency ECT under s 199, and details of any serious adverse event that occurred or is suspected — including premature consciousness, anaesthetic complications, an acute and persistent confused state, muscle tears or vertebral column damage, severe persistent headaches, or persistent memory deficit. A course counts as completed in the month its last treatment was performed, and as discontinued in the month the decision to stop was made.","reviewStatus":"drafted"}],"availability":"contact_ocp","officialRegisterUrl":"https://www.chiefpsychiatrist.wa.gov.au/laws-and-rights/legislation/mental-health-act-2014-forms/","officialTitleCheckedAt":"17 July 2026","archiveGeneratedAt":"2026-07-13T16:02:29.961824Z"}}},{"logicalId":"forms:form-1a","logicalDocumentId":"e2c51ac2-7896-5cfc-90b3-788cf0534465","logicalChunkId":"4f23e72b-10c1-5655-a826-028b21d1b40b","normalizedText":"Form: Referral for examination by a psychiatrist\nUse when a person needs referral for examination by a psychiatrist after assessment.\nRoute: Parallel: 1A attachment | After: 5A, 6A, 6B\nEligibility: Reasonable suspicion that involuntary treatment order criteria may be met, with illness features, risk, capacity and least restrictive reasoning documented.\nReferral: Before use: confirm the assessment basis, examination place, referral expiry and any linked detention or transport form.\nLocation: Western Australia\nBest use: Confirm the referral assessment, destination/place and expiry before any downstream detention, transport or treatment step; referral is not itself treatment, detention, transport or force authority. Use only the current approved form or PSOLIS pathway where available; only appropriately authorised people should complete MHA 2014 forms. Authority is limited to the Act sections, signature block, time limits and wording on the approved form.\nPrimary contact: Form 1A Official PDF\nTags: Form 1A, Referral and detention, Mental Health Act 2014, referral, psychiatrist exam, 1a referral, assessment referral, capacity, threshold, destination, expiry, transport, force, refused\nCatchments: Western Australia\nreferral for examination by a psychiatrist form-1a use when a person needs referral for examination by a psychiatrist after assessment. parallel 1a attachment after 5a 6a 6b reasonable suspicion that involuntary treatment order criteria may be met with illness features risk capacity and least restrictive reasoning documented. official wa mental health act 2014 form before use confirm the assessment basis examination place referral expiry and any linked detention or transport form. western australia confirm the referral assessment destination/place and expiry before any downstream detention transport or treatment step referral is not itself treatment detention transport or force authority. use only the current approved form or psolis pathway where available only appropriately authorised people should complete mha 2014 forms. authority is limited to the act sections signature block time limits and wording on the approved form. form 1a form 1a referral for examination by a psychiatrist referral and detention use when a person needs referral for examination by a psychiatrist after assessment. within 48 hours 72 hours referral practitioner treatment referred examination place time date within 48 hours 72 hours form 1a official pdf office of the chief psychiatrist wa approved mha 2014 forms source checked official register checked 17 july 2026 form 1a referral and detention mental health act 2014 referral psychiatrist exam 1a referral assessment referral capacity threshold destination expiry transport force refused western australia high risk referral and detention official pdf official public pdf https //www.chiefpsychiatrist.wa.gov.au/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/form1a mha2014 public.pdf current ocp source official forms register https //www.chiefpsychiatrist.wa.gov.au/laws-and-rights/legislation/mental-health-act-2014-forms/ check current availability clock / review usually 72 hours from referral within assessment window 48h cue made by / authority mp or amhp where act permits psychiatrist examination only criteria / threshold reasonable suspicion ito criteria may be met document illness risk capacity least restrictive act sections authority under the act 26 31 36 37 41 42 purpose use when a person needs referral for examination by a psychiatrist after assessment. authorises referral for examination by a psychiatrist. does not authorise treatment detention transport restraint seclusion or force by itself. parallel 1a attachment after 5a 6a 6b copies and filing use approved form pathway include confidential attachment only where required. documentation stem assessed at time/place . reasonably suspect need for involuntary treatment order because illness features risk capacity issue and least restrictive reasoning . examination destination place . safety pearl separate referral authority from any detention transport or treatment authority. common traps treating form 1a as detention treatment transport or force authority. destination mismatch between referral and transport order. capacity reasoning replaced by poor insight or refused treatment . pre-use checks before use confirm the assessment basis examination place referral expiry and any linked detention or transport form. confirm the patient/person identifiers maker date time place and signature fields are complete. confirm copies notices support-person communication and filing/psolis requirements before handover. open the official pdf when the form wording or authority boundary matters. before use confirm the assessment basis examination place referral expiry and any linked detention or transport form. caution confirm the patient/person identifiers maker date time place and signature fields are complete. caution confirm copies notices support-person communication and filing/psolis requirements before handover. caution treating form 1a as detention treatment transport or force authority. reject destination mismatch between referral and transport order. reject official title and availability checked 17 july 2026 archive guidance remains a prototype reference aid and requires local clinical/legal governance review use the current approved pdf or psolis form do not modify approved form content 1a referral and detention use when a person needs referral for examination by a psychiatrist after assessment. medical practitioner or authorised mental health practitioner where the act permits. reasonable suspicion that involuntary treatment order criteria may be met with illness features risk capacity and least restrictive reasoning documented. usually 72 hours from referral. the referral is made within the required assessment window. referral for examination by a psychiatrist. treatment detention transport restraint seclusion or force by itself. referral psychiatrist exam 1a referral assessment referral capacity threshold destination expiry transport force refused referral practitioner treatment referred examination place time date within 48 hours 72 hours section 26 referral for examination at authorised hospital or other place a medical practitioner or authorised mental health practitioner may refer a person for examination by a psychiatrist if having regard to the section 25 criteria they reasonably suspect the person needs an involuntary treatment order or if already under a community treatment order an inpatient treatment order. examination may be at an authorised hospital or another appropriate place with necessary arrangements made for that place. section 31 revoking referral an mp or amhp may revoke a section 26 referral if satisfied the person no longer needs an involuntary treatment order. revoking another practitioner s referral requires consultation or documented reasonable efforts to contact them. the person cannot continue to be detained once the referral is revoked. section 36 referral for examination at authorised hospital for a voluntary inpatient at an authorised hospital an mp or amhp may refer them for psychiatrist examination at that hospital if having regard to the section 25 criteria they reasonably suspect the inpatient needs an involuntary treatment order. section 37 revoking referral an mp or amhp may revoke a section 36 referral if satisfied the voluntary inpatient no longer needs an involuntary treatment order. the same consultation rules apply when revoking another practitioner s referral. detention under that referral cannot continue once revoked. section 41 form of referral a referral must be in the approved form and include the particulars required by the act. use only the current approved form 1a or psolis pathway do not invent wording or rely on an outdated pdf. section 42 providing information contained in referral to person referred the practitioner must give the refer...","contentHash":"3f73b30db2104c0acb5dfb9192173cf295e60c36d1b9455fb1419e1d73c1a850","publicationFingerprint":"1b6ef671a5ac074fe71dc0ccc3513827581e840463b246b31cab4d0a9350400d","governanceFingerprint":"6473895da16d5508d03ffea998bc94f9de73dd719d70d551126b969e94bd3eab","lineageFingerprint":"4f53cda18c2baa0c0354bb5f9a3ecbe5ed12ab4d8e11ba873c2f11161202b945","publicMetadataFingerprint":"fbb2bb3556407b4aee2aeb82fbd1e8dca59db4f2915b676834a1c19d177db16d","record":{"version":"site-content-record-v1","logicalId":"forms:form-1a","producerClass":"dynamic_registry","domain":"forms","route":"/forms/form-1a","title":"Referral for examination by a psychiatrist","body":"Form: Referral for examination by a psychiatrist\nUse when a person needs referral for examination by a psychiatrist after assessment.\nRoute: Parallel: 1A attachment | After: 5A, 6A, 6B\nEligibility: Reasonable suspicion that involuntary treatment order criteria may be met, with illness features, risk, capacity and least restrictive reasoning documented.\nReferral: Before use: confirm the assessment basis, examination place, referral expiry and any linked detention or transport form.\nLocation: Western Australia\nBest use: Confirm the referral assessment, destination/place and expiry before any downstream detention, transport or treatment step; referral is not itself treatme