atrist must make one of three orders on the section 25 criteria a continuation order extending the inpatient treatment order for a further detention period a community treatment order if an involuntary treatment order is still needed but not as an inpatient or an order revoking the inpatient...","sourceRole":"form_reference","access":"public","validationStatus":"unverified","sourceStatus":"current","sourceLineage":[],"publicationVersion":"c7e10b20abfea54a6708129b145c003e910b6ce01102717b8a43fe23fa07eb82","contentHash":"fbb20234c9a6bf8d9998159f2ca2355d28da8caff57a25a4165ab02fb7c25587"},"renderPayload":{"slug":"form-6a","title":"Inpatient treatment order in authorised hospital","subtitle":"Use where inpatient compulsory treatment in an authorised hospital is required.","statusChips":[{"label":"medium risk","tone":"warning"},{"label":"Inpatient treatment orders","tone":"info"},{"label":"Official PDF","tone":"success"}],"primaryContact":{"label":"Form code","value":"Form 6A","detail":"Official PDF","kind":"text"},"contacts":[{"label":"Official public PDF","value":"https://www.chiefpsychiatrist.wa.gov.au/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/FORM6A_MHA2014__public.pdf","detail":"Current OCP source","kind":"web"},{"label":"Official forms register","value":"https://www.chiefpsychiatrist.wa.gov.au/laws-and-rights/legislation/mental-health-act-2014-forms/","detail":"Check current availability","kind":"web"}],"route":"Before: 1A | Parallel: 7A | After: Continuation, leave, revocation, CTO","eligibility":"Inpatient treatment order criteria met and less restrictive options are insufficient.","cost":"Official WA Mental Health Act 2014 form","referral":"Before use: confirm criteria, capacity/consent reasoning where relevant, authorised setting, review date and notifications.","location":"Western Australia","summaryCards":[{"id":"clock","label":"Clock / review","title":"Order and review timing must be visible in handover.","detail":"Up to 21 days, 14 days, 21 days, within 7 days"},{"id":"authority","label":"Made by / authority","title":"Psychiatrist via treatment order pathway.","detail":"Inpatient compulsory treatment according to the order."},{"id":"criteria","label":"Criteria / threshold","title":"Inpatient treatment order criteria met and less restrictive options are insufficient.","detail":"General hospital pathway unless 6B criteria and consent pathway apply."},{"id":"act-sections","label":"Act sections","title":"Authority under the Act","detail":"8 sections cited"}],"referralInfo":[{"label":"Purpose","value":"Use where inpatient compulsory treatment in an authorised hospital is required."},{"label":"Authorises","value":"Inpatient compulsory treatment according to the order."},{"label":"Does not authorise","value":"General hospital pathway unless 6B criteria and consent pathway apply."},{"label":"Before","value":"1A"},{"label":"Parallel","value":"7A"},{"label":"After","value":"Continuation, leave, revocation, CTO"},{"label":"Copies and filing","value":"Notify and file according to approved form process."},{"label":"Documentation stem","value":"Inpatient order criteria met because [facts]. Less restrictive options insufficient because [reason]. Review timing documented."},{"label":"Safety pearl","value":"Name why inpatient care is needed now, not just diagnosis or risk label."},{"label":"Common traps","value":"Weak least restrictive reasoning. Static status without active review."},{"label":"Pre-use checks","value":"Before use: confirm criteria, capacity/consent reasoning where relevant, authorised setting, review date and notifications. Confirm the patient/person identifiers, maker, date, time, place and signature fields are complete. Confirm copies, notices, support-person communication and filing/PSOLIS requirements before handover. Open the official PDF when the form wording or authority boundary matters."}],"bestUse":"Treatment order forms should show criteria, least restrictive reasoning, authorised setting, review timing and required notices. Use only the current approved form or PSOLIS pathway where available; only appropriately authorised people should complete MHA 2014 forms. Authority is limited to the Act sections, signature block, time limits and wording on the approved form.","criteria":[{"label":"Before use: confirm criteria, capacity/consent reasoning where relevant, authorised setting, review date and notifications.","tone":"caution"},{"label":"Confirm the patient/person identifiers, maker, date, time, place and signature fields are complete.","tone":"caution"},{"label":"Confirm copies, notices, support-person communication and filing/PSOLIS requirements before handover.","tone":"caution"},{"label":"Weak least restrictive reasoning.","tone":"reject"},{"label":"Static status without active review.","tone":"reject"}],"verification":{"locallyVerified":false,"confidence":"Medium","notes":["Official title and availability checked 17 July 2026","Archive guidance remains a prototype reference aid and requires local clinical/legal governance review","Use the current approved PDF or PSOLIS form; do not modify approved form content"]},"tags":["Form 6A","Inpatient treatment orders","Mental Health Act 2014","inpatient order","authorised hospital","6a","least restrictive","review","inpatient"],"catchments":["Western Australia"],"catalogueLabel":"Form 6A","navigatorQuery":"Form 6A Inpatient treatment order in authorised hospital Inpatient treatment orders Use where inpatient compulsory treatment in an authorised hospital is required. Up to 21 days, 14 days, 21 days, within 7 days treatment inpatient psychiatrist making involuntary time patient authorised Up to 21 days 14 days 21 days within 7 days","source":{"label":"Office of the Chief Psychiatrist WA — approved MHA 2014 forms","status":"Source checked","url":"https://www.chiefpsychiatrist.wa.gov.au/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/FORM6A_MHA2014__public.pdf","reviewed":"Official register checked 17 July 2026","notes":["Open the official source to confirm the current approved version before use","PSOLIS is the preferred completion method where available"]},"catalogPayload":{"id":"form-6a","form":"6A","name":"Inpatient treatment order in authorised hospital","category":"Inpatient treatment orders","purpose":"Use where inpatient compulsory treatment in an authorised hospital is required.","maker":"Psychiatrist via treatment order pathway.","involved":"Authorised hospital, treating team, personal support contacts where required.","threshold":"Inpatient treatment order criteria met and less restrictive options are insufficient.","clock":"Order and review timing must be visible in handover.","destination":"Authorised hospital.","authorises":"Inpatient compulsory treatment according to the order.","doesNotAuthorise":"General hospital pathway unless 6B criteria and consent pathway apply.","before":["1A"],"parallel":["7A"],"after":["Continuation","leave","revocation","CTO"],"copies":"Notify and file according to approved form process.","documentationStem":"Inpatient order criteria met because [facts]. Less restrictive options insufficient because [reason]. Review timing documented.","traps":["Weak least restrictive reasoning.","Static status without active review."],"safetyPearl":"Name why inpatient care is needed now, not just diagnosis or risk label.","sourceNote":"Representative summary from supplied forms guide.","aliases":["inpatient order","authorised hospital","6a"],"searchTerms":["least restrictive","review","inpatient","authorised hospital"],"riskLevel":"medium","indexedClock":"Up to 21 days, 14 days, 21 days, within 7 days","indexedTerms":["treatment","inpatient","psychiatrist","making","involuntary","time","patient","authorised","Up to 21 days","14 days","21 days","within 7 days"],"legalNote":"Treatment order forms should show criteria, least restrictive reasoning, authorised setting, review timing and required notices. Use only the current approved form or PSOLIS pathway where available; only appropriately authorised people should complete MHA 2014 forms. Authority is limited to the Act sections, signature block, time limits and wording on the approved form.","practicePearls":["Name why inpatient care is needed now, not just diagnosis or risk label.","Name why this setting is needed now and what review point changes the plan.","Clock cue: Up to 21 days, 14 days, 21 days. Record the start time, expiry or review point, and next owner.","Source cue: sections 55, 56, 72, 89, 90, 120, 123, 131."],"preUseChecks":["Before use: confirm criteria, capacity/consent reasoning where relevant, authorised setting, review date and notifications.","Confirm the patient/person identifiers, maker, date, time, place and signature fields are complete.","Confirm copies, notices, support-person communication and filing/PSOLIS requirements before handover.","Open the official PDF when the form wording or authority boundary matters."],"actSections":[{"section":"55","title":"What psychiatrist must do on completing examination","summary":"On completing the examination the psychiatrist must make one of four orders: an inpatient treatment order detaining the person at the authorised hospital for a s 87 period; a community treatment order; an order continuing detention to allow a further psychiatrist examination; or an order that the person cannot continue to be detained. The order is in the approved form with the time, the reasons (except for a release order) and the psychiatrist's details, and is filed with a copy to the person. A continuation order runs only to the period specified, which cannot exceed 72 hours from reception or detention at the hospital, and cannot be extended. Making the order is a Part 9 notifiable event.","reviewStatus":"drafted"},{"section":"56","title":"Effect of order for continuation of detention","summary":"An order continuing detention under s 55(1)(c) lasts until whichever comes first: a psychiatrist completes the further examination and makes an inpatient treatment order, a community treatment order, or an order that detention cannot continue; or the period specified under s 55(3) expires. The resulting order is in the approved form with the time, the reasons (except for a release order) and the psychiatrist's details, and is filed with a copy to the person.","reviewStatus":"drafted"},{"section":"72","title":"What psychiatrist must do on completing examination","summary":"On completing the examination the psychiatrist must make one of three orders: an inpatient treatment order detaining the person at the authorised hospital for a s 87 period; a community treatment order; or an order that the person cannot continue to be detained. The order is in the approved form with the time, the reasons (except for a release order) and the psychiatrist's details, and is filed with a copy to the person.","reviewStatus":"drafted"},{"section":"89","title":"Examination before end of each detention period","summary":"The treating psychiatrist must ensure the involuntary inpatient is examined by a psychiatrist on, or within 7 days before, the day the detention period ends. On completing that examination the psychiatrist must make one of three orders on the section 25 criteria: a continuation order extending the inpatient treatment order for a further detention period; a community treatment order, if an involuntary treatment order is still needed but not as an inpatient; or an order revoking the inpatient treatment order. A further detention period cannot exceed 3 months for an adult or 28 days for a child. The order is in the approved form and is filed with a copy to the inpatient; a release under (2)(b) or (c) is a Part 9 notifiable event.","reviewStatus":"drafted"},{"section":"90","title":"Changing involuntary inpatient’s status","summary":"During the detention period a psychiatrist may, on the section 25 criteria, make a community treatment order where the inpatient no longer needs the inpatient treatment order but still needs an involuntary treatment order, or revoke the inpatient treatment order where no involuntary treatment order is needed at all. Either order may be made without examining the inpatient. It is in the approved form and is filed with a copy to the inpatient, and making it is a Part 9 notifiable event.","reviewStatus":"drafted"},{"section":"120","title":"What supervising psychiatrist may do after examination","summary":"After examining the involuntary community patient, or on receiving a report under s 118(5), the supervising psychiatrist must consider whether the patient still needs an involuntary treatment order. They may make an inpatient treatment order where the patient still needs an involuntary treatment order but the s 114 community criteria are not met, or an order revoking the community treatment order where no involuntary treatment order is needed. An inpatient treatment order requires examining the patient; a revocation may be made either after examination or on the basis of a s 118(5) report alone. The order is in the approved form and is filed with a copy to the patient, and making it is a Part 9 notifiable event.","reviewStatus":"drafted"},{"section":"123","title":"Making inpatient treatment order or revoking community treatment order","summary":"The supervising psychiatrist may at any time while a community treatment order is in force make an inpatient treatment order, if satisfied on the s 25(1) criteria that the patient needs one, or revoke the community treatment order, if satisfied on the section 25 criteria that no involuntary treatment order is needed. An inpatient treatment order requires examining the patient; a revocation may be made without examination provided the psychiatrist has regard to information from the patient, from any other person, and from the medical record. None of this depends on the patient having breached the order, been given a breach notice, or been made subject to an order to attend. The order is in the approved form and is filed with a copy to the patient, and making it is a Part 9 notifiable event.","reviewStatus":"drafted"},{"section":"131","title":"Other action that may be taken if non-compliance","summary":"Where a patient is in breach of a community treatment order, has been given notice of the breach, and has since either continued not complying or failed to comply with an order to attend, the supervising psychiatrist may make an inpatient treatment order — if satisfied on the section 25 criteria that an involuntary treatment order is still needed but the s 114 community criteria are not met — or revoke the community treatment order if no involuntary treatment order is needed. An inpatient treatment order requires examining the patient, and detention at a general hospital additionally requires that an authorised hospital would pose a significant risk to physical health and that the Chief Psychiatrist consents. A revocation may be made without examination, having regard to clinical observation, other people and the medical record. The order is in the approved form and is filed with a copy to the patient, and making it is a Part 9 notifiable event.","reviewStatus":"drafted"}],"sourceFacts":{"documentTitle":"Inpatient Treatment Order In Authorised Hospital","fileName":"📋 Form 6A.pdf","pages":2,"timings":["Up to 21 days","14 days","21 days","within 7 days"],"sectionCue":"sections 55, 56, 72, 89, 90, 120, 123, 131","indexedAt":"Official form PDF indexed locally"},"availability":"downloadable","officialPdfUrl":"https://www.chiefpsychiatrist.wa.gov.au/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/FORM6A_MHA2014__public.pdf","officialRegisterUrl":"https://www.chiefpsychiatrist.wa.gov.au/laws-and-rights/legislation/mental-health-act-2014-forms/","localPdfPath":"/forms-pdf/form-6a.pdf","localPdfSha256":"eadcec7cf32e0928788d3d75ef4d6e36743fb76e4c234a416e4bbad7f0bfc4b4","localPdfBytes":293751,"officialPdfPasswordProtected":true,"officialTitleCheckedAt":"17 July 2026","archiveGeneratedAt":"2026-07-13T16:02:29.961824Z"}}},{"logicalId":"forms:form-6b","logicalDocumentId":"cf3561a0-de4a-56c7-8edf-c4f8d05d0fe0","logicalChunkId":"449a2dbe-84d3-5431-9946-829bf7a8e07c","normalizedText":"Form: Inpatient treatment order in general hospital\nUse for compulsory inpatient treatment in a general hospital where authorised-hospital transfer or detention creates significant physical-health risk.\nRoute: Before: 1A | Parallel: 6B attachment | After: 6A, continuation, revocation\nEligibility: Treatment order criteria met, consent obtained, and physical-health transfer/detention risk is significant.\nReferral: Before use: confirm criteria, capacity/consent reasoning where relevant, authorised setting, review date and notifications.\nLocation: Western Australia\nBest use: Treatment order forms should show criteria, least restrictive reasoning, authorised setting, review timing and required notices. Use only the current approved form or PSOLIS pathway where available; only appropriately authorised people should complete MHA 2014 forms. Authority is limited to the Act sections, signature block, time limits and wording on the approved form.\nPrimary contact: Form 6B Official PDF\nTags: Form 6B, Inpatient treatment orders, Mental Health Act 2014, general hospital, medical ward, 6b, physical health, consent, 7 days, attachment\nCatchments: Western Australia\ninpatient treatment order in general hospital form-6b use for compulsory inpatient treatment in a general hospital where authorised-hospital transfer or detention creates significant physical-health risk. before 1a parallel 6b attachment after 6a continuation revocation treatment order criteria met consent obtained and physical-health transfer/detention risk is significant. official wa mental health act 2014 form before use confirm criteria capacity/consent reasoning where relevant authorised setting review date and notifications. western australia treatment order forms should show criteria least restrictive reasoning authorised setting review timing and required notices. use only the current approved form or psolis pathway where available only appropriately authorised people should complete mha 2014 forms. authority is limited to the act sections signature block time limits and wording on the approved form. form 6b form 6b inpatient treatment order in general hospital inpatient treatment orders use for compulsory inpatient treatment in a general hospital where authorised-hospital transfer or detention creates significant physical-health risk. up to 21 days 14 days 21 days within 7 days 7 days inpatient treatment psychiatrist hospital made involuntary making general up to 21 days 14 days 21 days within 7 days 7 days form 6b official pdf office of the chief psychiatrist wa approved mha 2014 forms source checked official register checked 17 july 2026 form 6b inpatient treatment orders mental health act 2014 general hospital medical ward 6b physical health consent 7 days attachment western australia high risk inpatient treatment orders official pdf official public pdf https //www.chiefpsychiatrist.wa.gov.au/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/form6b mha2014 public.pdf current ocp source official forms register https //www.chiefpsychiatrist.wa.gov.au/laws-and-rights/legislation/mental-health-act-2014-forms/ check current availability clock / review report/attachment pathway reviewed every 7 days while continuing. up to 21 days 14 days 21 days within 7 days 7 days made by / authority psychiatrist with chief psychiatrist consent pathway. inpatient treatment in a general hospital according to the specific pathway. criteria / threshold treatment order criteria met consent obtained and physical-health transfer/detention risk is significant. bypass of consent/reporting requirements. act sections authority under the act 61 89 90 131 purpose use for compulsory inpatient treatment in a general hospital where authorised-hospital transfer or detention creates significant physical-health risk. authorises inpatient treatment in a general hospital accordin