ansfer/detention risk is significant. report/attachment pathway reviewed every 7 days while continuing. inpatient treatment in a general hospital according to the specific pathway. bypass of consent/reporting requirements. general hospital medical ward 6b physical health consent 7 days general hospital attachment inpatient treatment psychiatrist hospital made involuntary making general up to 21 days 14 days 21 days within 7 days 7 days section 61 what psychiatrist must do on completing examination on completing an examination at a place that is not an authorised hospital the psychiatrist must make one of four orders an inpatient treatment order detaining the person at a specified general hospital a community treatment order an order for reception and detention at an authorised hospital for a psychiatrist examination or an order that the person cannot continue to be detained. a general-hospital inpatient treatment order is available only where taking or detaining the person at an authorised hospital would pose a significant risk to their physical health and only with the chief psychiatrist s consent. the order is in the approved form and is filed with a copy to the person and making it is a part 9 notifiable event. section 89 examination before end of each detention period the treating psychiatrist must ensure the involuntary inpatient is examined by a psychiatrist on or within 7 days before the day the detention period ends. on completing that examination the psychiatrist must make one of three orders on the section 25 criteria a continuation order extending the inpatient treatment order for a further detention period a community treatment order if an involuntary treatment order is still needed but not as an inpatient or an order revoking the inpatient treatment order. a further detention period cannot exceed 3 months for an adult or 28 days for a child. the order is in the approved form and is filed with a copy to the inpatient a release under 2 b or c is a part 9 