titioner may make a transport order for them but only if satisfied no other safe means of ensuring they attend the place is reasonably available. part 10 governs how the order is carried out and making it is a part 9 notifiable event. section 133 making transport order a medical practitioner or mental health practitioner may make a transport order for the involuntary community patient but only if satisfied the patient needs to be taken to the hospital and no other safe means of taking them is reasonably available. part 10 governs how the order is carried out. section 148 making transport order a transport order must be in the approved form and must name the person to be transported the place they are transported from and the hospital or place they must be taken to the reasons why the order is necessary to get them there and whether it is to be carr...","sourceRole":"form_reference","access":"public","validationStatus":"unverified","sourceStatus":"current","sourceLineage":[],"publicationVersion":"476091a3fa2e8cc6ab83d0dd677bdffe4c0eaaf5f0e492f5e166bd9375b24b8c","contentHash":"2ffaa9fd7b4bdf023a4a8ec67bcb2679868e13d918e65d96c5a67e59b6ed09fb"},"renderPayload":{"slug":"transport-crisis-form","title":"Transport order","subtitle":"Use where a person must be conveyed and no other safe means is reasonably available.","statusChips":[{"label":"high risk","tone":"danger"},{"label":"Transport and transfer","tone":"info"},{"label":"Official PDF","tone":"success"}],"primaryContact":{"label":"Form code","value":"Form 4A","detail":"Official PDF","kind":"text"},"contacts":[{"label":"Official public PDF","value":"https://www.chiefpsychiatrist.wa.gov.au/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/FORM4A_MHA2014__public.pdf","detail":"Current OCP source","kind":"web"},{"label":"Official forms register","value":"https://www.chiefpsychiatrist.wa.gov.au/laws-and-rights/legislation/mental-health-act-2014-forms/","detail":"Check current availability","kind":"web"}],"route":"Before: 1A | Parallel: 3A, 4B | After: Examination at destination","eligibility":"Transport is necessary and no less restrictive safe means is reasonably available.","cost":"Official WA Mental Health Act 2014 form","referral":"Before use: confirm origin, destination, transport provider, linked form, expiry and less restrictive travel options.","location":"Western Australia","summaryCards":[{"id":"clock","label":"Clock / review","title":"Must remain aligned with live referral/detention pathway.","detail":"72 hours"},{"id":"authority","label":"Made by / authority","title":"Authorised maker specified by the transport pathway.","detail":"Transport/conveyance according to the order."},{"id":"criteria","label":"Criteria / threshold","title":"Transport is necessary and no less restrictive safe means is reasonably available.","detail":"Psychiatric treatment or detention beyond the linked authority."},{"id":"act-sections","label":"Act sections","title":"Authority under the Act","detail":"9 sections cited"}],"referralInfo":[{"label":"Purpose","value":"Use where a person must be conveyed and no other safe means is reasonably available."},{"label":"Authorises","value":"Transport/conveyance according to the order."},{"label":"Does not authorise","value":"Psychiatric treatment or detention beyond the linked authority."},{"label":"Before","value":"1A"},{"label":"Parallel","value":"3A, 4B"},{"label":"After","value":"Examination at destination"},{"label":"Copies and filing","value":"Notify transport and receiving service of destination and authority limits."},{"label":"Documentation stem","value":"Transport required because [reason] and no other safe means is reasonably available. Destination: [place]. Linked authority: [form]."},{"label":"Safety pearl","value":"Transport is a separate legal step, not an automatic consequence of referral."},{"label":"Common traps","value":"Destination mismatch. Assuming 1A alone permits transport. Not checking less restrictive travel option."},{"label":"Pre-use checks","value":"Before use: confirm origin, destination, transport provider, linked form, expiry and less restrictive travel options. Confirm the patient/person identifiers, maker, date, time, place and signature fields are complete. Confirm copies, notices, support-person communication and filing/PSOLIS requirements before handover. Open the official PDF when the form wording or authority boundary matters."}],"bestUse":"Transport or transfer authority should be specific to the journey, destination and linked live authority, with no safer less restrictive option available. Use only the current approved form or PSOLIS pathway where available; only appropriately authorised people should complete MHA 2014 forms. Authority is limited to the Act sections, signature block, time limits and wording on the approved form.","criteria":[{"label":"Before use: confirm origin, destination, transport provider, linked form, expiry and less restrictive travel options.","tone":"caution"},{"label":"Confirm the patient/person identifiers, maker, date, time, place and signature fields are complete.","tone":"caution"},{"label":"Confirm copies, notices, support-person communication and filing/PSOLIS requirements before handover.","tone":"caution"},{"label":"Destination mismatch.","tone":"reject"},{"label":"Assuming 1A alone permits transport.","tone":"reject"}],"verification":{"locallyVerified":false,"confidence":"Medium","notes":["Official title and availability checked 17 July 2026","Archive guidance remains a prototype reference aid and requires local clinical/legal governance review","Use the current approved PDF or PSOLIS form; do not modify approved form content"]},"tags":["Form 4A","Transport and transfer","Mental Health Act 2014","transport","convey","ambulance","police transport","destination","force","least restrictive","referral"],"catchments":["Western Australia"],"catalogueLabel":"Form 4A","navigatorQuery":"Form 4A Transport order Transport and transfer Use where a person must be conveyed and no other safe means is reasonably available. 72 hours transport hospital taken revoked officer psychiatrist involuntary made 72 hours","source":{"label":"Office of the Chief Psychiatrist WA — approved MHA 2014 forms","status":"Source checked","url":"https://www.chiefpsychiatrist.wa.gov.au/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/FORM4A_MHA2014__public.pdf","reviewed":"Official register checked 17 July 2026","notes":["Open the official source to confirm the current approved version before use","PSOLIS is the preferred completion method where available"]},"catalogPayload":{"id":"form-4a","form":"4A","name":"Transport order","category":"Transport and transfer","purpose":"Use where a person must be conveyed and no other safe means is reasonably available.","maker":"Authorised maker specified by the transport pathway.","involved":"Transport provider, receiving service, referrer.","threshold":"Transport is necessary and no less restrictive safe means is reasonably available.","clock":"Must remain aligned with live referral/detention pathway.","destination":"Exact destination is essential and must match the referral pathway.","authorises":"Transport/conveyance according to the order.","doesNotAuthorise":"Psychiatric treatment or detention beyond the linked authority.","before":["1A"],"parallel":["3A","4B"],"after":["Examination at destination"],"copies":"Notify transport and receiving service of destination and authority limits.","documentationStem":"Transport required because [reason] and no other safe means is reasonably available. Destination: [place]. Linked authority: [form].","traps":["Destination mismatch.","Assuming 1A alone permits transport.","Not checking less restrictive travel option."],"safetyPearl":"Transport is a separate legal step, not an automatic consequence of referral.","sourceNote":"Representative summary from supplied workflow guide.","aliases":["transport","convey","ambulance","police transport"],"searchTerms":["destination","force","least restrictive","referral"],"riskLevel":"high","indexedClock":"72 hours","indexedTerms":["transport","hospital","taken","revoked","officer","psychiatrist","involuntary","made","72 hours"],"legalNote":"Transport or transfer authority should be specific to the journey, destination and linked live authority, with no safer less restrictive option available. Use only the current approved form or PSOLIS pathway where available; only appropriately authorised people should complete MHA 2014 forms. Authority is limited to the Act sections, signature block, time limits and wording on the approved form.","practicePearls":["Transport is a separate legal step, not an automatic consequence of referral.","A transport form should make the route, destination and linked authority easy to audit.","Clock cue: 72 hours. Record the start time, expiry or review point, and next owner.","Source cue: sections 29,63,67,92,112,129,133,148, 154."],"preUseChecks":["Before use: confirm origin, destination, transport provider, linked form, expiry and less restrictive travel options.","Confirm the patient/person identifiers, maker, date, time, place and signature fields are complete.","Confirm copies, notices, support-person communication and filing/PSOLIS requirements before handover.","Open the official PDF when the form wording or authority boundary matters."],"actSections":[{"section":"29","title":"Making transport order","summary":"An MP or AMHP may make a transport order for a referred person, but only if satisfied the person needs to be taken to the examination place and no other safe means of taking them is reasonably available. Part 10 governs how the order is carried out, and making it is a Part 9 notifiable event.","reviewStatus":"drafted"},{"section":"63","title":"Making transport order","summary":"A psychiatrist may make a transport order for a person under a s 61(1)(a) or (c) order, but only if satisfied the person needs to be taken to the hospital named in that order and no other safe means of taking them is reasonably available. Part 10 governs how the order is carried out.","reviewStatus":"drafted"},{"section":"67","title":"Making transport order","summary":"A psychiatrist may make a transport order for an inpatient under a s 66(1) transfer order, but only if satisfied no other safe means of taking them to the authorised hospital is reasonably available. Part 10 governs how the order is carried out.","reviewStatus":"drafted"},{"section":"92","title":"Making transport order","summary":"A psychiatrist may make a transport order for an inpatient under a s 91(2) transfer order, but only if satisfied no other safe means of taking them to the authorised hospital is reasonably available. Part 10 governs how the order is carried out.","reviewStatus":"drafted"},{"section":"112","title":"Making transport order","summary":"A psychiatrist may make a transport order for the involuntary inpatient, but only if satisfied no other safe means of taking them to the hospital is reasonably available. Part 10 governs how the order is carried out.","reviewStatus":"drafted"},{"section":"129","title":"Making transport order","summary":"Where an involuntary community patient does not comply with an order to attend, a medical practitioner or mental health practitioner may make a transport order for them, but only if satisfied no other safe means of ensuring they attend the place is reasonably available. Part 10 governs how the order is carried out, and making it is a Part 9 notifiable event.","reviewStatus":"drafted"},{"section":"133","title":"Making transport order","summary":"A medical practitioner or mental health practitioner may make a transport order for the involuntary community patient, but only if satisfied the patient needs to be taken to the hospital and no other safe means of taking them is reasonably available. Part 10 governs how the order is carried out.","reviewStatus":"drafted"},{"section":"148","title":"Making transport order","summary":"A transport order must be in the approved form and must name the person to be transported, the place they are transported from and the hospital or place they must be taken to, the reasons why the order is necessary to get them there, and whether it is to be carried out by a transport officer or — where s 149(2) applies — a police officer, with reasons why a transport officer cannot do so. It must also state the time it is made, the time it expires under s 150(2), whether and how it can be extended, and the maker's name, qualifications and signature. It must be filed as soon as practicable with a copy to the person and a copy to the officer carrying it out.","reviewStatus":"drafted"},{"section":"154","title":"Revocation of transport order if no longer needed","summary":"A medical practitioner or mental health practitioner may revoke a transport order if satisfied it is no longer needed. The revocation order is in the approved form with the time made, the reasons and the practitioner's details. It must be filed as soon as practicable with a copy to the person and a copy to the transport or police officer responsible for carrying out the transport order.","reviewStatus":"drafted"}],"sourceFacts":{"documentTitle":"Transport Order","fileName":"📋 Form 4A.pdf","pages":2,"timings":["72 hours"],"sectionCue":"sections 29,63,67,92,112,129,133,148, 154","indexedAt":"Official form PDF indexed locally"},"availability":"downloadable","officialPdfUrl":"https://www.chiefpsychiatrist.wa.gov.au/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/FORM4A_MHA2014__public.pdf","officialRegisterUrl":"https://www.chiefpsychiatrist.wa.gov.au/laws-and-rights/legislation/mental-health-act-2014-forms/","localPdfPath":"/forms-pdf/form-4a.pdf","localPdfSha256":"4defb088aa98a0175d9ff4612b125c45c263fb31a3d818f7d3f66b2043208511","localPdfBytes":223163,"officialPdfPasswordProtected":true,"officialTitleCheckedAt":"17 July 2026","archiveGeneratedAt":"2026-07-13T16:02:29.961824Z"}}},{"logicalId":"medications:acamprosate","logicalDocumentId":"3a4e8404-d41f-5e52-a831-278cfa7b4b48","logicalChunkId":"5374a4e3-86ee-585d-aecb-46b3a63551d4","normalizedText":"Medication: Acamprosate\nClass: Addiction\nSubclass: GABA / Glutamate Modulator\nSchedule: S4\nTag: AUD\nRapid Summary summary Overview Anti-craving agent indicated to maintain abstinence in alcohol-dependent patients. Use with counselling; initiate after the withdrawal period and continue if relapse occurs. Maximum Dose Max **1998 mg/day** (Requires taking 2 large tablets, 3 times a day). Clinical Focus Renal function is the key safety limiter: Australian PI contraindicates use in renal insufficiency (serum creatinine >120 micromol/L). Severe hepatic failure (Child-Pugh C), pregnancy, and breastfeeding are contraindications; avoid use in children/adolescents <18 and adults >65 because safety/efficacy are not established. Bottom Line Acamprosate is an anti-craving agent for maintaining alcohol abstinence after detoxification, but requires good renal function and is only effective when combined with psychosocial support. Risk Profile risk Gastrointestinal HIGH — Diarrhea, nausea, flatulence (very common, dose-dependent). Dermatological MODERATE — Pruritus, maculopapular rash. Neurological LOW — Mood swings, depression (ensure underlying psychiatric conditions are monitored). Dosing & Administration dose Alcohol Abstinence Maintenance (>60 kg) **PO** 2 tablets (666 mg) **TDS** with meals (Total 1998 mg/day). Alcohol Abstinence (<60 kg) **PO** 2 tabs morning, 1 tab midday, 1 tab night. Renal Impairment CONTRAINDICATED — Australian PI contraindicates acamprosate in renal insufficiency (serum creatinine >120 micromol/L). Do not rely on the current eGFR thresholds unless they are separately source-reviewed. Formulation & Access form Brand Names Campral Oral Routes Enteric-coated tablets (333 mg). Do not crush. Prescribing & PBS Authority Required (STREAMLINED PBS), item 8357W; acamprosate calcium 333 mg enteric tablet, 180. Indications & Use ind Primary Maintenance of abstinence in alcohol-dependent patients. Clinical Place First-line relapse-prevention option for patients seeking abstinence, alongside naltrexone. Particularly useful when opioid therapy makes naltrexone unsuitable, but avoid renal insufficiency and severe hepatic failure (Child-Pugh C). Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — Contraindicated with hypersensitivity to acamprosate, renal insufficiency (serum creatinine >120 micromol/L), severe hepatic failure (Child-Pugh C), pregnancy, or breastfeeding. Hepatic CONTRAINDICATED — Severe hepatic failure (Child-Pugh C). Although hepatic dysfunction does not alter pharmacokinetics, the PI states safety and efficacy are not established in severe hepatic failure. Pregnancy CONTRAINDICATED — Pregnancy category B2; do not administer during pregnancy. Breastfeeding is also contraindicated. Elderly AVOID — Safety and efficacy are not established in patients older than 65 years; Australian PI states acamprosate should not be administered to elderly patients. Paediatric AVOID — Safety and efficacy are not established in patients younger than 18 years; Australian PI states acamprosate should not be administered to children. Key Interactions inter Pharmacokinetic LOW — No pharmacokinetic interaction shown with diazepam, disulfiram, or imipramine. Naltrexone increases acamprosate exposure, but the PI states no dose adjustment is necessary. Monitor carefully with psychotropics not studied. Monitoring & Cautions mon Laboratory MANDATORY — Check renal function before prescribing; renal insufficiency with serum creatinine >120 micromol/L is contraindicated. If liver disease is suspected, assess hepatic status/LFTs because severe hepatic failure (Child-Pugh C) is contraindicated. Bedside MONITOR — Monitor depression and suicidality symptoms in alcohol-dependent patients. Also assess adherence, because three-times-daily dosing and tablet burden can limit effectiveness. Clinical Pearls pearl No Disulfiram Effect Ensure the patient knows that if they relapse and drink alcohol, Acamprosate will NOT make them violently ill. They can safely continue taking it while re-engaging with detox protocols. The Post-Detox Start Start as soon as possible after the withdrawal period. Continue treatment if relapse occurs; the recommended treatment period is 1 year. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Mechanism is not fully established. The PI describes acamprosate as structurally similar to amino acid neuromediators and notes animal evidence of reduced glutamatergic hyperactivity in alcohol withdrawal, rather than a confirmed direct GABA/NMDA effect. Onset Days to weeks to achieve steady state and clinical efficacy. Half-life Apparent elimination half-life after oral 666 mg dosing ranges from 13 to 28.4 hours. Hepatic dysfunction does not alter pharmacokinetics; acamprosate is not metabolised and is eliminated in urine. Sources src Source Review Source Check — TGA ARTG 286653, CAMPRAL Australian PI, PBS item 8357W, Australian Prescriber alcohol-dependence review, and DACAS acamprosate factsheet checked 2026-06-30 for this entry.\nClass & Role GABA / Glutamate Modulator — Anti-craving agent for Alcohol Use Disorder (AUD). Route / Formulation Enteric-coated tablets (333 mg). Do not crush. (Campral) Usual Dose & Max **PO** 2 tablets (666 mg) **TDS** with meals (Total 1998 mg/day). Max **1998 mg/day** (Requires taking 2 large tablets, 3 times a day). Key Indication Doses Alcohol Abstinence Maintenance (>60 kg): **PO** 2 tablets (666 mg) **TDS** with meals (Total 1998 mg/day). Alcohol Abstinence (<60 kg): **PO** 2 tabs morning, 1 tab midday, 1 tab night. Renal Impairment: contraindicated if serum creatinine >120 micromol/L; do not rely on eGFR dose thresholds unless separately source-reviewed. Best Uses Acamprosate is an anti-craving agent for maintaining alcohol abstinence after detoxification, but requires good renal function and is only effective when combined with psychosocial support. Avoid / Cautions Contraindicated in renal insufficiency (serum creatinine >120 micromol/L), severe hepatic failure (Child-Pugh C), pregnancy, breastfeeding, and hypersensitivity. Do not use in children/adolescents <18 or adults >65 because safety/efficacy are not established. Pregnancy category B2. Key Risks Gastrointestinal: Diarrhea, nausea, flatulence (very common, dose-dependent). Dermatological: Pruritus, maculopapular rash. Neurological: Mood swings, depression (ensure underlying psychiatric conditions are monitored). Key Interactions No pharmacokinetic interaction shown with diazepam, disulfiram, or imipramine. Naltrexone increases acamprosate exposure, but no dose adjustment is necessary. Monitor carefully with psychotropics not studied. Monitoring Check renal function before prescribing. Assess hepatic status/LFTs where liver disease is suspected. Monitor depression/suicidality symptoms and adherence during treatment. Clinical Pearl Ensure the patient knows that if they relapse and drink alcohol, Acamprosate will NOT make them violently ill. They can safely continue taking it while re-engaging with detox protocols.","contentHash":"1397b6a6f5e48a855a44ca3f6dd483796c4448ea29b08d9be60700e0caf1b27e","publicationFingerprint":"55d4535ec052588b70cf2a9d7d466c311db74f60c6ffb0f9c6defbea15d03087","governanceFingerprint":"6473895da16d5508d03ffea998bc94f9de73dd719d70d551126b969e94bd3eab","lineageFingerprint":"4f53cda18c2baa0c0354bb5f9a3ecbe5ed12ab4d8e11ba873c2f11161202b945","publicMetadataFingerprint":"8b55e1707b0c2ee64d9823560f3353f8a619ab435dce25a174c2706600c6547c","record":{"version":"site-content-record-v1","logicalId":"medications:acamprosate","producerClass":"dynamic_registry","domain":"medications","route":"/medications/acamprosate","title":"Acamprosate","body":"Medication: Acamprosate\nClass: Addiction\nSubclass: GABA / Glutamate Modulator\nSchedule: S4\nTag: AUD\nRapid Summary summary Overview Anti-craving agent indicated to maintain abstinence in alcohol-dependent patients. Use w