e antihypertensive, especially in elderly and Afro-Caribbean patients who respond poorly to ACE inhibitors. Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — Severe hypotension, cardiogenic shock, severe aortic stenosis (vasodilation will drop coronary perfusion fatally). Pregnancy CAUTION — **Pregnancy** Category C. Key Interactions inter Pharmacokinetic HIGH — **CYP3A4** inhibitors (Clarithromycin, Ketoconazole) spike Amlodipine levels, worsening edema and hypotension. Pharmacokinetic SAFE — Unlike Felodipine, Amlodipine does NOT interact significantly with Grapefruit juice. Monitoring & Cautions mon Bedside MANDATORY — Check **BP**. Inspect the patient's ankles for severe pitting edema before escalating from 5mg to 10mg. Clinical Pearls pearl The Diuretic Myth If a patient on Amlodipine develops swollen ankles, prescribing Frusemide (a diuretic) will NOT fix it. The edema is caused by capillary hydrostatic pressure leaking fluid into the tissues, not by whole-body fluid overload. You must either halve the Amlodipine dose or add an ACEi (which dilates the venules to drain the fluid). The Gingival Swell If a patient presents with massive, overgrown gums bleeding over their teeth, check their med list. Amlodipine, Phenytoin, and Cyclosporin are the classic triad for gingival hyperplasia. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Blocks L-type voltage-gated calcium channels in vascular smooth muscle. Prevents calcium influx, halting muscle contraction, leading to massive, sustained vasodilation of peripheral and coronary arteries. Onset **PO** 6-12 hours (Slow onset, zero reflex tachycardia). Half-life 30-50 hours (Allows perfect once-daily dosing and forgives missed doses). Metabolised by CYP3A4. Sources src Source Review Source-backed review 2026-05-11: NORDIP/amlodipine Australian PI. Entry checked for dosing, access, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and app structure.\nClass & Role DHP Calcium Channel Blocker — Dihydropyridine (DHP) Calcium Channel Blocker. Route / Formulation Tablets (5, 10 mg). Often combined with Perindopril (Reaptan) or Valsartan (Exforge). (Norvasc) Usual Dose & Max Start **PO** 5 mg **OD**. Titrate to Max 10 mg **OD**. Key Indication Doses Hypertension / Angina: Start **PO** 5 mg **OD**. Titrate to Max 10 mg **OD**. Elderly / Hepatic Impairment: Start at 2.5 mg **OD**. Clearance is severely delayed in liver disease. Best Uses Amlodipine is a first-line antihypertensive with no monitoring burden and safe in most populations, but peripheral oedema is common and dose-limiting. Avoid / Cautions Severe hypotension, cardiogenic shock, severe aortic stenosis (vasodilation will drop coronary perfusion fatally). **Pregnancy** Category C. Key Risks Cardiovascular: Severe peripheral edema (swollen, pitting ankles/legs). Extremely common at 10mg. HIGH — Flushing, palpitations, hypotension. Gastrointestinal: Gingival hyperplasia (swollen gums). Key Interactions **CYP3A4** inhibitors (Clarithromycin, Ketoconazole) spike Amlodipine levels, worsening edema and hypotension. Unlike Felodipine, Amlodipine does NOT interact significantly with Grapefruit juice. Monitoring Check **BP**. Inspect the patient's ankles for severe pitting edema before escalating from 5mg to 10mg. Clinical Pearl If a patient on Amlodipine develops swollen ankles, prescribing Frusemide (a diuretic) will NOT fix it. The edema is caused by capillary hydrostatic pressure leaking fluid into the tissues, not by whole-body fluid overload. You must either halve the Amlodipine dose or add an ACEi (which dilates the venules to drain the fluid).","sourceRole":"clinical_reference","access":"public","validationStatus":"unverified","sourceStatus":"current","sourceLineage":[],"publicationVersion":"f59f01785b71b87637d7632abb78263bfe71955f1ef4e67f44f0d04965c81b43","contentHash":"46384b8bab647285659b4057973c31e815a8dac2cd544f72174f0543903d3b67"},"renderPayload":{"slug":"amlodipine","name":"Amlodipine","