tifies risk and monitor neonate if used near delivery. Key Interactions inter Pharmacodynamic CRITICAL — Extreme additive CNS depression and weakness if combined with Opioids, Benzodiazepines, or Alcohol. Pharmacodynamic HIGH — Antihypertensives (Additive hypotension). Monitoring & Cautions mon Laboratory MANDATORY — Baseline and routine **U&E** and **eGFR**. Bedside MANDATORY — Assess muscle tone. Over-treating spasticity will remove the 'stiffness' the patient relies on to stand up and walk, rendering them bedbound. Clinical Pearls pearl The Intrathecal Pump Trap If an implanted intrathecal baclofen pump runs dry or the catheter snaps, the patient will be plunged into massive, fatal withdrawal within hours. This is a medical emergency requiring massive doses of oral baclofen or ICU sedation with diazepam/propofol until the pump is fixed. The CKD Coma A classic ward presentation: an MS patient with a UTI develops AKI, their Baclofen accumulates, and they are found comatose. Check renal function before giving Naloxone/Flumazenil. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Specific agonist at the GABA-B receptor in the spinal cord. Hyperpolarizes presynaptic nerve terminals, severely restricting the release of excitatory neurotransmitters (glutamate, aspartate), suppressing reflex arcs. Onset **PO** Hours to days for full antispastic effect. Half-life 3-4 hours (Rapidly and predominantly excreted unchanged by the kidneys). Sources src Source Review Source-backed review 2026-05-11: LIORESAL/baclofen Australian PI/CMI source pack. Entry checked for dosing, access, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and app structure.\nClass & Role GABA-B Agonist — Central muscle relaxant. Route / Formulation Tablets (10 mg, 25 mg). Oral liquid (5 mg/mL). (Lioresal, Clofen) Usual Dose & Max Start **PO** 5 mg **TDS**. Titrate up by 15 mg/day every 3 days. Maintenance usually 30-75 mg/day. Max 100 mg/day. Key Indication Doses Chronic Spasticity: Start **PO** 5 mg **TDS**. Titrate up by 15 mg/day every 3 days. Maintenance usually 30-75 mg/day. Max 100 mg/day. Intrathecal (Severe): Continuous infusion directly into the spinal fluid via an implanted pump (Specialist only). Renal Impairment: Reduce dose to 5 mg/day if **eGFR** < 30. High risk of severe encephalopathy and coma. Best Uses Baclofen is the first-line skeletal muscle relaxant for spasticity (MS, spinal cord injury), but causes significant sedation and abrupt withdrawal can cause life-threatening seizures and autonomic instability. Avoid / Cautions Active peptic ulceration, severe psychosis/schizophrenia (can exacerbate). **Pregnancy** Category B3. Key Risks Neurological: Seizures, acute psychosis, and hallucinations upon abrupt withdrawal. HIGH — Profound sedation, dizziness, severe hypotonia (muscle weakness making the patient unable to stand). Cardiovascular: Hypotension. Gastrointestinal: Nausea (usually transient). Key Interactions Extreme additive CNS depression and weakness if combined with Opioids, Benzodiazepines, or Alcohol. Antihypertensives (Additive hypotension). Monitoring Baseline and routine **U&E** and **eGFR**. Assess muscle tone. Over-treating spasticity will remove the 'stiffness' the patient relies on to stand up and walk, rendering them bedbound. Clinical Pearl If an implanted intrathecal baclofen pump runs dry or the catheter snaps, the patient will be plunged into massive, fatal withdrawal within hours. This is a medical emergency requiring massive doses of oral baclofen or ICU sedation with diazepam/propofol until the pump is fixed.","sourceRole":"clinical_reference","access":"public","validationStatus":"unverified","sourceStatus":"current","sourceLineage":[],"publicationVersion":"e50a8abdacc2ef92f4049a3f7b84173df7285920dba7a5461ec845ddac7fe40d","contentHash":"af27442b70cfec5a9ecb48514771a04a34f7e6ba768f3efc935e2b3b1579a632"},"renderPayload":{"slug":"baclofen","name":"Baclofen","class":"Muscle Relaxant","subclass":"GABA-B Agonist","category":"Musculoskeletal & Rheumatology","accent":"#d97706","tag":"SPASMOLYTIC","schedule":"S4","stats":[{"label":"Max Dose","value":"100 mg/day","cls":"hi","flag":"hi"},{"label":"Half-life","value":"3-4 h","cls":"","flag":""},{"label":"Withdrawal","value":"SEIZURE RISK","cls":"hi","flag":"warn"},{"label":"Renal Adj.","value":"MANDATORY","cls":"warn","flag":""}],"sections":[{"title":"Rapid Summary","type":"summary","rows":[{"key":"Overview","val":"Central muscle relaxant. Highly effective for spasticity originating from spinal cord or brain injury. Acts as a potent depressant on spinal reflexes.","tags":[]},{"key":"Maximum Dose","val":"Max **100 mg/day** (Requires very slow titration to prevent profound sedation/weakness).","tags":[]},{"key":"Clinical Focus","val":"Abrupt cessation causes severe, life-threatening withdrawal (seizures, hallucinations, hyperthermia). Must be tapered over weeks.","tags":[]},{"key":"Bottom Line","val":"Baclofen is the first-line skeletal muscle relaxant for spasticity (MS, spinal cord injury), but causes significant sedation and abrupt withdrawal can cause life-threatening seizures and autonomic instability.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Risk Profile","type":"risk","rows":[{"key":"Neurological","val":"CRITICAL — Seizures, acute psychosis, and hallucinations upon abrupt withdrawal. HIGH — Profound sedation, dizziness, severe hypotonia (muscle weakness making the patient unable to stand).","tags":[]},{"key":"Cardiovascular","val":"MODERATE — Hypotension.","tags":[]},{"key":"Gastrointestinal","val":"LOW — Nausea (usually transient).","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Dosing & Administration","type":"dose","rows":[{"key":"Chronic Spasticity","val":"Start **PO** 5 mg **TDS**. Titrate up by 15 mg/day every 3 days. Maintenance usually 30-75 mg/day. Max 100 mg/day.","tags":[]},{"key":"Intrathecal (Severe)","val":"Continuous infusion directly into the spinal fluid via an implanted pump (Specialist only).","tags":[]},{"key":"Renal Impairment","val":"MANDATORY — Reduce dose to 5 mg/day if **eGFR** < 30. High risk of severe encephalopathy and coma.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Formulation & Access","type":"form","rows":[{"key":"Brand Names","val":"Lioresal, Clofen","tags":[]},{"key":"Oral Routes","val":"Tablets (10 mg, 25 mg). Oral liquid (5 mg/mL).","tags":[]},{"key":"Parenteral","val":"Ampoules for Intrathecal injection/infusion ONLY. Never IV.","tags":[]},{"key":"Prescribing & PBS","val":"Authority Required (PBS) for specific neurological indications.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Indications & Use","type":"ind","rows":[{"key":"Primary","val":"Severe muscle spasticity associated with MS, spinal cord injury, cerebral palsy, stroke.","tags":["PBS","TGA"]},{"key":"Off-Label","val":"Hiccups, severe alcohol cravings (high dose).","tags":["OFF"]},{"key":"Clinical Place","val":"The gold-standard oral anti-spasmodic for upper motor neuron lesions.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Contraindications & Populations","type":"contra","rows":[{"key":"Absolute","val":"CRITICAL — Active peptic ulceration, severe psychosis/schizophrenia (can exacerbate).","tags":[]},{"key":"Renal Impairment","val":"CRITICAL — Predominantly renally excreted; reduce dose and monitor closely in CKD/AKI. Severe renal impairment can cause profound encephalopathy/coma.","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["renal"],"action":"dose-adjust","severity":"danger","match":{},"note":"Baclofen accumulates in renal impairment and can cause severe encephalopathy or coma."}},{"key":"Pregnancy","val":"CAUTION — **Pregnancy** Category B3; use only if benefit justifies risk and monitor neonate if used near delivery.","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["pregnancy"],"action":"caution","severity":"caution","note":"Baclofen in pregnancy requires benefit-risk review; neonatal effects are possible if used near delivery."}}]},{"title":"Key Interactions","type":"inter","rows":[{"key":"Pharmacodynamic","val":"CRITICAL — Extreme additive CNS depression and weakness if combined with Opioids, Benzodiazepines, or Alcohol.","tags":[]},{"key":"Pharmacodynamic","val":"HIGH — Antihypertensives (Additive hypotension).","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Monitoring & Cautions","type":"mon","rows":[{"key":"Laboratory","val":"MANDATORY — Baseline and routine **U&E** and **eGFR**.","tags":[]},{"key":"Bedside","val":"MANDATORY — Assess muscle tone. Over-treating spasticity will remove the 'stiffness' the patient relies on to stand up and walk, rendering them bedbound.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Clinical Pearls","type":"pearl","rows":[{"key":"The Intrathecal Pump Trap","val":"If an implanted intrathecal baclofen pump runs dry or the catheter snaps, the patient will be plunged into massive, fatal withdrawal within hours. This is a medical emergency requiring massive doses of oral baclofen or ICU sedation with diazepam/propofol until the pump is fixed.","tags":[]},{"key":"The CKD Coma","val":"A classic ward presentation: an MS patient with a UTI develops AKI, their Baclofen accumulates, and they are found comatose. Check renal function before giving Naloxone/Flumazenil.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics","type":"evid","rows":[{"key":"Mechanism","val":"Specific agonist at the GABA-B receptor in the spinal cord. Hyperpolarizes presynaptic nerve terminals, severely restricting the release of excitatory neurotransmitters (glutamate, aspartate), suppressing reflex arcs.","tags":[]},{"key":"Onset","val":"**PO** Hours to days for full antispastic effect.","tags":[]},{"key":"Half-life","val":"3-4 hours (Rapidly and predominantly excreted unchanged by the kidneys).","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Sources","type":"src","rows":[{"key":"Source Review","val":"Source-backed review 2026-05-11: LIORESAL/baclofen Australian PI/CMI source pack. Entry checked for dosing, access, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and app structure."}]}],"quick":[{"label":"Class & Role","value":"GABA-B Agonist — Central muscle relaxant."},{"label":"Route / Formulation","value":"Tablets (10 mg, 25 mg). Oral liquid (5 mg/mL). (Lioresal, Clofen)"},{"label":"Usual Dose & Max","value":"Start **PO** 5 mg **TDS**. Titrate up by 15 mg/day every 3 days. Maintenance usually 30-75 mg/day. Max 100 mg/day."},{"label":"Key Indication Doses","value":"Chronic Spasticity: Start **PO** 5 mg **TDS**. Titrate up by 15 mg/day every 3 days. Maintenance usually 30-75 mg/day. Max 100 mg/day.  Intrathecal (Severe): Continuous infusion directly into the spinal fluid via an implanted pump (Specialist only).  Renal Impairment: Reduce dose to 5 mg/day if **eGFR** < 30. High risk of severe encephalopathy and coma."},{"label":"Best Uses","value":"Baclofen is the first-line skeletal muscle relaxant for spasticity (MS, spinal cord injury), but causes significant sedation and abrupt withdrawal can cause life-threatening seizures and autonomic instability."},{"label":"Avoid / Cautions","value":"Active peptic ulceration, severe psychosis/schizophrenia (can exacerbate). **Pregnancy** Category B3."},{"label":"Key Risks","value":"Neurological: Seizures, acute psychosis, and hallucinations upon abrupt withdrawal. HIGH — Profound sedation, dizziness, severe hypotonia (muscle weakness making the patient unable to stand).  Cardiovascular: Hypotension.  Gastrointestinal: Nausea (usually transient)."},{"label":"Key Interactions","value":"Extreme additive CNS depression and weakness if combined with Opioids, Benzodiazepines, or Alcohol.  Antihypertensives (Additive hypotension)."},{"label":"Monitoring","value":"Baseline and routine **U&E** and **eGFR**.  Assess muscle tone. Over-treating spasticity will remove the 'stiffness' the patient relies on to stand up and walk, rendering them bedbound."},{"label":"Clinical Pearl","value":"If an implanted intrathecal baclofen pump runs dry or the catheter snaps, the patient will be plunged into massive, fatal withdrawal within hours. This is a medical emergency requiring massive doses of oral baclofen or ICU sedation with diazepam/propofol until the pump is fixed."}]}},{"logicalId":"medications:beclometasone","logicalDocumentId":"8bc0dd4c-1cde-5a2c-92cf-73831d640992","logicalChunkId":"38dce0f6-6a52-5c7d-bcee-3015fe15edb4","normalizedText":"Medication: Beclometasone\nClass: Steroid\nSubclass: Topical/Inhaled Glucocorticoid\nSchedule: S4\nTag: STEROID\nRapid Summary summary Overview Inhaled Corticosteroid (ICS). Formulated as extra-fine particles that penetrate deep into the small airways, providing excellent control of chronic asthma inflammation. Maximum Dose Max **800 mcg/day**. Clinical Focus Must use a spacer and rinse/spit after every dose to prevent oral thrush and vocal cord myopathy. Bottom Line Beclometasone is a standard inhaled corticosteroid for asthma preventer therapy, but requires consistent use for effect and mouth rinsing to prevent oral candidiasis. Risk Profile risk Oral/Pharyngeal HIGH — Oropharyngeal candidiasis, dysphonia (hoarseness). Endocrine LOW — Minimal systemic adrenal suppression at standard doses. Dosing & Administration dose Asthma Maintenance **Inhaled** 100-400 mcg **BD**. Titrate to lowest effective dose. Combo Therapy Often combined with Formoterol (Fostair) for MART regimens. Formulation & Access form Brand Names Qvar, Fostair (combo) Oral Routes Metered Dose Inhaler (50, 100 mcg/actuation). Prescribing & PBS Authority Required (PBS). Indications & Use ind Primary Maintenance treatment of asthma. Clinical Place First-line preventer. The extra-fine particle formulation (Qvar) means 100mcg of Beclometasone provides the same lung effect as 200mcg of older ICS formulations. Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — Acute asthma attacks (preventer, not a reliever). Pregnancy CAUTION — **Pregnancy** Category B3; avoid/initiate only if the expected benefit outweighs fetal risk. Paediatric CAUTION — Do not use QVAR in children < 5 years; safety and effectiveness are not established. Key Interactions inter Pharmacokinetic MODERATE — CYP3A4 inhibitors can slightly raise systemic levels, but much less dangerous than with Fluticasone. Monitoring & Cautions mon Bedside MANDATORY — Ensure 'rinse and spit' routine to prevent thrush. Clinical Pearls pearl The Small Airways Asthma isn't just in the big bronchi; it affects the tiny terminal bronchioles. The ultra-fine particles of Qvar (Beclometasone) physically reach deeper into the lungs than standard Flixotide, offering superior control in difficult cases. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Glucocorticoid receptor agonist. Suppresses airway inflammation, reduces mucosal oedema, and decreases hyper-responsiveness. Onset Days to weeks for full effect. Half-life 2-3 hours. High first-pass metabolism minimizes systemic absorption of the swallowed fraction. Sources src Source Review Source-backed review 2026-05-11: QVAR/beclometasone Australian PI and CMI source pack. Entry checked for dosing, access, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and app structure.\nClass & Role Topical/Inhaled Glucocorticoid — Inhaled Corticosteroid (ICS). Route / Formulation Metered Dose Inhaler (50, 100 mcg/actuation). (Qvar, Fostair (combo)) Usual Dose & Max **Inhaled** 100-400 mcg **BD**. Titrate to lowest effective dose. Max **800 mcg/day**. Key Indication Doses Asthma Maintenance: **Inhaled** 100-400 mcg **BD**. Titrate to lowest effective dose. Combo Therapy: Often combined with Formoterol (Fostair) for MART regimens. Best Uses Beclometasone is a standard inhaled corticosteroid for asthma preventer therapy, but requires consistent use for effect and mouth rinsing to prevent oral candidiasis. Avoid / Cautions Acute asthma attacks (preventer, not a reliever). **Pregnancy** Category B3. Key Risks Oral/Pharyngeal: Oropharyngeal candidiasis, dysphonia (hoarseness). Endocrine: Minimal systemic adrenal suppression at standard doses. Key Interactions CYP3A4 inhibitors can slightly raise systemic levels, but much less dangerous than with Fluticasone. Monitoring Ensure 'rinse and spit' routine to prevent thrush. Clinical Pearl Asthma isn't just in the big bronchi; it affects the tiny terminal bronchioles. The ultra-fine particles of Qvar (Beclometasone) physically reach deeper into the lungs than standard Flixotide, offering superior control in difficult cases.","contentHash":"98e33a8bee78b1df046533d07262517b764e447dd6dc1b2a2c1e91254af1a629","publicationFingerprint":"fe184b6f13981c2fcc975a7b1b487ffd9bb627750646d31288170b834948be5b","governanceFingerprint":"6473895da16d5508d03ffea998bc94f9de73dd719d70d551126b969e94bd3eab","lineageFingerprint":"4f53cda18c2baa0c0354bb5f9a3ecbe5ed12ab4d8e11ba873c2f11161202b945","publicMetadataFingerprint":"652c66886ed93a11f74a76f57869adb9e0a11e152bb3194f612e6f3248895bf5","record":{"version":"site-content-record-v1","logicalId":"medications:beclometasone","producerClass":"dynamic_registry","domain":"medications","route":"/medications/beclometasone","title":"Beclometasone","body":"Medication: Beclometasone\nClass: Steroid\nSubclass: Topical/Inhaled Glucocorticoid\nSchedule: S4\nTag: STEROID\nRapid Summary summary Overview Inhaled Corticosteroid (ICS). Formulated as extra-fine particles that penetrate deep into the small airways, providing excellent control of chronic asthma inflammation. Maximum Dose Max **800 mcg/day**. Clinical Focus Must use a spacer and rinse/spit after every dose to prevent oral thrush and vocal cord myopathy. Bottom Line Beclometasone is a standard inhaled corticosteroid for asthma preventer therapy, but requires consistent use for effect and mouth rinsing to prevent oral candidiasis. Risk Profile risk Oral/Pharyngeal HIGH — Oropharyngeal candidiasis, dysphonia (hoarseness). Endocrine LOW — Minimal systemic adrenal suppression at standard doses. Dosing & Administration dose Asthma Maintenance **Inhaled** 100-400 mcg **BD**. Titrate to lowest effective dose. Combo Therapy Often combined with Formoterol (Fostair) for MART regimens. Formulation & Access form Bra