, acute severe constipation. Clinical Place Highly effective 'push' laxative. Usually reserved for when Senna is ineffective or rapid PR action is needed. Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — Ileus, intestinal obstruction, acute surgical abdomen (e.g., appendicitis). Pregnancy CAUTION — **Pregnancy** Category A; reserve stimulant laxatives for short-term use when gentler options are inadequate. Key Interactions inter Pharmacokinetic CRITICAL — Antacids, Milk, and PPIs (Pantoprazole). These raise gastric pH, causing the enteric coating of Bisacodyl to dissolve in the stomach instead of the colon, leading to severe vomiting and gastric cramps. Separate by 2 hours. Monitoring & Cautions mon Bedside MANDATORY — Monitor bowel chart. Cease immediately once bowels have opened to avoid griping and diarrhea. Clinical Pearls pearl The Suppository Speed Oral bisacodyl takes all night to work. A bisacodyl suppository (**PR**) works in 20 minutes. Use the suppository if the ward needs an immediate result. Not for Daily Use Chronic use causes 'cathartic colon' (loss of normal bowel tone) and dependence. Restrict to short-term acute use. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Contact laxative. Acts directly on the colonic mucosa to stimulate parasympathetic reflexes, increasing peristalsis and fluid secretion. Onset **PO** 6-12 hours. **PR** 15-60 mins. Half-life Minimal systemic absorption (acts locally in gut lumen). Sources src Source Review Source-backed review 2026-05-11: DULCOLAX/BISALAX bisacodyl Australian PI source pack. Entry checked for dosing, access, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and app structure.\nClass & Role Stimulant Laxative — Potent diphenylmethane stimulant laxative. Route / Formulation Enteric-coated tablets (5 mg). (Dulcolax, Bisalax) Usual Dose & Max **PO** 5-10 mg **NOCTE**. OR **PR** 10 mg suppository STAT. Max **10 mg/day** (oral) or **10 mg** (rectal). Key Indication Doses S