on. Monitoring & Cautions mon Bedside MANDATORY — Monitor closely for akathisia. Because of the 3-week half-life, you must wait 2-3 weeks between dose increases to accurately assess the patient's true steady-state tolerance. Laboratory Routine **BSL** and **Weight**. Clinical Pearls pearl The D3 Difference Standard antipsychotics block D2 to stop hallucinations, but this worsens apathy and motivation. Cariprazine's unique D3 partial-agonism gently stimulates the brain's reward centers, waking the patient up and restoring emotional drive. The Ghost Side-Effect If a patient develops severe akathisia and you stop the Cariprazine, the akathisia will NOT go away the next day. The active metabolite will remain in their brain for another 3 weeks. You must prescribe Propranolol or Benztropine to bridge them until it washes out. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Partial agonist at D3 and D2 receptors, with a 10-fold higher affinity for D3. D3 receptors are heavily localized in the ventral striatum (reward, motivation, and emotion pathways). Onset Weeks. Half-life Parent drug: 2-4 days. Major active metabolite (DDCAR): 1-3 WEEKS. CYP3A4 metabolism. Sources src Source Review Source Check - REAGILA ARTG/PI/AusPMDS and PBS search checked 2026-05-10 for antipsychotic/LAI batch appraisal: identity/access, indication, dosing, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and source-row status.\nClass & Role SGA / Partial D2/D3 — Unique partial agonist with a massive affinity for the Dopamine D3 receptor over the D2 receptor. Route / Formulation Capsules (1.5 mg, 3 mg, 4.5 mg, 6 mg). (Reagila) Usual Dose & Max Start **PO** 1.5 mg **OD**. Titrate slowly (e.g., every 1-2 weeks) to 3-6 mg/day. Max **6 mg/day**. Key Indication Doses Schizophrenia: Start **PO** 1.5 mg **OD**. Titrate slowly (e.g., every 1-2 weeks) to 3-6 mg/day. Bipolar Mania: Start **PO** 1.5 mg **OD**. Titrate to 3-6 mg/day. Renal / Hepatic: Avoid if severe renal (**eGFR**