cephalosporin. Route / Formulation Capsules (250 mg, 500 mg). Oral liquid (125 mg/5mL, 250 mg/5mL). (Keflex, Rilexine) Usual Dose & Max **PO** 500 mg **QID**. Max **4 g/day**. Key Indication Doses Cellulitis / Skin Infection: **PO** 500 mg **QID**. Uncomplicated UTI: **PO** 500 mg **BD** for 5 days. Renal Impairment: Max 500 mg **BD** if **eGFR** < 30. Best Uses Cephalexin is a safe, well-tolerated first-generation cephalosporin for skin and simple UTIs, but has limited gram-negative coverage and ~2% cross-reactivity in penicillin allergy. Avoid / Cautions Cephalosporin anaphylaxis. Avoid in severe immediate (IgE) Penicillin allergy (e.g., hives/anaphylaxis). Safe to use if penicillin allergy is just a mild childhood rash. **Pregnancy** Category A. Key Risks Gastrointestinal: Nausea, diarrhea, mild C. diff risk. Immunological: Hypersensitivity rash, rare anaphylaxis. Renal: Interstitial nephritis. Key Interactions Generally free of major liver/CYP interactions. Monitoring Check **eGFR** if using high doses (**QID**) in the elderly. Clinical Pearl Cephalexin has a very short half-life (1 hour). For a tissue infection like cellulitis, dosing it **QID** (every 6 hours) is mandatory to maintain blood levels. Dosing it BD for skin guarantees clinical failure.","sourceRole":"clinical_reference","access":"public","validationStatus":"unverified","sourceStatus":"current","sourceLineage":[],"publicationVersion":"c8515ef64784fd90cfaeacf259dd113e225222e8e423b7a0314a457baa95d1d7","contentHash":"68f3c7618b45398fd4f38ed1a122cd7d9113b5901765b46e7c10e5756290ed25"},"renderPayload":{"slug":"cephalexin","name":"Cephalexin","class":"Antibiotic","subclass":"1st Gen Cephalosporin","category":"Infectious Diseases - Cephalosporins","accent":"#ea580c","tag":"CEPHALOSPORIN","schedule":"S4","stats":[{"label":"Max Dose","value":"4 g/day","cls":"hi","flag":"hi"},{"label":"Half-life","value":"1 h","cls":"","flag":""},{"label":"Cross-Allergy","value":"CAUTION","cls":"warn","flag":""},{"label":"Renal Adj.","value":"DOSE RED.","cls":"warn","flag":""}],"sections":[{"title":"Rapid Summary","type":"summary","rows":[{"key":"Overview","val":"First-generation oral cephalosporin. Outstanding coverage against MSSA and basic Strep. The workhorse for mild-to-moderate skin infections and simple UTIs.","tags":[]},{"key":"Maximum Dose","val":"Max **4 g/day**.","tags":[]},{"key":"Clinical Focus","val":"Approximately 1-5% of patients with a true penicillin allergy will cross-react with cephalosporins.","tags":[]},{"key":"Bottom Line","val":"Cephalexin is a safe, well-tolerated first-generation cephalosporin for skin and simple UTIs, but has limited gram-negative coverage and ~2% cross-reactivity in penicillin allergy.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Risk Profile","type":"risk","rows":[{"key":"Gastrointestinal","val":"HIGH — Nausea, diarrhea, mild C. diff risk.","tags":[]},{"key":"Immunological","val":"MODERATE — Hypersensitivity rash, rare anaphylaxis.","tags":[]},{"key":"Renal","val":"LOW — Interstitial nephritis.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Dosing & Administration","type":"dose","rows":[{"key":"Cellulitis / Skin Infection","val":"**PO** 500 mg **QID**.","tags":[]},{"key":"Uncomplicated UTI","val":"**PO** 500 mg **BD** for 5 days.","tags":[]},{"key":"Renal Impairment","val":"MANDATORY — Max 500 mg **BD** if **eGFR** < 30.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Formulation & Access","type":"form","rows":[{"key":"Brand Names","val":"Keflex, Rilexine","tags":[]},{"key":"Oral Routes","val":"Capsules (250 mg, 500 mg). Oral liquid (125 mg/5mL, 250 mg/5mL).","tags":[]},{"key":"Prescribing & PBS","val":"Unrestricted General Benefit (S4).","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Indications & Use","type":"ind","rows":[{"key":"Primary","val":"Staphylococcal/Streptococcal skin infections, cystitis in pregnancy.","tags":["PBS","TGA"]},{"key":"Clinical Place","val":"Oral step-down from IV Cefazolin. Excellent safe choice for UTIs during pregnancy.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Contraindications & Populations","type":"contra","rows":[{"key":"Absolute","val":"CRITICAL — Cephalosporin anaphylaxis. Avoid in severe immediate (IgE) Penicillin allergy (e.g., hives/anaphylaxis). Safe to use if penicillin allergy is just a mild childhood rash.","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["allergy-pcn","allergy-ceph"],"action":"contraindication","severity":"danger","note":"Inferred from existing decision-section wording; review before clinical use."}},{"key":"Pregnancy","val":"SAFE — **Pregnancy** Category A.","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["pregnancy"],"action":"info","severity":"info","note":"Cephalexin pregnancy row is Category A/source-reviewed safe-use context, not an automatic contraindication alert."}}]},{"title":"Key Interactions","type":"inter","rows":[{"key":"Pharmacokinetic","val":"LOW — Generally free of major liver/CYP interactions.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Monitoring & Cautions","type":"mon","rows":[{"key":"Laboratory","val":"Check **eGFR** if using high doses (**QID**) in the elderly.","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["elderly"],"action":"dose-adjust","severity":"caution","match":{"age":{}},"note":"Inferred from existing decision-section wording; review before clinical use."}}]},{"title":"Clinical Pearls","type":"pearl","rows":[{"key":"QID vs BD","val":"Cephalexin has a very short half-life (1 hour). For a tissue infection like cellulitis, dosing it **QID** (every 6 hours) is mandatory to maintain blood levels. Dosing it BD for skin guarantees clinical failure.","tags":[]},{"key":"UTI Exception","val":"Because Cephalexin concentrates massively in the urine, it *can* be dosed **BD** for simple cystitis, as the urinary levels remain high enough for 12 hours.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics","type":"evid","rows":[{"key":"Mechanism","val":"Bactericidal. Binds to PBPs, inhibiting cell wall synthesis. Highly resistant to staphylococcal beta-lactamase.","tags":[]},{"key":"Onset","val":"**PO** 1 hour.","tags":[]},{"key":"Half-life","val":"1 hour. Almost 100% renally excreted unchanged.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Sources","type":"src","rows":[{"key":"Source Review","val":"Source Check - CEPHALEXIN-WGR ARTG/PI and PBS search checked 2026-05-13 for high-risk opioid/general batch appraisal: identity/access, indication, dosing, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, pregnancy/lactation, patient-context metadata, and source-row status."}]}],"quick":[{"label":"Class & Role","value":"1st Gen Cephalosporin — First-generation oral cephalosporin."},{"label":"Route / Formulation","value":"Capsules (250 mg, 500 mg). Oral liquid (125 mg/5mL, 250 mg/5mL). (Keflex, Rilexine)"},{"label":"Usual Dose & Max","value":"**PO** 500 mg **QID**. Max **4 g/day**."},{"label":"Key Indication Doses","value":"Cellulitis / Skin Infection: **PO** 500 mg **QID**.  Uncomplicated UTI: **PO** 500 mg **BD** for 5 days.  Renal Impairment: Max 500 mg **BD** if **eGFR** < 30."},{"label":"Best Uses","value":"Cephalexin is a safe, well-tolerated first-generation cephalosporin for skin and simple UTIs, but has limited gram-negative coverage and ~2% cross-reactivity in penicillin allergy."},{"label":"Avoid / Cautions","value":"Cephalosporin anaphylaxis. Avoid in severe immediate (IgE) Penicillin allergy (e.g., hives/anaphylaxis). Safe to use if penicillin allergy is just a mild childhood rash. **Pregnancy** Category A."},{"label":"Key Risks","value":"Gastrointestinal: Nausea, diarrhea, mild C. diff risk.  Immunological: Hypersensitivity rash, rare anaphylaxis.  Renal: Interstitial nephritis."},{"label":"Key Interactions","value":"Generally free of major liver/CYP interactions."},{"label":"Monitoring","value":"Check **eGFR** if using high doses (**QID**) in the elderly."},{"label":"Clinical Pearl","value":"Cephalexin has a very short half-life (1 hour). For a tissue infection like cellulitis, dosing it **QID** (every 6 hours) is mandatory to maintain blood levels. Dosing it BD for skin guarantees clinical failure."}]}},{"logicalId":"medications:cetirizine","logicalDocumentId":"75024a1c-41a9-5e44-acc0-a9e978a57547","logicalChunkId":"5329450e-6b69-560f-a741-7eb063b160e4","normalizedText":"Medication: Cetirizine\nClass: Allergy\nSubclass: H1 Antihistamine (2nd Gen)\nSchedule: S2\nTag: ANTIHISTAMINE\nRapid Summary summary Overview Second-generation H1-receptor antagonist. Highly effective for allergic rhinitis and urticaria. 'Non-sedating', but actually the most sedating of the 2nd-gen class. Maximum Dose Max **20 mg/day** (Standard dose 10 mg). Clinical Focus Preferred for intense acute urticaria as it works rapidly, but warn patients it may cause mild drowsiness. Bottom Line Cetirizine is a reliable second-generation antihistamine for allergic rhinitis and urticaria, but causes mild sedation in some patients despite being classified as non-sedating. Risk Profile risk Neurological MODERATE — Drowsiness, fatigue (Occurs in ~14% of patients, highest among 2nd gen antihistamines). Gastrointestinal LOW — Dry mouth (very minimal anticholinergic effect). Dosing & Administration dose Allergic Rhinitis / Urticaria **PO** 10 mg **OD**. Severe Urticaria **PO** 10 mg **BD** (Off-label specialist escalation). Renal Impairment MANDATORY — Reduce dose to 5 mg OD if **eGFR** 10-30. Formulation & Access form Brand Names Zyrtec, Zilarex Oral Routes Tablets (10 mg), Oral liquid (1 mg/mL), Oral drops (10 mg/mL). Prescribing & PBS Pharmacy Medicine (S2). PBS listed for specific chronic conditions. Indications & Use ind Primary Seasonal allergic rhinitis, chronic idiopathic urticaria. Clinical Place First-line antihistamine when rapid onset is required and mild sedation is acceptable or desired (e.g., nighttime itching). Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — Severe renal impairment without dose adjustment. Pregnancy CAUTION — **Pregnancy** Category B2. (Loratadine is typically preferred). Key Interactions inter Pharmacodynamic MODERATE — Additive CNS depression if combined with alcohol or sedatives (due to slight BBB penetration). Monitoring & Cautions mon Bedside Warn against driving or operating machinery until they know how the drug affects them. Laboratory Check **eGFR** in the elderly before prescribing. Clinical Pearls pearl The 'Non-Sedating' Myth Cetirizine is the active metabolite of hydroxyzine (a heavily sedating 1st-gen antihistamine). It crosses the blood-brain barrier just enough to cause drowsiness in 1 in 7 people. Prescribe at **NOCTE** to utilize this for sleep. Renal Clearance Unlike Loratadine/Fexofenadine which are hepatic, Cetirizine relies on the kidneys. Dose must be halved in severe CKD. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Competitive, highly selective antagonist of the peripheral H1 histamine receptor. Stabilizes mast cells. Onset **PO** 20-60 mins (Very fast for oral). Duration 24 h. Half-life 8 hours. Excreted largely unchanged in urine (requires renal adjustment). Sources src Source Review Source-backed review 2026-05-11: ALZENE/cetirizine Australian PI. Entry checked for dosing, access, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and app structure.\nClass & Role H1 Antihistamine (2nd Gen) — Second-generation H1-receptor antagonist. Route / Formulation Tablets (10 mg), Oral liquid (1 mg/mL), Oral drops (10 mg/mL). (Zyrtec, Zilarex) Usual Dose & Max **PO** 10 mg **OD**. Max **20 mg/day** (Standard dose 10 mg). Key Indication Doses Allergic Rhinitis / Urticaria: **PO** 10 mg **OD**. Severe Urticaria: **PO** 10 mg **BD** (Off-label specialist escalation). Renal Impairment: Reduce dose to 5 mg OD if **eGFR** 10-30. Best Uses Cetirizine is a reliable second-generation antihistamine for allergic rhinitis and urticaria, but causes mild sedation in some patients despite being classified as non-sedating. Avoid / Cautions Severe renal impairment without dose adjustment. **Pregnancy** Category B2. (Loratadine is typically preferred). Key Risks Neurological: Drowsiness, fatigue (Occurs in ~14% of patients, highest among 2nd gen antihistamines). Gastrointestinal: Dry mouth (very minimal anticholinergic effect). Key Interactions Additive CNS depression if combined with alcohol or sedatives (due to slight BBB penetration). Monitoring Warn against driving or operating machinery until they know how the drug affects them. Check **eGFR** in the elderly before prescribing. Clinical Pearl Cetirizine is the active metabolite of hydroxyzine (a heavily sedating 1st-gen antihistamine). It crosses the blood-brain barrier just enough to cause drowsiness in 1 in 7 people. Prescribe at **NOCTE** to utilize this for sleep.","contentHash":"3c88e0f1ed00eaa651880574e0f780ec752f4fcc0c85a1e94d700fe56d4eca2f","publicationFingerprint":"76cfa0a815f40868a9283b7420dbccefaeb507c8d3b7108b79844c77bd37bf48","governanceFingerprint":"6473895da16d5508d03ffea998bc94f9de73dd719d70d551126b969e94bd3eab","lineageFingerprint":"4f53cda18c2baa0c0354bb5f9a3ecbe5ed12ab4d8e11ba873c2f11161202b945","publicMetadataFingerprint":"b31b6c66c6200f7747f644110347d75e3b8a1d9701cce77f22c718ed55cd6829","record":{"version":"site-content-record-v1","logicalId":"medications:cetirizine","producerClass":"dynamic_registry","domain":"medications","route":"/medications/cetirizine","title":"Cetirizine","body":"Medication: Cetirizine\nClass: Allergy\nSubclass: H1 Antihistamine (2nd Gen)\nSchedule: S2\nTag: ANTIHISTAMINE\nRapid Summary summary Overview Second-generation H1-receptor antagonist. Highly effective for allergic rhinitis and urticaria. 'Non-sedating', but actually the most sedating of the 2nd-gen class. Maximum Dose Max **20 mg/day** (Standard dose 10 mg). Clinical Focus Preferred for intense acute urticaria as it works rapidly, but warn patients it may cause mild drowsiness. Bottom Line Cetirizine is a reliable second-generation antihistamine for allergic rhinitis and urticaria, but causes mild sedation in some patients despite being classified as non-sedating. Risk Profile risk Neurological MODERATE — Drowsiness, fatigue (Occurs in ~14% of patients, highest among 2nd gen antihistamines). Gastrointestinal LOW — Dry mouth (very minimal anticholinergic effect). Dosing & Administration dose Allergic Rhinitis / Urticaria **PO** 10 mg **OD**. Severe Urticaria **PO** 10 mg **BD** (Off-label specialist escalation). Renal Impairment MANDATORY — Reduce dose to 5 mg OD if **eGFR** 10-30. Formulation & Access form Brand Names Zyrtec, Zilarex Oral Routes Tablets (10 mg), Oral liquid (1 mg/mL), Oral drops (10 mg/mL). Prescribing & PBS Pharmacy Medicine (S2). PBS listed for specific chronic conditions. Indications & Use ind Primary Seasonal allergic rhinitis, chronic idiopathic urticaria. Clinical Place First-line antihistamine when rapid onset is required and mild sedation is acceptable or desired (e.g., nighttime itching). Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — Severe renal impairment without dose adjustment. Pregnancy CAUTION — **Pregnancy** Category B2. (Loratadine is typically preferred). Key Interactions inter Pharmacodynamic MODERATE — Additive CNS depression if combined with alcohol or sedatives (due to slight BBB penetration). Monitoring & Cautions mon Bedside Warn against driving or operating machinery until they know how the drug affects them. Laboratory Check **eGFR** in the elderly before prescribing. Clinical Pearls pearl The 'Non-Sedating' Myth Cetirizine is the active metabolite of hydroxyzine (a heavily sedating 1st-gen antihistamine). It crosses the blood-brain barrier just enough to cause drowsiness in 1 in 7 people. Prescribe at **NOCTE** to utilize this for sleep. Renal Clearance Unlike Loratadine/Fexofenadine which are hepatic, Cetirizine relies on the kidneys. Dose must be halved in severe CKD. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Competitive, highly selective antagonist of the peripheral H1 histamine receptor. Stabilizes mast cells. Onset **PO** 20-60 mins (Very fast for oral). Duration 24 h. Half-life 8 hours. Excreted largely unchanged in urine (requires renal adjustment). Sources src