ve CNS depression with Benzodiazepines, Alcohol, and Opioids.","tags":[]},{"key":"Pharmacodynamic","val":"HIGH — Additive anticholinergic burden with TCAs and Antipsychotics.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Monitoring & Cautions","type":"mon","rows":[{"key":"Bedside","val":"MANDATORY — In Serotonin Syndrome, monitor core temperature, rigidity, and clonus strictly. Ensure NGT placement is correct before pushing crushed tablets.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Clinical Pearls","type":"pearl","rows":[{"key":"The NGT Requirement","val":"Because Serotonin Syndrome causes rigidity, altered consciousness, and hyperthermia, patients often cannot swallow pills safely. Crushing Cyproheptadine and flushing it down a nasogastric tube is a mandatory ICU skill.","tags":[]},{"key":"The Hunger Pill","val":"Because of its intense anti-serotonin action in the hypothalamus, it completely turns off the brain's satiety (fullness) signal. It is heavily utilized off-label to force weight gain in severe anorexia nervosa or cancer cachexia.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics","type":"evid","rows":[{"key":"Mechanism","val":"Potent antagonist at H1 (histamine) and 5-HT2A (serotonin) receptors. Also exerts strong anticholinergic (muscarinic) and mild calcium-channel blocking effects.","tags":[]},{"key":"Onset","val":"**PO** 30-60 mins.","tags":[]},{"key":"Half-life","val":"16 hours. Hepatically metabolised.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Sources","type":"src","rows":[{"key":"Source Review","val":"Source Check - PERIACTIN ARTG/PI and PBS search checked 2026-05-12 for sedative/acute-calming batch appraisal: identity/access, indication, dosing, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, pregnancy/lactation, patient-context metadata, and source-row status."}]}],"quick":[{"label":"Class & Role","value":"5HT2A Antagonist — First-generation antihistamine with exceptionally potent anti-serotonergic (5-HT2A) properties."},{"label":"Route / Formulation","value":"Tablets (4 mg). (Periactin)"},{"label":"Usual Dose & Max","value":"**PO** / **NGT** 12 mg STAT, followed by 2 mg every 2 hours until symptoms resolve (or 8 mg q6h). Max 32 mg/day."},{"label":"Key Indication Doses","value":"Serotonin Syndrome (Antidote): **PO** / **NGT** 12 mg STAT, followed by 2 mg every 2 hours until symptoms resolve (or 8 mg q6h). Max 32 mg/day.  Severe Pruritus / Urticaria: **PO** 4-8 mg **TDS**.  Appetite Stimulation (Off-Label): **PO** 2-4 mg **TDS**."},{"label":"Best Uses","value":"Cyproheptadine is a first-generation antihistamine used as an appetite stimulant and serotonin syndrome antidote, but causes significant sedation and weight gain."},{"label":"Avoid / Cautions","value":"Narrow-angle glaucoma, severe BPH/urinary retention, concurrent MAOI use (paradoxical, but standard for most antihistamines), neonates. **Pregnancy** Category A."},{"label":"Key Risks","value":"Neurological: Profound sedation, dizziness, confusion, paradoxical excitation in children.  Gastrointestinal: Intense appetite stimulation, weight gain, dry mouth (anticholinergic effect).  Genitourinary: Urinary retention."},{"label":"Key Interactions","value":"Additive CNS depression with Benzodiazepines, Alcohol, and Opioids.  Additive anticholinergic burden with TCAs and Antipsychotics."},{"label":"Monitoring","value":"In Serotonin Syndrome, monitor core temperature, rigidity, and clonus strictly. Ensure NGT placement is correct before pushing crushed tablets."},{"label":"Clinical Pearl","value":"Because Serotonin Syndrome causes rigidity, altered consciousness, and hyperthermia, patients often cannot swallow pills safely. Crushing Cyproheptadine and flushing it down a nasogastric tube is a mandatory ICU skill."}]}},{"logicalId":"medications:dabigatran","logicalDocumentId":"6f24c581-c7a4-5bf4-ba5f-5127813b74fc","logicalChunkId":"9da8ea08-c311-59ab-b78e-34ae66360cc1","normalizedText":"Medication: Dabigatran\nClass: Anticoagulant\nSubclass: Direct Thrombin Inhibitor\nSchedule: S4\nTag: DOAC\nRapid Summary summary Overview Direct Oral Anticoagulant (DOAC). The only DOAC that acts directly on Thrombin (Factor IIa) rather than Factor Xa. Highly effective but has strict storage and renal limitations. Maximum Dose Max **150 mg BD**. Clinical Focus The capsules are extremely moisture-sensitive. If removed from the foil blister and put into a dosette box, they rapidly degrade into a useless paste. The patient will clot. Bottom Line Dabigatran is the only DOAC with a specific reversal agent (idarucizumab) for AF and VTE, but is predominantly renally cleared and contraindicated in severe renal impairment. Risk Profile risk Haematological CRITICAL — Major haemorrhage (especially GI bleeding in the elderly). Gastrointestinal HIGH — Dyspepsia, gastritis (The capsule contains a tartaric acid core to aid absorption, which burns the stomach). Dosing & Administration dose Non-Valvular AF / VTE Treatment **PO** 150 mg **BD**. Elderly (> 75 yrs) / High Bleed Risk MANDATORY — **PO** 110 mg **BD**. Renal Impairment CRITICAL — Avoid entirely if **eGFR** < 30 (Highly renally cleared). Formulation & Access form Brand Names Pradaxa Oral Routes Capsules (75 mg, 110 mg, 150 mg). Do NOT crush, chew, or open (increases bioavailability by 75%, causing fatal bleeding). Prescribing & PBS Authority Required (PBS). Indications & Use ind Primary Stroke prevention in non-valvular AF, treatment and prevention of DVT/PE, VTE prophylaxis post-orthopaedic surgery. Clinical Place Highly effective alternative to Warfarin. Has a specific, instantaneous reversal agent (Idarucizumab). Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — Mechanical heart valves, **eGFR** < 30, active bleeding, active GI ulcer. Pregnancy ABSOLUTE — **Pregnancy** Category C. Key Interactions inter Pharmacokinetic CRITICAL — P-gp inhibitors (e.g., Amiodarone, Verapamil, Clarithromycin). These drastically increase Dabigatran absorption, causing severe bleeding. Dose reduction to 110mg BD is mandatory. Pharmacodynamic CRITICAL — NSAIDs, SSRIs, Aspirin (Additive bleeding risk). Monitoring & Cautions mon Laboratory MANDATORY — Baseline and 6-monthly **eGFR**. Standard INR/aPTT are completely unreliable to measure efficacy. Bedside Warn the patient not to use a dosette box/pill organizer for this specific drug. Clinical Pearls pearl The Dyspepsia Trap Because of the tartaric acid core, up to 10% of patients get severe indigestion and stop taking the drug, risking a massive stroke. Taking it with a heavy meal and a large glass of water fixes this. The Instant Antidote If a patient on Dabigatran comes in with a massive intracranial hemorrhage, you can give Idarucizumab (Praxbind) IV. It binds to the Dabigatran in the blood and neutralizes it completely in under 5 minutes. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Prodrug. Converted to active dabigatran, which acts as a potent, competitive, and reversible direct inhibitor of Thrombin (Factor IIa), halting the conversion of fibrinogen to fibrin. Onset **PO** 2 hours. Duration 12-24 h. Half-life 12-17 hours (Requires strict **BD** dosing). 80% renally excreted. Sources src Source Review Source Check - PRADAXA ARTG/PI, TGA dabigatran/oral anticoagulant safety updates, and PBS search checked 2026-05-13 for high-risk opioid/general batch appraisal: identity/access, indication, dosing, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, pregnancy/lactation, patient-context metadata, and source-row status.\nClass & Role Direct Thrombin Inhibitor — Direct Oral Anticoagulant (DOAC). Route / Formulation Capsules (75 mg, 110 mg, 150 mg). Do NOT crush, chew, or open (increases bioavailability by 75%, causing fatal bleeding). (Pradaxa) Usual Dose & Max **PO** 150 mg **BD**. Max **150 mg BD**. Key Indication Doses Non-Valvular AF / VTE Treatment: **PO** 150 mg **BD**. Elderly (> 75 yrs) / High Bleed Risk: **PO** 110 mg **BD**. Renal Impairment: Avoid entirely if **eGFR** < 30 (Highly renally cleared). Best Uses Dabigatran is the only DOAC with a specific reversal agent (idarucizumab) for AF and VTE, but is predominantly renally cleared and contraindicated in severe renal impairment. Avoid / Cautions Mechanical heart valves, **eGFR** < 30, active bleeding, active GI ulcer. **Pregnancy** Category C. Key Risks Haematological: Major haemorrhage (especially GI bleeding in the elderly). Gastrointestinal: Dyspepsia, gastritis (The capsule contains a tartaric acid core to aid absorption, which burns the stomach). Key Interactions P-gp inhibitors (e.g., Amiodarone, Verapamil, Clarithromycin). These drastically increase Dabigatran absorption, causing severe bleeding. Dose reduction to 110mg BD is mandatory. NSAIDs, SSRIs, Aspirin (Additive bleeding risk). Monitoring Baseline and 6-monthly **eGFR**. Standard INR/aPTT are completely unreliable to measure efficacy. Warn the patient not to use a dosette box/pill organizer for this specific drug. Clinical Pearl Because of the tartaric acid core, up to 10% of patients get severe indigestion and stop taking the drug, risking a massive stroke. Taking it with a heavy meal and a large glass of water fixes this.","contentHash":"1bc111385c2f28fdaa04f5948e520225977bb729021f141ddd454b6b9606464d","publicationFingerprint":"960cac8b5808ac03910f5b216d4fe5ecc848206a107220e8e1b385fa5f69c1d4","governanceFingerprint":"6473895da16d5508d03ffea998bc94f9de73dd719d70d551126b969e94bd3eab","lineageFingerprint":"4f53cda18c2baa0c0354bb5f9a3ecbe5ed12ab4d8e11ba873c2f11161202b945","publicMetadataFingerprint":"47dd8972f29093dbcabc519157ced7057180dad2dcb5cb589e3340bcea1a640a","record":{"version":"site-content-record-v1","logicalId":"medications:dabigatran","producerClass":"dynamic_registry","domain":"medications","route":"/medications/dabigatran","title":"Dabigatran","body":"Medication: Dabigatran\nClass: Anticoagulant\nSubclass: Direct Thrombin Inhibitor\nSchedule: S4\nTag: DOAC\nRapid Summary summary Overview Direct Oral Anticoagulant (DOAC). The only DOAC that acts directly on Thrombin (Factor IIa) rathe