*. Best Uses Docusate sodium is a gentle stool softener for patients where straining is contraindicated, but has weak evidence for efficacy and is often insufficient as monotherapy. Avoid / Cautions Intestinal obstruction. **Pregnancy** Category A. Widely used post-partum. Key Risks Gastrointestinal: Mild abdominal cramping, bitter taste (if liquid is used). Systemic: Extremely benign safety profile. Very safe in the elderly. Key Interactions Liquid paraffin (mineral oil laxatives). Docusate increases the systemic absorption of mineral oil, leading to lipoid pneumonia and hepatic granulomas. Never combine them. Monitoring Monitor bowel chart. Efficacy is often poor in severe constipation without a stimulant. Clinical Pearl Opioids paralyze the gut. Giving Docusate alone to a patient on Oxycodone just creates soft stool sitting in a paralyzed bowel. You MUST add Senna (the push).","sourceRole":"clinical_reference","access":"public","validationStatus":"unverified","sourceStatus":"current","sourceLineage":[],"publicationVersion":"c59e48d512d9463abea5d89b40391b84f9e6ee5565a6083d967de244fea026cc","contentHash":"d6017e3cb032821ad6431cfa9d9b07837549a96265f4c8ca2cd8ff4b0d131f6e"},"renderPayload":{"slug":"docusate-sodium","name":"Docusate sodium","class":"Aperient","subclass":"Stool Softener","category":"Gastrointestinal - Laxatives","accent":"#b45309","tag":"SOFTENER","schedule":"UNSCHEDULED","stats":[{"label":"Max Dose","value":"480 mg/day","cls":"hi","flag":"hi"},{"label":"Onset","value":"1-3 Days","cls":"","flag":""},{"label":"Stimulation","value":"NONE","cls":"warn","flag":""},{"label":"Efficacy","value":"LOW","cls":"warn","flag":""}],"sections":[{"title":"Rapid Summary","type":"summary","rows":[{"key":"Overview","val":"Anionic surfactant (detergent). Lowers the surface tension of stool, allowing water and fats to mix in, softening the fecal mass. 'Mush without the push'.","tags":[]},{"key":"Maximum Dose","val":"Max **480 mg/day**.","tags":[]},{"key":"Clinical Focus","v