aindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — Anaphylaxis to carbapenems/penicillins. Known Pseudomonas infection. Pregnancy SAFE — **Pregnancy** Category B3. Key Interactions inter Pharmacokinetic CRITICAL — Sodium Valproate. Ertapenem rapidly destroys valproate blood levels, dropping them to sub-therapeutic levels within 24 hours, guaranteeing breakthrough seizures in epileptics. AVOID. Monitoring & Cautions mon Laboratory MANDATORY — Check **eGFR** to ensure the 1g dose is safe. Track **FBC** if on prolonged OPAT courses. Bedside Observe for neurological changes, myoclonus or seizures, especially in older adults or renal impairment. Clinical Pearls pearl The OPAT Champion If a patient is clinically stable but their urine grows an E. coli resistant to Ceftriaxone and Tazocin (an ESBL), they usually require IV Meropenem TDS, keeping them stuck in hospital. Ertapenem 1g OD allows them to go home immediately. The A-P-E Mnemonic Ertapenem is the 'A-P-E' exception among carbapenems: It does NOT cover Acinetobacter, Pseudomonas, or Enterococcus. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Binds PBPs, shattering the bacterial cell wall. Extremely resistant to destruction by beta-lactamases (including ESBLs). Onset **IV** Immediate. Duration 24 h. Half-life 4 hours (Highly protein bound to albumin, which keeps it in the blood far longer than Meropenem, allowing OD dosing). Sources src Source Review Source Check - INVANZ ertapenem ARTG/PI and PBS check checked 2026-05-13 for high-risk opioid/general batch appraisal: identity/access, indication, dosing, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, pregnancy/lactation, patient-context metadata, and source-row status.\nClass & Role Carbapenem — Highly convenient, once-daily IV carbapenem. Route / Formulation Vials (1 g powder) for **IV** infusion or deep **IM** injection (mixed with 1% lidocaine). (Invanz) Usual Dose & Max **IV** / **IM** 1 g **OD**. Max **1 g/day**. Key Indication Doses ESBL UTI / Intra-a