, 25, 50, 75, 100 mcg/hour). Prescribing & PBS Highly Restricted (S8). Authority Required (PBS). Indications & Use ind Primary Intra-operative analgesia, severe trauma, palliative cancer pain, chronic intractable pain. Clinical Place The gold standard in ICU/Anaesthetics due to instant onset/offset and cardiovascular stability. Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — Patch use in opioid-naive patients or for acute/post-op pain (Fatal overdose risk). Pregnancy CAUTION — **Pregnancy** Category C. Key Interactions inter Pharmacokinetic HIGH — **CYP3A4** inhibitors (Clarithromycin, Ketoconazole) block hepatic clearance, spiking blood levels and risking fatal apnoea. Pharmacodynamic CRITICAL — Benzodiazepines (Synergistic respiratory arrest). Monitoring & Cautions mon Bedside MANDATORY — For patches: No heat packs or hot baths. Heat massively accelerates absorption, causing lethal overdose. Ensure old patch is removed before placing a new one. Cardiac Continuous **SpO2**, **RR**, and **BP** when giving IV. Clinical Pearls pearl The Patch Lag A fentanyl patch takes 12-24 hours to reach therapeutic blood levels. Do NOT place a patch on a patient in agony and expect it to work today. You must bridge them with regular IV/PO opioids until the patch kicks in tomorrow. Micrograms! Fentanyl is dosed in MICROGRAMS. A standard starting IV dose is 25-50 mcg. Giving 1 mg (a standard morphine dose) of IV Fentanyl will kill the patient before you finish pushing the syringe. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Pure, highly potent mu-opioid agonist. Massive lipid solubility allows it to crash through the blood-brain barrier instantly. Onset **IV** 1-2 mins. **Topical** 12-24 hours to steady state. Duration **IV** 30-60 mins. **Topical** 72 h. Half-life IV is minutes (redistributes into fat). Patch has a 17-hour half-life *after* removal due to skin depot. Sources src Source Review Source Check - DUROGESIC ARTG/PI and PBS search checked 2026-05-13 for high-risk