specially with courses > 14 days. Avoid / Cautions History of flucloxacillin-induced hepatic dysfunction, severe penicillin allergy. **Pregnancy** Category B1. Key Risks Tissue: Severe chemical phlebitis and vein thrombosis via peripheral IV. Hepatic: Cholestatic hepatitis. Risk increases with age > 55 and courses > 14 days. Renal: Interstitial nephritis. Key Interactions Paracetamol. Co-administration of IV Flucloxacillin and high-dose Paracetamol in malnourished/elderly women can trigger severe High Anion Gap Metabolic Acidosis (HAGMA) due to 5-oxoproline accumulation. Monitoring Monitor **LFTs** regularly if used IV for prolonged periods (e.g., osteomyelitis). Inspect peripheral IV cannula sites strictly every shift for redness/pain. Clinical Pearl Oral flucloxacillin absorption drops by 50% if taken with food. Ward nurses routinely give it with meals to prevent nausea, guaranteeing sub-therapeutic blood levels and clinical failure. Prescribe it exactly 1 hr before meals.","sourceRole":"clinical_reference","access":"public","validationStatus":"unverified","sourceStatus":"current","sourceLineage":[],"publicationVersion":"1a2eb8130eb2f2fab32b124e02321b02c16ca61e2d3fdf5b265adda82503b25f","contentHash":"06ff0096fc37a57d2e24346cfdf947feaa6dd05c207d7e4d36c8b5fdfaa7dcc2"},"renderPayload":{"slug":"flucloxacillin","name":"Flucloxacillin","class":"Antibiotic","subclass":"Antistaphylococcal Penicillin","category":"Infectious Diseases - Penicillins","accent":"#ea580c","tag":"BETA-LACTAM","schedule":"S4","stats":[{"label":"Max Dose","value":"8 g/day (IV)","cls":"hi","flag":"hi"},{"label":"Half-life","value":"1 h","cls":"","flag":""},{"label":"Phlebitis","value":"HIGH RISK","cls":"warn","flag":"warn"},{"label":"Empty Stomach","value":"MANDATORY","cls":"good","flag":""}],"sections":[{"title":"Rapid Summary","type":"summary","rows":[{"key":"Overview","val":"Narrow-spectrum, highly potent anti-staphylococcal penicillin. The definitive first-line treatment for MSSA bacteremia and 