o) Oral Routes MDI (50, 125, 250 mcg/actuation). Dry powder Accuhaler/Ellipta. Prescribing & PBS Authority Required (PBS). Indications & Use ind Primary Asthma prophylaxis, severe COPD. Clinical Place Highly prescribed maintenance inhaler. The Furoate salt allows for ultra-convenient once-daily dosing. Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — Acute asthma attacks (not a reliever). Pregnancy CAUTION — **Pregnancy** Category B3 (budesonide is generally preferred in pregnancy, though fluticasone may be continued if already effective). Key Interactions inter Pharmacokinetic CRITICAL — **CYP3A4** inhibitors (Ritonavir, Cobicistat) completely block hepatic breakdown. Swallowed fluticasone builds up, causing profound iatrogenic Cushing's syndrome and adrenal crisis. Monitoring & Cautions mon Bedside MANDATORY — Ensure 'rinse and spit' routine to prevent thrush. Laboratory No specific routine laboratory tests required. Clinical Pearls pearl The HIV/Hep C Trap Never prescribe Fluticasone to a patient on HIV/HCV protease inhibitors (Ritonavir). It is a classic exam and ward error resulting in rapid, severe adrenal suppression. Pneumonia in COPD ICS use in COPD (especially Fluticasone) is statistically linked to a higher risk of developing pneumonia. Use ICS in COPD only if they have frequent exacerbations or an eosinophilic phenotype. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Potent glucocorticoid receptor agonist. Reduces airway inflammation. Modifies gene transcription. Onset Days. Full effect takes 1-2 weeks. Duration 12-24 h. Half-life 8-14 hours. First-pass metabolism is nearly 100%, meaning any drug swallowed is immediately destroyed by the liver, severely restricting systemic side effects. Sources src Source Review Source-backed review 2026-05-11: FLIXOTIDE/fluticasone Australian PI source pack. Entry checked for dosing, access, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and app structure.\nClass & Role Inhaled Glucocorticoid — Highly lipophilic, extremely potent inhaled corticosteroid. Route / Formulation MDI (50, 125, 250 mcg/actuation). Dry powder Accuhaler/Ellipta. (Flixotide, Seretide (combo), Breo Ellipta (combo)) Usual Dose & Max **Inhaled** 100-500 mcg **BD**. Max 1000 mcg/day. Key Indication Doses Asthma Maintenance (Propionate): **Inhaled** 100-500 mcg **BD**. Max 1000 mcg/day. Asthma Maintenance (Furoate): **Inhaled** 100-200 mcg **OD**. COPD (Combo only): **Inhaled** Typically given combined with Salmeterol (Seretide) or Vilanterol (Breo). Best Uses Fluticasone is a highly potent inhaled corticosteroid for moderate-severe asthma, but has greater systemic absorption than budesonide increasing adrenal suppression risk at high doses. Avoid / Cautions Acute asthma attacks (not a reliever). **Pregnancy** Category B3 (Budesonide is generally preferred in pregnancy, though fluticasone is continued if the patient is stable on it). Key Risks Oral/Pharyngeal: Oropharyngeal candidiasis, dysphonia. Respiratory: Paradoxical bronchospasm upon inhalation (often due to the propellant, use a spacer). Endocrine: Adrenal suppression at extremely high doses, though less likely than with Beclometasone due to 99% hepatic clearance of swallowed fractions. Key Interactions **CYP3A4** inhibitors (Ritonavir, Cobicistat) completely block hepatic breakdown. Swallowed fluticasone builds up, causing profound iatrogenic Cushing's syndrome and adrenal crisis. Monitoring Ensure 'rinse and spit' routine to prevent thrush. No specific routine laboratory tests required. Clinical Pearl Never prescribe Fluticasone to a patient on HIV/HCV protease inhibitors (Ritonavir). It is a classic exam and ward error resulting in rapid, severe adrenal suppression.","sourceRole":"clinical_reference","access":"public","validationStatus":"unverified","sourceStatus":"current","sourceLineage":[],"publicationVersion":"4e7617b02c6dff1f47b87bf8e3c21649c1bc0984dd8b7eb5b098d43bb6df42fb","contentHash":"d3be8896bd6827a11d0104fc40dc619441e166fa7088cdef489ee24ba9685b02"},"renderPayload":{"slug":"fluticasone","name":"Fluticasone","class":"Steroid","subclass":"Inhaled Glucocorticoid","category":"Respiratory - Asthma & COPD","accent":"#16a34a","tag":"STEROID","schedule":"S4","stats":[{"label":"Max Dose","value":"1000 mcg/day","cls":"hi","flag":"hi"},{"label":"Half-life","value":"8-14 h","cls":"","flag":""},{"label":"Lipophilic","value":"HIGH","cls":"warn","flag":""},{"label":"Systemic Abs.","value":"LOW","cls":"good","flag":""}],"sections":[{"title":"Rapid Summary","type":"summary","rows":[{"key":"Overview","val":"Highly lipophilic, extremely potent inhaled corticosteroid. Stronger receptor binding than budesonide, providing excellent local control with minimal systemic swallowing absorption.","tags":[]},{"key":"Maximum Dose","val":"Max **1000 mcg/day** (Adults).","tags":[]},{"key":"Clinical Focus","val":"Fluticasone Propionate (Flixotide) and Fluticasone Furoate (Arnuity) have different potencies and dosing regimens. Do not mix them up.","tags":[]},{"key":"Bottom Line","val":"Fluticasone is a highly potent inhaled corticosteroid for moderate-severe asthma, but has greater systemic absorption than budesonide increasing adrenal suppression risk at high doses.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Risk Profile","type":"risk","rows":[{"key":"Oral/Pharyngeal","val":"HIGH — Oropharyngeal candidiasis, dysphonia.","tags":[]},{"key":"Respiratory","val":"MODERATE — Paradoxical bronchospasm upon inhalation (often due to the propellant, use a spacer).","tags":[]},{"key":"Endocrine","val":"MODERATE — Adrenal suppression at extremely high doses, though less likely than with Beclometasone due to 99% hepatic clearance of swallowed fractions.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Dosing & Administration","type":"dose","rows":[{"key":"Asthma Maintenance (Propionate)","val":"**Inhaled** 100-500 mcg **BD**. Max 1000 mcg/day.","tags":[]},{"key":"Asthma Maintenance (Furoate)","val":"**Inhaled** 100-200 mcg **OD**.","tags":[]},{"key":"COPD (Combo only)","val":"**Inhaled** Typically given combined with Salmeterol (Seretide) or Vilanterol (Breo).","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Formulation & Access","type":"form","rows":[{"key":"Brand Names","val":"Flixotide, Seretide (combo), Breo Ellipta (combo)","tags":[]},{"key":"Oral Routes","val":"MDI (50, 125, 250 mcg/actuation). Dry powder Accuhaler/Ellipta.","tags":[]},{"key":"Prescribing & PBS","val":"Authority Required (PBS).","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Indications & Use","type":"ind","rows":[{"key":"Primary","val":"Asthma prophylaxis, severe COPD.","tags":["PBS","TGA"]},{"key":"Clinical Place","val":"Highly prescribed maintenance inhaler. The Furoate salt allows for ultra-convenient once-daily dosing.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Contraindications & Populations","type":"contra","rows":[{"key":"Absolute","val":"CRITICAL — Acute asthma attacks (not a reliever).","tags":[]},{"key":"Pregnancy","val":"CAUTION — **Pregnancy** Category B3 (budesonide is generally preferred in pregnancy, though fluticasone may be continued if already effective).","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["pregnancy"],"action":"caution","severity":"caution","note":"Fluticasone pregnancy row should prompt benefit-risk review rather than automatic contraindication."}}]},{"title":"Key Interactions","type":"inter","rows":[{"key":"Pharmacokinetic","val":"CRITICAL — **CYP3A4** inhibitors (Ritonavir, Cobicistat) completely block hepatic breakdown. Swallowed fluticasone builds up, causing profound iatrogenic Cushing's syndrome and adrenal crisis.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Monitoring & Cautions","type":"mon","rows":[{"key":"Bedside","val":"MANDATORY — Ensure 'rinse and spit' routine to prevent thrush.","tags":[]},{"key":"Laboratory","val":"No specific routine laboratory tests required.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Clinical Pearls","type":"pearl","rows":[{"key":"The HIV/Hep C Trap","val":"Never prescribe Fluticasone to a patient on HIV/HCV protease inhibitors (Ritonavir). It is a classic exam and ward error resulting in rapid, severe adrenal suppression.","tags":[]},{"key":"Pneumonia in COPD","val":"ICS use in COPD (especially Fluticasone) is statistically linked to a higher risk of developing pneumonia. Use ICS in COPD only if they have frequent exacerbations or an eosinophilic phenotype.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics","type":"evid","rows":[{"key":"Mechanism","val":"Potent glucocorticoid receptor agonist. Reduces airway inflammation. Modifies gene transcription.","tags":[]},{"key":"Onset","val":"Days. Full effect takes 1-2 weeks.","tags":[]},{"key":"Duration","val":"12-24 h.","tags":[]},{"key":"Half-life","val":"8-14 hours. First-pass metabolism is nearly 100%, meaning any drug swallowed is immediately destroyed by the liver, severely restricting systemic side effects.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Sources","type":"src","rows":[{"key":"Source Review","val":"Source-backed review 2026-05-11: FLIXOTIDE/fluticasone Australian PI source pack. Entry checked for dosing, access, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and app structure."}]}],"quick":[{"label":"Class & Role","value":"Inhaled Glucocorticoid — Highly lipophilic, extremely potent inhaled corticosteroid."},{"label":"Route / Formulation","value":"MDI (50, 125, 250 mcg/actuation). Dry powder Accuhaler/Ellipta. (Flixotide, Seretide (combo), Breo Ellipta (combo))"},{"label":"Usual Dose & Max","value":"**Inhaled** 100-500 mcg **BD**. Max 1000 mcg/day."},{"label":"Key Indication Doses","value":"Asthma Maintenance (Propionate): **Inhaled** 100-500 mcg **BD**. Max 1000 mcg/day.  Asthma Maintenance (Furoate): **Inhaled** 100-200 mcg **OD**.  COPD (Combo only): **Inhaled** Typically given combined with Salmeterol (Seretide) or Vilanterol (Breo)."},{"label":"Best Uses","value":"Fluticasone is a highly potent inhaled corticosteroid for moderate-severe asthma, but has greater systemic absorption than budesonide increasing adrenal suppression risk at high doses."},{"label":"Avoid / Cautions","value":"Acute asthma attacks (not a reliever). **Pregnancy** Category B3 (Budesonide is generally preferred in pregnancy, though fluticasone is continued if the patient is stable on it)."},{"label":"Key Risks","value":"Oral/Pharyngeal: Oropharyngeal candidiasis, dysphonia.  Respiratory: Paradoxical bronchospasm upon inhalation (often due to the propellant, use a spacer).  Endocrine: Adrenal suppression at extremely high doses, though less likely than with Beclometasone due to 99% hepatic clearance of swallowed fractions."},{"label":"Key Interactions","value":"**CYP3A4** inhibitors (Ritonavir, Cobicistat) completely block hepatic breakdown. Swallowed fluticasone builds up, causing profound iatrogenic Cushing's syndrome and adrenal crisis."},{"label":"Monitoring","value":"Ensure 'rinse and spit' routine to prevent thrush.  No specific routine laboratory tests required."},{"label":"Clinical Pearl","value":"Never prescribe Fluticasone to a patient on HIV/HCV protease inhibitors (Ritonavir). It is a classic exam and ward error resulting in rapid, severe adrenal suppression."}]}},{"logicalId":"medications:fluvoxamine","logicalDocumentId":"92553bb9-976d-5a54-8067-ecdcf70dac1f","logicalChunkId":"457a84e2-1b72-53e9-b691-6d7a60ebc428","normalizedText":"Medication: Fluvoxamine\nClass: Antidepressant\nSubclass: SSRI (Sigma-1)\nSchedule: S4\nTag: SSRI\nRapid Summary summary Overview Unique SSRI with potent Sigma-1 receptor agonism. The gold standard for severe Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD). However, it is an incredibly dangerous inhibitor of liver enzymes. Maximum Dose Max **300 mg/day** (given in divided doses). Clinical Focus Completely shuts down CYP1A2. Fatal interaction with Clozapine, Agomelatine, and Theophylline. Bottom Line Fluvoxamine is an SSRI primarily used for OCD with potent CYP1A2 inhibition, but has significant drug interactions (especially with clozapine and theophylline) and more GI side effects than other SSRIs. Risk Profile risk Gastrointestinal HIGH — Nausea and vomiting (Higher rate than most other SSRIs, usually transient). Neurological MODERATE — Sedation (Unlike Fluoxetine which is activating, Fluvoxamine is mildly sedating, hence NOCTE dosing). Metabolic MODERATE — Hyponatremia (SIADH). Dosing & Administration dose Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder Start **PO** 50 mg **NOCTE**. Titrate up to 100-300 mg/day. Doses > 150 mg should be divided **BD**. Depression Start **PO** 50 mg **NOCTE**. Target 100-200 mg/day. Formulation & Access form Brand Names Luvox, Faverin Oral Routes Tablets (50 mg, 100 mg). Prescribing & PBS Authority Required (PBS) for OCD/Depression. Indications & Use ind Primary Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder, Major Depressive Disorder. Clinical Place Niche use for severe OCD. Largely avoided for simple depression due to terrifying drug interaction profile. Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL - Concurrent use of MAOIs, tizanidine, agomelatine, or other contraindicated serotonergic/interacting medicines. Avoid concurrent clozapine unless under specialist monitoring. Pregnancy CAUTION — **Pregnancy** Category B2. Key Interactions inter Pharmacokinetic CRITICAL — Absolute blockade of **CYP1A2** and **CYP2C19**. If given to a patient on Clozapine, Clozapine levels will skyrocket by 500-1000%, causing fatal seizures and coma. If given to a patient on Duloxetine or Agomelatine, severe liver toxicity ensues. Monitoring & Cautions mon Bedside MANDATORY — Run a comprehensive drug-interaction check before prescribing this drug. Laboratory Routine **U&E** (sodium). Clinical Pearls pearl The OCD Specialist While Sertraline and Fluoxetine treat OCD, Fluvoxamine is universally regarded by psychiatrists as the most potent agent for crippling, treatment-resistant OCD due to the unique Sigma-1 action. The Clozapine Hack In highly specialized settings, psychiatrists actually *use* the deadly interaction on purpose. If a patient is rapid-metabolizer of Clozapine, giving them a tiny 25mg dose of Fluvoxamine safely boosts their Clozapine levels without needing to prescribe massive, toxic doses of Clozapine. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Inhibits serotonin reuptake. Unique potent agonism at the Sigma-1 receptor (which regulates glutamate/calcium signaling and neuroplasticity), thought to drive its superior efficacy in OCD. Onset 2-6 WEEKS. Half-life 15 hours. Requires BD dosing at high levels. Sources src Source Review Source Check - LUVOX Australian CMI and PBS search checked 2026-05-10 for antidepressant batch appraisal: identity/access, indication, dosing, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and source-row status.\nClass & Role SSRI (Sigma-1) — Unique SSRI with potent Sigma-1 receptor agonism. Route / Formulation Tablets (50 mg, 100 mg). (Luvox, Faverin) Usual Dose & Max Start **PO** 50 mg **NOCTE**. Titrate up to 100-300 mg/day. Doses > 150 mg should be divided **BD**. Max **300 mg/day** (given in divided doses). Key Indication Doses Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder: Start **PO** 50 mg **NOCTE**. Titrate up to 100-300 mg/day. Doses > 150 mg should be divided **BD**. Depression: Start **PO** 50 mg **NOCTE**. Target 100-200 mg/day. Best Uses Fluvoxamine is an SSRI primarily used for OCD with potent CYP1A2 inhibition, but has significant drug interactions (especially with clozapine and theophylline) and more GI side effects than other SSRIs. Avoid / Cautions Concurrent use of MAOIs, Tizanidine, Agomelatine, or Tizanidine. Avoid concurrent Clozapine without specialist monitoring. **Pregnancy** Category B2. Key Risks Gastrointestinal: Nausea and vomiting (Higher rate than most other SSRIs, usually transient). Neurological: Sedation (Unlike Fluoxetine which is activating, Fluvoxamine is mildly sedating, hence NOCTE dosing). Metabolic: Hyponatremia (SIADH). Key Interactions Absolute blockade of **CYP1A2** and **CYP2C19**. If given to a patient on Clozapine, Clozapine levels will skyrocket by 500-1000%, causing fatal seizures and coma. If given to a patient on Duloxetine or Agomelatine, severe liver toxicity ensues. Monitoring Run a comprehensive drug-interaction check before prescribing this drug. Routine **U&E** (sodium). Clinical Pearl While Sertraline and Fluoxetine treat OCD, Fluvoxamine is universally regarded by psychiatrists as the most potent agent for crippling, treatment-resistant OCD due to the unique Sigma-1 action.","contentHash":"dd00a9929fe964c9dafecf5c8a45d74ed05c1382226249dca39b65fc10aed192","publicationFingerprint":"3e305c3434809a7a73a5c459e00e46ffcf09f700f2ac31a5ffb2e548a826dbf5","governanceFingerprint":"6473895da16d5508d03ffea998bc94f9de73dd719d70d551126b969e94bd3eab","lineageFingerprint":"4f53cda18c2baa0c0354bb5f9a3ecbe5ed12ab4d8e11ba873c2f11161202b945","publicMetadataFingerprint":"fb97133d0b7a2b1e24f2a4a9dd05618aaa71beec6f65cddc4025974f454fc353","record":{"version":"site-content-record-v1","logicalId":"medications:fluvoxamine","producerClass":"dynamic_registry","domain":"medications","route":"/medications/fluvoxamine","title":"Fluvoxamine","body":"Medication: Fluvoxamine\nClass: Antidepressant\nSubclass: SSRI (Sigma-1)\nSchedule: S4\nTag: SSRI\nRapid Summary summary Overview Unique SSRI with potent Sigma-1 receptor agonism. The gold standard for severe Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD). However, it is an incredibly dangerous inhibitor of liver enzymes. Maximum Dose Max **300 mg/day** (given in divided doses). Clinical Focus Completely shuts down CYP1A2. Fatal interaction with Clozapine, Agomelatine, and Theophylline. Bottom Line Fluvoxamine is an SSRI primarily used for OCD with potent CYP1A2 inhibition, but has significant drug interactions (especially with clozapine and theophylline) and more GI side effects than other SSRIs. Risk Profile risk Gastrointestinal HIGH — Nausea and vomiting (Higher rate than most other SSRIs, usually transient). Neurological MODERATE — Sedation (Unlike Fluoxetine which is activating, Fluvoxamine is mildly sedating, hence NOCTE dosing). Metabolic MODERATE — Hyponatremia (SIADH). Dosing & Administration dose Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder Start **PO** 50 mg **NOCTE**. Titrate up to 100-300 mg/day. Doses > 150 mg should be divided **BD**. Depression Start **PO** 50 mg **NOCTE**. Target 100-200 mg/day. Formulation & Access form Brand Names Luvox, Faverin Oral Routes Tablets (50 mg, 100 mg). Prescribing & PBS Authority Required (PBS) for OCD/Depression. Indications & Use ind Primary Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder, Major Depressive Disorder. Clinical Place Niche use for severe OCD. Largely avoided for simple depression due to terrifying drug interaction profile. Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL - Concurrent use of MAOIs, tizanidine, agomelatine, or other contraindicated serotonergic/interacting medicines. Avoid concurrent clozapine unless under specialist monitoring. Pregnancy CAUTION — **Pregnancy** Category B2. Key Interactions inter Pharmacokinetic CRITICAL — Absolute blockade of **CYP1A2** and **CYP2C19**. If given to a patient on Clozapine, Clozapine levels will skyrocket by 500-1000%, causing fatal seizures and coma. If given to a patient on Duloxetine or Agomelatine, severe liver toxicity ensues. Monitoring & Cautions mon Bedside MANDATORY — Run a comprehensive drug-interaction check before prescribing this drug. Laboratory Routine **U&E** (sodium). Clinical Pearls pearl The OCD Specialist While Sertraline and Fluoxetine treat OCD, Fluvoxamine is universally regarded by psychiatrists as the most potent agent for crippling, treatment-resistant OCD due to the unique Sigma-1 action. The Clozapine Hack In highly specialized settings, psychiatrists actually *use* the deadly interaction on purpose. If a patient is rapid-metabolizer of Clozapine, giving them a tiny 25mg dose of Fluvoxamine safely boosts their Clozapine levels without needing to prescribe massive, toxic doses of Clozapine. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Inhibits serotonin reuptake. Unique potent agonism at the Sigma-1 receptor (which regulates glutamate/calcium signaling and neuroplasticity), thought to drive its superior efficacy in OCD. Onset 2-6 WEEKS. Half-life 15 hours. Requires BD dosing at high levels. Sources src Source Review Source Check - LUVOX Australian CMI and PBS search checked 2026-05-10 for antidepressant batch appraisal: identity/access, indication, dosing, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and source-row status.\nClass & Role SSRI (Sigma-1) — Unique SSRI with potent Sigma-1 receptor agonism. Route / Formulation Tablets (50 mg, 100 mg). (Luvox, Faverin) Usual Dose & Max Start **PO** 50 mg **NOCTE**. Titrate up to 100-300 mg/day. Doses > 150 mg should be divided **BD**. Max **300 mg/day** (given in divided doses). Key Indication Doses Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder: Start **PO** 50 mg **NOCTE**. Titrate up to 100-300 mg/day. Doses > 150 mg should be divided **BD**. Depression: Start **PO** 50 mg **NOCTE**. Target 100-200 mg/day. Best Uses Fluvoxamine is an SSRI primarily used for OCD with potent CYP1A2 inhibition, but has significant drug interactions (especially with clozapine and theophylline) and more GI side effects than other SSRIs. Avoid / Cautions Concurrent use of MAOIs, Tizanidine, Agomelatine, or Tizanidine. Avoid concurrent Clozapine without specialist monitoring. **Pregnancy** Category B2. Key Risks Gastrointestinal: Nausea and vomiting (Higher rate than most other SSRIs, usually transient). Neurological: Sedation (Unlike Fluoxetine which is activating, Fluvoxamine is mildly sedating, hence NOCTE dosing). Metabolic: Hyponatremia (SIADH). Key Interactions Absolute blockade of **CYP1A2** and **CYP2C19**. If given to a patient on Clozapine, Clozapine levels will skyrocket by 500-1000%, causing fatal seizures and coma. If given to a patient on Duloxetine or Agomelatine, severe liver toxicity ensues. Monitoring Run a comprehensive drug-interaction check before