dications & Use ind Primary Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus. Clinical Place Second-line agent after metformin. Rapidly lowers HbA1c but promotes weight gain and pancreatic burnout over time. Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — Type 1 Diabetes (requires functioning beta-cells to work), Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA). Renal / Hepatic HIGH — Avoid in severe renal (**eGFR** < 30) or hepatic failure due to drug accumulation leading to profound hypos. Pregnancy ABSOLUTE — **Pregnancy** Category C. Causes severe neonatal hypoglycemia. Key Interactions inter Pharmacodynamic HIGH — Beta-blockers mask the physiological warning signs of a hypo (tremor, tachycardia), leaving only sweating as a warning sign. The patient may collapse without realizing their BSL dropped. Monitoring & Cautions mon Bedside MANDATORY — Regular **BSL** checks on the ward. Withhold if the patient is NBM (Nil By Mouth). Laboratory Routine **HbA1c** and **U&E**. Clinical Pearls pearl The NBM Trap If a patient on Gliclazide is made 'Nil By Mouth' for surgery or a scan, you MUST withhold their morning dose. The drug forces insulin release regardless of food intake; they will crash. The Pancreatic Burnout Sulfonylureas essentially whip a tired horse (the failing pancreas). Over 5-10 years, they accelerate beta-cell destruction, eventually forcing the patient onto injectable insulin. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Binds to sulfonylurea receptors on pancreatic beta-cells, closing ATP-sensitive potassium channels. This depolarizes the cell, forcing calcium influx and massive insulin exocytosis. Onset **PO** 1-2 hours. Duration 24 h (MR). Half-life 16 hours (Metabolised hepatically, renally cleared). Sources src Source Review Source Check - GLICLAZIDE MR ARTG/PI and PBS search checked 2026-05-13 for high-risk opioid/general batch appraisal: identity/access, indication, dosing, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, pregnancy/lactation, patient-context metadata, and source-row status.\nClass & Role Sulfonylurea — Second-generation sulfonylurea. Route / Formulation Tablets IR (80 mg). Modified Release Tablets (30 mg, 60 mg). (Diamicron, Diamicron MR) Usual Dose & Max **PO** 30-120 mg **OD** with breakfast. Max **120 mg/day** (Modified Release) or **320 mg/day** (Immediate Release). Key Indication Doses Type 2 Diabetes (MR): **PO** 30-120 mg **OD** with breakfast. Type 2 Diabetes (IR): **PO** 40-160 mg **BD** with meals. Elderly / Renal Impairment: Start at 30 mg MR. Highly sensitive to prolonged hypoglycemia. Best Uses Gliclazide is a sulfonylurea with proven efficacy for type 2 diabetes and lower hypoglycaemia risk than glibenclamide, but causes weight gain and hypoglycaemia especially in elderly and renal impairment. Avoid / Cautions Type 1 Diabetes (requires functioning beta-cells to work), Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA). **Pregnancy** Category C. Causes severe neonatal hypoglycemia. Key Risks Endocrine: Severe, prolonged hypoglycemia (can be fatal in the elderly). HIGH — Weight gain (insulin is an anabolic, fat-storing hormone). Gastrointestinal: Mild nausea. Immunological: Sulfa-allergy cross-reactivity (rash). Key Interactions Beta-blockers mask the physiological warning signs of a hypo (tremor, tachycardia), leaving only sweating as a warning sign. The patient may collapse without realizing their BSL dropped. Monitoring Regular **BSL** checks on the ward. Withhold if the patient is NBM (Nil By Mouth). Routine **HbA1c** and **U&E**. Clinical Pearl If a patient on Gliclazide is made 'Nil By Mouth' for surgery or a scan, you MUST withhold their morning dose. The drug forces insulin release regardless of food intake; they will crash.","sourceRole":"clinical_reference","access":"public","validationStatus":"unverified","sourceStatus":"current","sourceLineage":[],"publicationVersion":"aedb19e53d38b66566901d6a067cfde3869760c7f5460a61f26ca75cdc57c461","contentHash":"9c88dd8792faa6e2b14e0ead85cd326e8b560747657482818a0fcb5a5dd49e1b"},"re