ty risk in older adults."}}]},{"title":"Key Interactions","type":"inter","rows":[{"key":"Pharmacodynamic","val":"CRITICAL — Additive anticholinergic and CNS-depressant effects with TCAs, antipsychotics, promethazine, oxybutynin, benzodiazepines, opioids, and alcohol.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Monitoring & Cautions","type":"mon","rows":[{"key":"Bedside","val":"MANDATORY — Ensure the patch is applied to a clean, hairless area behind the ear. Warn patients to wash their hands immediately after applying the patch, or they will accidentally dilate their own pupil if they rub their eye.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Clinical Pearls","type":"pearl","rows":[{"key":"The Name Trap","val":"Hyoscine HYDRObromide (Kwells/Scopoderm) crosses the BBB and puts you to sleep. Hyoscine BUTYLbromide (Buscopan) stays in the gut and fixes stomach cramps. Confusing them on the ward will result in a delirious or seizing patient.","tags":[]},{"key":"The Palliative Patch","val":"A Scopoderm patch behind the ear of a dying patient is a painless, non-invasive way to completely dry up the distressing rattling airway secretions without needing regular needles.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics","type":"evid","rows":[{"key":"Mechanism","val":"Competitive antagonist of central and peripheral muscarinic receptors. Blocks transmission from the vestibular nuclei to the CNS vomiting center. Dries up salivary and respiratory glands.","tags":[]},{"key":"Onset","val":"**Topical** 4-6 hours. **SC** 15-30 mins.","tags":[]},{"key":"Duration","val":"72 h (Patch).","tags":[]},{"key":"Half-life","val":"9.5 hours. Extensively metabolised by the liver.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Sources","type":"src","rows":[{"key":"Source Review","val":"Source-backed review 2026-05-12: KWELLS hyoscine hydrobromide Australian medicine-information source pack. Entry checked for dosing, access, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and app structure."}]}],"quick":[{"label":"Class & Role","value":"Anticholinergic — Centrally acting, highly lipid-soluble antimuscarinic agent."},{"label":"Route / Formulation","value":"Tablets (300 mcg). (Kwells, Scopoderm (Patch))"},{"label":"Usual Dose & Max","value":"**Topical** Apply 1 patch (1.5 mg) behind the ear 5-6 hours before travel. Leave on for 72 hours. OR **PO** 300 mcg STAT. Max **1.5 mg patch every 72 hours** (Topical) or **1.2 mg/day** (Oral/IV)."},{"label":"Key Indication Doses","value":"Motion Sickness: **Topical** Apply 1 patch (1.5 mg) behind the ear 5-6 hours before travel. Leave on for 72 hours. OR **PO** 300 mcg STAT.  Palliative Care (Secretions): **SC** / **IV** 400 mcg q4h PRN or via 24-hour syringe driver (1.2 - 2.4 mg/24h)."},{"label":"Best Uses","value":"Hyoscine hydrobromide is an anticholinergic used for motion sickness and palliative secretion management, but crosses the blood-brain barrier causing sedation and confusion unlike hyoscine butylbromide."},{"label":"Avoid / Cautions","value":"Narrow-angle glaucoma (will precipitate acute blindness), severe BPH with urinary retention."},{"label":"Key Risks","value":"Neurological: Toxic delirium, hallucinations, profound sedation, severe confusion (especially in the elderly).  Gastrointestinal: Severe dry mouth, constipation, paralytic ileus.  Ocular: Blurred vision, pupillary dilation (mydriasis)."},{"label":"Key Interactions","value":"TCAs, Promethazine, Olanzapine. Additive anticholinergic burden causes fatal hyperthermia and toxic megacolon."},{"label":"Monitoring","value":"Ensure the patch is applied to a clean, hairless area behind the ear. Warn patients to wash their hands immediately after applying the patch, or they will accidentally dilate their own pupil if they rub their eye."},{"label":"Clinical Pearl","value":"Hyoscine HYDRObromide (Kwells/Scopoderm) crosses the BBB and puts you to sleep. Hyoscine BUTYLbromide (Buscopan) stays in the gut and fixes stomach cramps. Confusing them on the ward will result in a delirious or seizing patient."}]}},{"logicalId":"medications:ibuprofen","logicalDocumentId":"3adc7cfe-1393-5dd1-8514-096097a14849","logicalChunkId":"235cbc1f-8283-553b-ba04-8e9b9aacf7a6","normalizedText":"Medication: Ibuprofen\nClass: Analgesia\nSubclass: NSAID\nSchedule: UNSCHEDULED\nTag: NSAID\nRapid Summary summary Overview Non-selective Non-Steroidal Anti-Inflammatory Drug (NSAID). Highly effective for inflammatory and musculoskeletal pain. Short half-life. Maximum Dose Max **2400 mg/day** (strictly in divided doses, e.g., 400 mg q6h or 800 mg TDS). OTC limits often say 1200 mg/day. Clinical Focus Avoid in patients with asthma, severe kidney disease, or active peptic ulcers. Bottom Line Ibuprofen is a widely available NSAID for pain, inflammation, and fever, but carries significant GI bleeding, cardiovascular, and renal risk especially in elderly and dehydrated patients. Risk Profile risk Gastrointestinal HIGH — Dyspepsia, gastric ulceration, severe GI haemorrhage/perforation (due to blockade of protective COX-1 prostaglandins in the stomach). Renal HIGH — Acute Kidney Injury (blocks vasodilatory prostaglandins in the afferent arteriole, choking off blood supply to the glomerulus). Fluid retention, hyperkalemia. Cardiovascular MODERATE — Can precipitate acute heart failure via fluid retention. Increases risk of MI/stroke at high chronic doses. Respiratory MODERATE — Can trigger severe bronchospasm in susceptible asthmatics (Aspirin-Exacerbated Respiratory Disease). Dosing & Administration dose Mild-to-Moderate Pain / Fever **PO** 200-400 mg q4-6h PRN. Max 2400 mg/day. Rheumatoid / Osteoarthritis **PO** 400-800 mg **TDS** to **QID** (Max 2400 mg/day). Renal Impairment MANDATORY — Avoid entirely if **eGFR** < 30. Use with extreme caution if eGFR 30-60. Formulation & Access form Brand Names Nurofen, Advil, Brufen Oral Routes Tablets/Capsules (200, 400 mg). Oral liquid (100 mg/5mL). Parenteral Ampoules for **IV** use (Rare, usually reserved for patent ductus arteriosus closure in neonates). Prescribing & PBS Over The Counter (OTC) for 200 mg. S4 for higher strengths/large packs. Indications & Use ind Primary Inflammatory pain, dysmenorrhoea, fever, rheumatoid arthritis, osteoarthritis. Clinical Place Second step on analgesic ladder. The most widely used NSAID due to a relatively fast onset and acceptable safety profile at low doses. Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — Active peptic ulcer disease, severe heart failure (NYHA IV), severe renal impairment, NSAID-sensitive asthma. Pregnancy ABSOLUTE — **Pregnancy** Category C. STRICTLY CONTRAINDICATED in the 3rd trimester (causes premature closure of the fetal ductus arteriosus and oligohydramnios). Key Interactions inter Triple Whammy CRITICAL — NSAID + ACEi/ARB + Diuretic. Will virtually guarantee acute renal failure. NEVER prescribe this combination. Pharmacodynamic CRITICAL — Anticoagulants (Warfarin, DOACs) and Antiplatelets (Clopidogrel). Massive increase in fatal GI bleeding. Pharmacokinetic HIGH — Blocks the cardioprotective effect of low-dose Aspirin by competing for the COX-1 binding site. Take aspirin 2 hours before ibuprofen. Monitoring & Cautions mon Bedside MANDATORY — Take strictly with food or milk to minimize gastric irritation. Co-prescribe a PPI (Pantoprazole) in the elderly or those with ulcer history. Laboratory Check **U&E** and **eGFR** if using for > 3-5 days in older adults. Clinical Pearls pearl The Ceiling Effect For pure analgesia, doses > 400mg per dose provide zero extra pain relief, they only increase GI and renal toxicity. Doses up to 800mg are strictly to maximise the anti-inflammatory effect for severe arthritis. The Dehydration Trap Never give an NSAID to a patient with severe vomiting, diarrhea, or hypovolemia. Their kidneys are relying 100% on prostaglandins to keep the afferent arteriole open. Ibuprofen will snap it shut, causing instant renal necrosis. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Reversibly and non-selectively inhibits Cyclooxygenase-1 (COX-1) and Cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2) enzymes. Blocks the conversion of arachidonic acid to prostaglandins, dampening peripheral inflammation and pain sensitization. Onset **PO** 30-60 mins. Duration 4-6 h. Half-life 2 hours. Hepatically metabolised, renally cleared. Sources src Source Review Source-backed review 2026-05-11: BRUFEN/ibuprofen Australian PI. Entry checked for dosing, access, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and app structure.\nClass & Role NSAID — Non-selective Non-Steroidal Anti-Inflammatory Drug (NSAID). Route / Formulation Tablets/Capsules (200, 400 mg). Oral liquid (100 mg/5mL). (Nurofen, Advil, Brufen) Usual Dose & Max **PO** 200-400 mg q4-6h PRN. Max 2400 mg/day. Key Indication Doses Mild-to-Moderate Pain / Fever: **PO** 200-400 mg q4-6h PRN. Max 2400 mg/day. Rheumatoid / Osteoarthritis: **PO** 400-800 mg **TDS** to **QID** (Max 2400 mg/day). Renal Impairment: Avoid entirely if **eGFR** < 30. Use with extreme caution if eGFR 30-60. Best Uses Ibuprofen is a widely available NSAID for pain, inflammation, and fever, but carries significant GI bleeding, cardiovascular, and renal risk especially in elderly and dehydrated patients. Avoid / Cautions Active peptic ulcer disease, severe heart failure (NYHA IV), severe renal impairment, NSAID-sensitive asthma. **Pregnancy** Category C. STRICTLY CONTRAINDICATED in the 3rd trimester (causes premature closure of the fetal ductus arteriosus and oligohydramnios). Key Risks Gastrointestinal: Dyspepsia, gastric ulceration, severe GI haemorrhage/perforation (due to blockade of protective COX-1 prostaglandins in the stomach). Renal: Acute Kidney Injury (blocks vasodilatory prostaglandins in the afferent arteriole, choking off blood supply to the glomerulus). Fluid retention, hyperkalemia. Cardiovascular: Can precipitate acute heart failure via fluid retention. Increases risk of MI/stroke at high chronic doses. Respiratory: Can trigger severe bronchospasm in susceptible asthmatics (Aspirin-Exacerbated Respiratory Disease). Key Interactions NSAID + ACEi/ARB + Diuretic. Will virtually guarantee acute renal failure. NEVER prescribe this combination. Anticoagulants (Warfarin, DOACs) and Antiplatelets (Clopidogrel). Massive increase in fatal GI bleeding. Blocks the cardioprotective effect of low-dose Aspirin by competing for the COX-1 binding site. Take aspirin 2 hours before ibuprofen. Monitoring Take strictly with food or milk to minimize gastric irritation. Co-prescribe a PPI (Pantoprazole) in the elderly or those with ulcer history. Check **U&E** and **eGFR** if using for > 3-5 days in older adults. Clinical Pearl For pure analgesia, doses > 400mg per dose provide zero extra pain relief, they only increase GI and renal toxicity. Doses up to 800mg are strictly to maximise the anti-inflammatory effect for severe arthritis.","contentHash":"de045c25cb162b740323b10297be51df7bc7839e7e6a555435e870a5a8d9f938","publicationFingerprint":"c5f691c23d017fbe812d357c38b4dc371bcf8642f32b75e82da28f0ca3db2d85","governanceFingerprint":"6473895da16d5508d03ffea998bc94f9de73dd719d70d551126b969e94bd3eab","lineageFingerprint":"4f53cda18c2baa0c0354bb5f9a3ecbe5ed12ab4d8e11ba873c2f11161202b945","publicMetadataFingerprint":"8789273efe7a25ad04431cd83e4beea025fed34d9955445bc5d8338119a5fadf","record":{"version":"site-content-record-v1","logicalId":"medications:ibuprofen","producerClass":"dynamic_registry","domain":"medications","route":"/medications/ibuprofen","title":"Ibuprofen","body":"Medication: Ibuprofen\nClass: Analgesia\nSubclass: NSAID\nSchedule: UNSCHEDULED\nTag: NSAID\nRapid Summary summary Overview Non-selective Non-Steroidal Anti-Inflammatory Drug (NSAID). Highly effective for inflammatory and musculoskeletal pain. Short half-life. Maximum Dose Max **2400 mg/day** (strictly in divided doses, e.g., 400 mg q6h or 800 mg TDS). OTC limits often say 1200 mg/day. Clinical Focus Avoid in patients with asthma, severe kidney disease, or active peptic ulcers. Bottom Line Ibuprofen is a widely available NSAID for pain, inflammation, and fever, but carries significant GI bleeding, cardiovascular, and renal risk especially in elderly and dehydrated patients. Risk Profile risk Gastrointestinal HIGH — Dyspepsia, gastric ulceration, severe GI haemorrhage/perforation (due to blockade of protective COX-1 prostaglandins in the stomach). Renal HIGH — Acute Kidney Injury (blocks vasodilatory prostaglandins in the afferent arteriole, choking off blood supply to the glomerulus). Fluid retention, hyperkalemia. Cardiovascular MODERATE — Can precipitate acute heart failure via fluid retention. Increases risk of MI/stroke at high chronic doses. Respiratory MODERATE — Can trigger severe bronchospasm in susceptible asthmatics (Aspirin-Exacerbated Respiratory Disease). Dosing & Administration dose Mild-to-Moderate Pain / Fever **PO** 200-400 mg q4-6h PRN. Max 2400 mg/day. Rheumatoid / Osteoarthritis **PO** 400-800 mg **TDS** to **QID** (Max 2400 mg/day). Renal Impairment MANDATORY — Avoid entirely if **eGFR** < 30. Use with extreme caution if eGFR 30-60. Formulation & Access form Brand Names Nurofen, Advil, Brufen Oral Routes Tablets/Capsules (200, 400 mg). Oral liquid (100 mg/5mL). Parenteral Ampoules for **IV** use (Rare, usually reserved for patent ductus arteriosus closure in neonates). Prescribing & PBS Over The Counter (OTC) for 200 mg. S4 for higher strengths/large packs. Indications & Use ind Primary Inflammatory pain, dysmenorrhoea, fever, rheumatoid arthritis, osteoarthritis. Clinical Place Second step on analgesic ladder. The most widely used NSAID due to a relatively fast onset and acceptable safety profile at low doses. Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — Active peptic ulcer disease, severe heart failure (NYHA IV), severe renal impairment, NSAID-sensitive asthma. Pregnancy ABSOLUTE — **Pregnancy** Category C. STRICTLY CONTRAINDICATED in the 3rd trimester (causes premature closure of the fetal ductus arteriosus and oligohydramnios). Key Interactions inter Triple Whammy CRITICAL — NSAID + ACEi/ARB + Diuretic. Will virtually guarantee acute renal failure. NEVER prescribe this combination. Pharmacodynamic CRITICAL — Anticoagulants (Warfarin, DOACs) and Antiplatelets (Clopidogrel). Massive increase in fatal GI bleeding. Pharmacokinetic HIGH — Blocks the cardioprotective effect of low-dose Aspirin by competing for the COX-1 binding site. Take aspirin 2 hours before ibuprofen. Monitoring & Cautions mon Bedside MANDATORY — Take strictly with food or milk to minimize gastric irritation. Co-prescribe a PPI (Pantoprazole) in the elderly or those with ulcer history. Laboratory Check **U&E** and **eGFR** if using for > 3-5 days in older adults. Clinical Pearls pearl The Ceiling Effect For pure analgesia, doses > 400mg per dose provide zero extra pain relief, they only increase GI and renal toxicity. Doses up to 800mg are strictly to maximise the anti-inflammatory effect for severe arthritis. The Dehydration Trap Never give an NSAID to a patient with severe vomiting, diarrhea, or hypovolemia. Their kidneys are relying 100% on prostaglandins to keep the afferent arteriole open. Ibuprofen will snap it shut, causing instant renal necrosis. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Reversibly and non-selectively inhibits Cyclooxygenase-1 (COX-1) and Cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2) enzymes. Blocks the conversion of arachidonic acid to prostaglandins, dampening peripheral inflammation and pain sensitization. Onset **PO** 30-60 mins. Duration 4-6 h. Half-life 2 hours. Hepatically metabolised, renally cleared. Sources src Source Review Source-backed review 2026-05-11: BRUFEN/ibuprofen Australian PI. Entry checked for dosing, access, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and app structure.\nClass & Role NSAID — Non-selective Non-Steroidal Anti-Inflammatory Drug (NSAID). Route / Formulation Tablets/Capsules (200, 400 mg). Oral liq