afety profile at low doses.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Contraindications & Populations","type":"contra","rows":[{"key":"Absolute","val":"CRITICAL — Active peptic ulcer disease, severe heart failure (NYHA IV), severe renal impairment, NSAID-sensitive asthma.","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["renal","allergy-nsaid"],"action":"contraindication","severity":"danger","match":{},"note":"Ibuprofen is contraindicated in severe renal impairment and NSAID-sensitive asthma."}},{"key":"Pregnancy","val":"ABSOLUTE — **Pregnancy** Category C. STRICTLY CONTRAINDICATED in the 3rd trimester (causes premature closure of the fetal ductus arteriosus and oligohydramnios).","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["pregnancy"],"action":"contraindication","severity":"danger","note":"Inferred from existing decision-section wording; review before clinical use."}}]},{"title":"Key Interactions","type":"inter","rows":[{"key":"Triple Whammy","val":"CRITICAL — NSAID + ACEi/ARB + Diuretic. Will virtually guarantee acute renal failure. NEVER prescribe this combination.","tags":[]},{"key":"Pharmacodynamic","val":"CRITICAL — Anticoagulants (Warfarin, DOACs) and Antiplatelets (Clopidogrel). Massive increase in fatal GI bleeding.","tags":[]},{"key":"Pharmacokinetic","val":"HIGH — Blocks the cardioprotective effect of low-dose Aspirin by competing for the COX-1 binding site. Take aspirin 2 hours before ibuprofen.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Monitoring & Cautions","type":"mon","rows":[{"key":"Bedside","val":"MANDATORY — Take strictly with food or milk to minimize gastric irritation. Co-prescribe a PPI (Pantoprazole) in the elderly or those with ulcer history.","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["elderly"],"action":"dose-adjust","severity":"caution","match":{"age":{}},"note":"Inferred from existing decision-section wording; review before clinical use."}},{"key":"Laboratory","val":"Check **U&E** and **eGFR** if using for > 3-5 days in older adults.","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["elderly"],"action":"dose-adjust","severity":"caution","match":{"age":{}},"note":"Inferred from existing decision-section wording; review before clinical use."}}]},{"title":"Clinical Pearls","type":"pearl","rows":[{"key":"The Ceiling Effect","val":"For pure analgesia, doses > 400mg per dose provide zero extra pain relief, they only increase GI and renal toxicity. Doses up to 800mg are strictly to maximise the anti-inflammatory effect for severe arthritis.","tags":[]},{"key":"The Dehydration Trap","val":"Never give an NSAID to a patient with severe vomiting, diarrhea, or hypovolemia. Their kidneys are relying 100% on prostaglandins to keep the afferent arteriole open. Ibuprofen will snap it shut, causing instant renal necrosis.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics","type":"evid","rows":[{"key":"Mechanism","val":"Reversibly and non-selectively inhibits Cyclooxygenase-1 (COX-1) and Cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2) enzymes. Blocks the conversion of arachidonic acid to prostaglandins, dampening peripheral inflammation and pain sensitization.","tags":[]},{"key":"Onset","val":"**PO** 30-60 mins.","tags":[]},{"key":"Duration","val":"4-6 h.","tags":[]},{"key":"Half-life","val":"2 hours. Hepatically metabolised, renally cleared.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Sources","type":"src","rows":[{"key":"Source Review","val":"Source-backed review 2026-05-11: BRUFEN/ibuprofen Australian PI. Entry checked for dosing, access, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and app structure."}]}],"quick":[{"label":"Class & Role","value":"NSAID — Non-selective Non-Steroidal Anti-Inflammatory Drug (NSAID)."},{"label":"Route / Formulation","value":"Tablets/Capsules (200, 400 mg). Oral liquid (100 mg/5mL). (Nurofen, Advil, Brufen)"},{"label":"Usual Dose & Max","value":"**PO** 200-400 mg q4-6h PRN. Max 2400 mg/day."},{"label":"Key Indication Doses","value":"Mild-to-Moderate Pain / Fever: **PO** 200-400 mg q4-6h PRN. Max 2400 mg/day.  Rheumatoid / Osteoarthritis: **PO** 400-800 mg **TDS** to **QID** (Max 2400 mg/day).  Renal Impairment: Avoid entirely if **eGFR** < 30. Use with extreme caution if eGFR 30-60."},{"label":"Best Uses","value":"Ibuprofen is a widely available NSAID for pain, inflammation, and fever, but carries significant GI bleeding, cardiovascular, and renal risk especially in elderly and dehydrated patients."},{"label":"Avoid / Cautions","value":"Active peptic ulcer disease, severe heart failure (NYHA IV), severe renal impairment, NSAID-sensitive asthma. **Pregnancy** Category C. STRICTLY CONTRAINDICATED in the 3rd trimester (causes premature closure of the fetal ductus arteriosus and oligohydramnios)."},{"label":"Key Risks","value":"Gastrointestinal: Dyspepsia, gastric ulceration, severe GI haemorrhage/perforation (due to blockade of protective COX-1 prostaglandins in the stomach).  Renal: Acute Kidney Injury (blocks vasodilatory prostaglandins in the afferent arteriole, choking off blood supply to the glomerulus). Fluid retention, hyperkalemia.  Cardiovascular: Can precipitate acute heart failure via fluid retention. Increases risk of MI/stroke at high chronic doses.  Respiratory: Can trigger severe bronchospasm in susceptible asthmatics (Aspirin-Exacerbated Respiratory Disease)."},{"label":"Key Interactions","value":"NSAID + ACEi/ARB + Diuretic. Will virtually guarantee acute renal failure. NEVER prescribe this combination.  Anticoagulants (Warfarin, DOACs) and Antiplatelets (Clopidogrel). Massive increase in fatal GI bleeding.  Blocks the cardioprotective effect of low-dose Aspirin by competing for the COX-1 binding site. Take aspirin 2 hours before ibuprofen."},{"label":"Monitoring","value":"Take strictly with food or milk to minimize gastric irritation. Co-prescribe a PPI (Pantoprazole) in the elderly or those with ulcer history.  Check **U&E** and **eGFR** if using for > 3-5 days in older adults."},{"label":"Clinical Pearl","value":"For pure analgesia, doses > 400mg per dose provide zero extra pain relief, they only increase GI and renal toxicity. Doses up to 800mg are strictly to maximise the anti-inflammatory effect for severe arthritis."}]}},{"logicalId":"medications:imipramine","logicalDocumentId":"573a244c-f23b-5f3c-baac-8ecbc668441f","logicalChunkId":"90477b3c-7ac5-59fb-9f8e-445556acddf9","normalizedText":"Medication: Imipramine\nClass: Antidepressant\nSubclass: TCA\nSchedule: S4\nTag: TCA\nRapid Summary summary Overview The very first Tricyclic Antidepressant (TCA). A highly potent serotonin and noradrenaline reuptake inhibitor. Extremely toxic in overdose. Uniquely used in pediatrics for bedwetting. Maximum Dose Max **200 mg/day** (Depression). Max **75 mg/day** (Childhood enuresis). Clinical Focus As with all TCAs, a 10-day supply taken at once is a fatal cardiac overdose. Do not dispense large quantities to suicidal patients. Bottom Line Imipramine is a classic TCA for depression and nocturnal enuresis in children, but is cardiotoxic in overdose and has significant anticholinergic and sedative effects. Risk Profile risk Cardiovascular CRITICAL — QRS widening, arrhythmias, heart block, severe orthostatic hypotension. Gastrointestinal HIGH — Severe dry mouth, constipation, urinary retention (desired in enuresis, dangerous in adults). Neurological HIGH — Sedation, delirium in the elderly, lowers seizure threshold. Dosing & Administration dose Depression Start **PO** 25-50 mg **NOCTE**. Titrate up to 100-200 mg/day. Nocturnal Enuresis (Children > 6 yrs) **PO** 10-75 mg **NOCTE** (Strictly weight/age based. Used only after bed alarms/desmopressin fail). Formulation & Access form Brand Names Tofranil Oral Routes Tablets (10, 25 mg). Prescribing & PBS Unrestricted General Benefit (S4). Indications & Use ind Primary Major Depressive Disorder, Nocturnal enuresis (bedwetting). Clinical Place Largely obsolete for depression due to SSRIs. Retains a niche in treatment-resistant childhood bedwetting due to its profound anticholinergic bladder-relaxing effect. Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — Recent myocardial infarction, heart block, concurrent MAOIs, narrow-angle glaucoma. Elderly CRITICAL — Do not use. Will cause severe falls and confusion. Key Interactions inter Pharmacodynamic CRITICAL — MAOIs, Tramadol (Serotonin Syndrome). Pharmacokinetic HIGH — **CYP2D6** inhibitors (Fluoxetine) spike Imipramine levels into the toxic range. Monitoring & Cautions mon Cardiac MANDATORY — Baseline **ECG** before escalating doses. Bedside MANDATORY — Keep medication locked away from children. A toddler ingesting a few adult tablets will suffer a fatal cardiac arrest. Clinical Pearls pearl The Bedwetting Mechanism Imipramine stops bedwetting through three pathways: it paralyzes the bladder (anticholinergic), it makes sleep lighter so the child wakes to a full bladder (noradrenaline), and it alters ADH secretion. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Inhibits reuptake of serotonin and noradrenaline. Its strong muscarinic (M1) blockade paralyzes the detrusor muscle of the bladder and tightens the internal sphincter, stopping bedwetting. Onset 2-4 WEEKS for depression. Days for enuresis. Half-life 10-20 hours. Metabolised to the active drug desipramine. Sources src Source Review Source Check - TOFRANIL Australian PI/CMI, ARTG 11064/60673, and PBS search checked 2026-05-10 for antidepressant batch appraisal: identity/access, indication, dosing, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and source-row status.\nClass & Role TCA — The very first Tricyclic Antidepressant (TCA). Route / Formulation Tablets (10, 25 mg). (Tofranil) Usual Dose & Max Start **PO** 25-50 mg **NOCTE**. Titrate up to 100-200 mg/day. Max **200 mg/day** (Depression). Key Indication Doses Depression: Start **PO** 25-50 mg **NOCTE**. Titrate up to 100-200 mg/day. Nocturnal Enuresis (Children > 6 yrs): **PO** 10-75 mg **NOCTE** (Strictly weight/age based. Used only after bed alarms/desmopressin fail). Best Uses Imipramine is a classic TCA for depression and nocturnal enuresis in children, but is cardiotoxic in overdose and has significant anticholinergic and sedative effects. Avoid / Cautions Recent myocardial infarction, heart block, concurrent MAOIs, narrow-angle glaucoma. Key Risks Cardiovascular: QRS widening, arrhythmias, heart block, severe orthostatic hypotension. Gastrointestinal: Severe dry mouth, constipation, urinary retention (desired in enuresis, dangerous in adults). Neurological: Sedation, delirium in the elderly, lowers seizure threshold. Key Interactions MAOIs, Tramadol (Serotonin Syndrome). **CYP2D6** inhibitors (Fluoxetine) spike Imipramine levels into the toxic range. Monitoring Baseline **ECG** before escalating doses. Keep medication locked away from children. A toddler ingesting a few adult tablets will suffer a fatal cardiac arrest. Clinical Pearl Imipramine stops bedwetting through three pathways: it paralyzes the bladder (anticholinergic), it makes sleep lighter so the child wakes to a full bladder (noradrenaline), and it alters ADH secretion.","contentHash":"7db4e447e695995acfbc7e026fef11cb02eeb2c25292897f86c2e473ebe15e26","publicationFingerprint":"a8ca963acb0118e545a428ea3e9dcffea6b4e703d85eabc8fc989c4ebcf76df1","governanceFingerprint":"6473895da16d5508d03ffea998bc94f9de73dd719d70d551126b969e94bd3eab","lineageFingerprint":"4f53cda18c2baa0c0354bb5f9a3ecbe5ed12ab4d8e11ba873c2f11161202b945","publicMetadataFingerprint":"5c310e00e2a2ff192a958420bb86ebb193323282af7d4b4c3e4e101c74fd6fc2","record":{"version":"site-content-record-v1","logicalId":"medications:imipramine","producerClass":"dynamic_registry","domain":"medications","route":"/medications/imipramine","title":"Imipramine","body":"Medication: Imipramine\nClass: Antidepressant\nSubclass: TCA\nSchedule: S4\nTag: TCA\nRapid Summary summary Overview The very first Tricyclic Antidepressant (TCA). A highly potent serotonin and noradrenaline reuptake inhibitor. Extremely toxic in overdose. Uniquely used in pediatrics for bedwetting. Maximum Dose Max **200 mg/day** (Depression). Max **75 mg/day** (Childhood enuresis). Clinical Focus As with all TCAs, a 10-day supply taken at once is a fatal cardiac overdose. Do not dispense large quantities to suicidal patients. Bottom Line Imipramine is a classic TCA for depression and nocturnal enuresis in children, but is cardiotoxic in overdose and has significant anticholinergic and sedative effects. Risk Profile risk Cardiovascular CRITICAL — QRS widening, arrhythmias, heart block, severe orthostatic hypotension. Gastrointestinal HIGH — Severe dry mouth, constipation, urinary retention (desired in enuresis, dangerous in adults). Neurological HIGH — Sedation, delirium in the elderly, lowers seizure threshold. Dosing & Administration dose Depression Start **PO** 25-50 mg **NOCTE**. Titrate up to 100-200 mg/day. Nocturnal Enuresis (Children > 6 yrs) **PO** 10-75 mg **NOCTE** (Strictly weight/age based. Used only after bed alarms/desmopressin fail). Formulation & Access form Brand Names Tofranil Oral Routes Tablets (10, 25 mg). Prescribing & PBS Unrestricted General Benefit (S4). Indications & Use ind Primary Major Depressive Disorder, Nocturnal enuresis (bedwetting). Clinical Place Largely obsolete for depression due to SSRIs. Retains a niche in treatment-resistant childhood bedwetting due to its profound anticholinergic bladder-relaxing effect. Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — Recent myocardial infarction, heart block, concurrent MAOIs, narrow-angle glaucoma. Elderly CRITICAL — Do not use. Will cause severe falls and confusion. Key Interactions inter Pharmacodynamic CRITICAL — MAOIs, Tramadol (Serotonin Syndrome). Pharmacokinetic HIGH — **CYP2D6** inhibitors (Fluoxetine) spike Imipramine levels into the toxic range. Monitoring & Cautions mon Cardiac MANDATORY — Baseline **ECG** before escalating doses. Bedside MANDATORY — Keep medication locked away from children. A toddler ingesting a few adult tablets will suffer a fatal cardiac arrest. Clinical Pearls pearl The Bedwetting Mechanism Imipramine stops bedwetting through three pathways: it paralyzes the bladder (anticholinergic), it makes sleep lighter so the child wakes to a full bladder (noradrenaline), and it alters ADH secretion. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Inhibits reuptake of serotonin and noradrenaline. Its strong muscarinic (M1) blockade paralyzes the detrusor muscle of the bladder and tightens the internal sphincter, stopping bedwetting. Onset 2-4 WEEKS for depression. Days for enuresis. Half-life 10-20 hours. Metabolised to the active drug desipramine. Sources src Source Review Source Check - TOFRANIL Australian PI/CMI, ARTG 11064/60673, and PBS search checked 2026-05-10 for antidepressant batch appraisal: identity/access, indication, dosing, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and source-row status.\nClass & Role TCA — The very first Tricyclic Antidepressant (TCA). Route / Formulation Tablets (10, 25 mg). (Tofranil) Usual Dose & Max Start **PO** 25-50 mg **NOCTE**. Titrate up to 100-200 mg/day. Max **200 mg/day** (Depression). Key Indication Doses Depression: Start **PO** 25-50 mg **NOCTE**. Titrate up to 100-200 mg/day. Nocturnal Enuresis (Children > 6 yrs): **PO** 10-75 mg **NOCTE** (Strictly weight/age based. Used only after bed alarms/desmopressin fail). Best Uses Imipramine is a classic TCA for depression and nocturnal enuresis in children, but is cardiotoxic in overdose and has significant anticholinergic and sedative effects. Avoid / Cautions Recent myocardial infarction, heart block, concurrent MAOIs, narrow-angle glaucoma. Key Risks Cardiovascular: QRS widening, arrhythmias, heart block, severe orthostatic hypotension. Gastrointestinal: Severe dry mouth, constipation, urinary retention (desired in enuresis, dangerous in adults). Neurological: Sedation, delirium in the elderly, lowers seizure threshold. Key Interactions MAOIs, Tramadol (Serotonin Syndrome). **CYP2D6** inhibitors (Fluoxetine) spike Imipramine levels into the toxic range. Monitoring Baseline **ECG** before escalating doses. Keep medication locked away from children. A toddler ingesting a few adult tablets will suffer a fatal cardiac arrest. Clinical Pearl Imipramine stops bedwetting through three pathways: it paralyzes the bladder (anticholinergic), it makes sleep lighter so the child wakes to a full bladder (noradrenaline), and it alters ADH secretion.","sourceRole":"clinical_reference","access":"public","validationStatus":"unverified","sourceStatus":"current","sourceLineage":[],"publicationVersion":"a8ca963acb0118e545a428ea3e9dcffea6b4e703d85eabc8fc989c4ebcf76df1","contentHash":"7db4e447e695995acfbc7e026fef11cb02eeb2c25292897f86c2e473ebe15e26"},"renderPayload":{"slug":"imipramine","name":"Imipramine","class":"Antidepressant","subclass":"TCA","category":"Psychiatry - Antidepressants","accent":"#7c3aed","tag":"TCA","schedule":"S4","stats":[{"label":"Max Dose","value":"200 mg/day","cls":"hi","flag":"hi"},{"label":"Half-life","value":"10-20 h","cls":"","flag":""},{"label":"Enuresis","value":"PAEDS USE","cls":"good","flag":""},{"label":"Toxicity in OD","value":"CRITICAL","cls":"hi","flag":"warn"}],"sections":[{"title":"Rapid Summary","type":"summary","rows":[{"key":"Overview","val":"The very first Tricyclic Antidepressant (TCA). A highly potent serotonin and noradrenaline reuptake inhibitor. Extremely toxic in overdose. Uniquely used in pediatrics for bedwetting.","tags":[]},{"key":"Maximum Dose","val":"Max **200 mg/day** (Depression). Max **75 mg/day** (Childhood enuresis).","tags":[]},{"key":"Clinical Focus","val":"As with all TCAs, a 10-day supply taken at once is a fatal cardiac overdose. Do not dispense large quantities to suicidal patients.","tags":[]},{"key":"Bottom Line","val":"Imipramine is a classic TCA for depression and nocturnal enuresis in children, but is cardiotoxic in overdose and has significant anticholinergic and sedative effects.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Risk Profile","type":"risk","rows":[{"key":"Cardiovascular","val":"CRITICAL — QRS widening, arrhythmias, heart block, severe orthostatic hypotension.","tags":[]},{"key":"Gastrointestinal","val":"HIGH — Severe dry mouth, constipation, urinary retention (desired in enuresis, dangerous in adults).","tags":[]},{"key":"Neurological","val":"HIGH — Sedation, delirium in the elderly, lowers seizure threshold.","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["elderly"],"action":"dose-adjust","severity":"caution","match":{"age":{}},"note":"Inferred from existing decision-section wording; review before clinical use."}}]},{"title":"Dosing & Administration","type":"dose","rows":[{"key":"Depression","val":"Start **PO** 25-50 mg **NOCTE**. Titrate up to 100-200 mg/day.","tags":[]},{"key":"Nocturnal Enuresis (Children > 6 yrs)","val":"**PO** 10-75 mg **NOCTE** (Strictly weight/age based. Used only after bed alarms/desmopressin fail).","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Formulation & Access","type":"form","rows":[{"key":"Brand Names","val":"Tofranil","tags":[]},{"key":"Oral Routes","val":"Tablets (10, 25 mg).","tags":[]},{"key":"Prescribing & PBS","val":"Unrestricted General Benefit (S4).","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Indications & Use","type":"ind","rows":[{"key":"Primary","val":"Major Depressive Disorder, Nocturnal enuresis (bedwetting).","tags":["PBS","TGA"]},{"key":"Clinical Place","val":"Largely obsolete for depression due to SSRIs. Retains a niche in treatment-resistant childhood bedwetting due to its profound anticholinergic bladder-relaxing effect.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Contraindications & Populations","type":"contra","rows":[{"key":"Absolute","val":"CRITICAL — Recent myocardial infarction, heart block, concurrent MAOIs, narrow-angle glaucoma.","tags":[]},{"key":"Elderly","val":"CRITICAL — Do not use. Will cause severe falls and confusion.","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["elderly"],"action":"contraindication","severity":"danger","match":{"age":{}},"note":"Inferred from existing decision-section wording; review before clinical use."}}]},{"title":"Key Interactions","type":"inter","rows":[{"key":"Pharmacodynamic","val":"CRITICAL — MAOIs, Tramadol (Serotonin Syndrome).","tags":[]},{"key":"Pharmacokinetic","val":"HIGH — **CYP2D6** inhibitors (Fluoxetine) spike Imipramine levels into the toxic range.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Monitoring & Cautions","type":"mon","rows":[{"key":"Cardiac","val":"MANDATORY — Baseline **ECG** before escalating doses.","tags":[]},{"key":"Bedside","val":"MANDATORY — Keep medication locked away from children. A toddler ingesting a few adult tablets will suffer a fatal cardiac arrest.","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["paediatric"],"action":"dose-adjust","severity":"caution","match":{"age":{"lt":18}},"note":"Inferred from existing decision-section wording; review before clinical use."}}]},{"title":"Clinical Pearls","type":"pearl","rows":[{"key":"The Bedwetting Mechanism","val":"Imipramine stops bedwetting through three pathways: it paralyzes the bladder (anticholinergic), it makes sleep lighter so the child wakes to a full bladder (noradrenaline), and it alters ADH secretion.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics","type":"evid","rows":[{"key":"Mechanism","val":"Inhibits reuptake of serotonin and noradrenaline. Its strong muscarinic (M1) blockade paralyzes the detrusor muscle of the bladder and tightens the internal sphincter, stopping bedwetting.","tags":[]},{"key":"Onset","val":"2-4 WEEKS for depression. Days for enuresis.","tags":[]},{"key":"Half-life","val":"10-20 hours. Metabolised to the active drug desipramine.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Sources","type":"src","rows":[{"key":"Source Review","val":"Source Check - TOFRANIL Australian PI/CMI, ARTG 11064/60673, and PBS search checked 2026-05-10 for antidepressant batch appraisal: identity/access, indication, dosing, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and source-row status."}]}],"quick":[{"label":"Class & Role","value":"TCA — The very first Tricyclic Antidepressant (TCA)."},{"label":"Route / Formulation","value":"Tablets (10, 25 mg). (Tofranil)"},{"label":"Usual Dose & Max","value":"Start **PO** 25-50 mg **NOCTE**. Titrate up to 100-200 mg/day. Max **200 mg/day** (Depression)."},{"label":"Key Indication Doses","value":"Depression: Start **PO** 25-50 mg **NOCTE**. Titrate up to 100-200 mg/day.  Nocturnal Enuresis (Children > 6 yrs): **PO** 10-75 mg **NOCTE** (Strictly weight/age based. Used only after bed alarms/desmopressin fail)."},{"label":"Best Uses","value":"Imipramine is a classic TCA for depression and nocturnal enuresis in children, but is cardiotoxic in overdose and has significant anticholinergic and sedative effects."},{"label":"Avoid / Cautions","value":"Recent myocardial infarction, heart block, concurrent MAOIs, narrow-angle glaucoma."},{"label":"Key Risks","value":"Cardiovascular: QRS widening, arrhythmias, heart block, severe orthostatic hypotension.  Gastrointestinal: Severe dry mouth, constipation, urinary retention (desired in enuresis, dangerous in adults).  Neurological: Sedation, delirium in the elderly, lowers seizure threshold."},{"label":"Key Interactions","value":"MAOIs, Tramadol (Serotonin Syndrome).  **CYP2D6** inhibitors (Fluoxetine) spike Imipramine levels into the toxic range."},{"label":"Monitoring","value":"Baseline **ECG** before escalating doses.  Keep medication locked away from children. A toddler ingesting a few adult tablets will suffer a fatal cardiac arrest."},{"label":"Clinical Pearl","value":"Imipramine stops bedwetting through three pathways: it paralyzes the bladder (anticholinergic), it makes sleep lighter so the child wakes to a full bladder (noradrenaline), and it alters ADH secretion."}]}},{"logicalId":"medications:indapamide","logicalDocumentId":"a3c6be02-b065-5ab8-92ea-04637aae7676","logicalChunkId":"62bec7d7-c544-5f03-8c5a-70f2087dbdc6","normalizedText":"Medication: Indapamide\nClass: Antihypertensive\nSubclass: Thiazide-like Diuretic\nSchedule: S4\nTag: DIURETIC\nRapid Summary summary Overview Thiazide-like diuretic. Superior to hydrochlorothiazide for blood pressure lowering with slightly less metabolic disruption (glucose/lipids). Maximum Dose Max **2.5 mg/day** (IR) or **1.5 mg/day** (SR). Clinical Focus Preferred over HCTZ in patients with metabolic syndrome or diabetes due to neutrality on glucose tolerance. Bottom Line Indapamide is a thiazide-like diuretic with superior stroke prevention evidence (ADVANCE, HYVET), but carries the same electrolyte disturbance risks as conventional thiazides. Risk Profile risk Electrolytes CRITICAL — Severe hyponatremia and hypokalemia. Metabolic LOW — Mildly increases uric acid (gout risk), but largely neutral regarding blood glucose and lipids (unlike HCTZ). Dermatological MODERATE — Sulfa-allergy cross-reactivity, rare rash. Dosing & Administration dose Hypertension **PO** 1.25-2.5 mg (IR) **OD** morning. OR **PO** 1.5 mg (SR) **OD** morning. Elderly / Frail Prefer 1.5 mg SR **OD** to minimise rapid electrolyte shifts. Formulation & Access form Brand Names Natrilix, Natrilix SR, Coversyl Plus (combo) Oral Routes Tablets IR (2.5 mg), Tablets SR (1.5 mg). Prescribing & PBS Unrestricted General Benefit (S4). Indications & Use ind Primary Hypertension. Clinical Place First-line diuretic option for hypertension. Supported by strong evidence in the elderly (HYVET trial). Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — Severe renal failure/anuria, hepatic coma/encephalopathy or severe hepatic impairment, severe sulfa allergy, hypokalaemia. Renal Impairment HIGH — Loses diuretic efficacy at **eGFR** < 30. Pregnancy CAUTION — **Pregnancy** Category C. Key Interactions inter Pharmacodynamic HIGH — Lithium (decreases clearance, high toxicity risk). Triple Whammy CRITICAL — NSAIDs + ACEi + Indapamide. Monitoring & Cautions mon Laboratory MANDATORY — Check **U&E** within 1-2 weeks of initiation. Elderly women are at massive risk of silent hyponatremia. Bedside MANDATORY — Monitor **BP**. Clinical Pearls pearl Metabolic Advantage If a hypertensive patient has pre-diabetes or hyperlipidemia, switch them from HCTZ to Indapamide to protect their metabolic profile. The Elderly Trap Hyponatremia on indapamide can present as confusion, lethargy, or frequent falls in the elderly, mimicking dementia. Always check a U&E first. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Inhibits Na+/Cl- cotransporter in distal tubule. Also has direct smooth muscle relaxant properties, acting as a direct vasodilator. Onset **PO** 1-2 hours. Duration 24 h (SR). Half-life 14-24 hours (Highly lipophilic, concentrates in vascular smooth muscle). Sources src Source Review Source-backed review 2026-05-11: NATRILIX/NATRILIX SR indapamide Australian PI. Entry checked for dosing, access, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and app structure.\nClass & Role Thiazide-like Diuretic — Thiazide-like diuretic. Route / Formulation Tablets IR (2.5 mg), Tablets SR (1.5 mg). (Natrilix, Natrilix SR, Coversyl Plus (combo)) Usual Dose & Max **PO** 1.25-2.5 mg (IR) **OD** morning. OR **PO** 1.5 mg (SR) **OD** morning. Max **2.5 mg/day** (IR) or **1.5 mg/day** (SR). Key Indication Doses Hypertension: **PO** 1.25-2.5 mg (IR) **OD** morning. OR **PO** 1.5 mg (SR) **OD** morning. Elderly / Frail: Prefer 1.5 mg SR **OD** to minimise rapid electrolyte shifts. Best Uses Indapamide is a thiazide-like diuretic with superior stroke prevention evidence (ADVANCE, HYVET), but carries the same electrolyte disturbance risks as conventional thiazides. Avoid / Cautions Severe hepatic failure (can precipitate hepatic encephalopathy), anuria, severe sulfa allergy. **Pregnancy** Category C. Key Risks Electrolytes: Severe hyponatremia and hypokalemia. Metabolic: Mildly increases uric acid (gout risk), but largely neutral regarding blood glucose and lipids (unlike HCTZ). Dermatological: Sulfa-allergy cross-reactivity, rare rash. Key Interactions Lithium (decreases clearance, high toxicity risk). NSAIDs + ACEi + Indapamide. Monitoring Check **U&E** within 1-2 weeks of initiation. Elderly women are at massive risk of silent hyponatremia. Monitor **BP**. Clinical Pearl If a hypertensive patient has pre-diabetes or hyperlipidemia, switch them from HCTZ to Indapamide to protect their metabolic profile.","contentHash":"37f9f52015641f70518c3110c755f30a62ee786c1863a4bf7aa22d8d2e1fa07f","publicationFingerprint":"fd1ff212dc46ca3417fdf5edab2f57a1251e452a03363b7f4fccd548dde1e22b","governanceFingerprint":"6473895da16d5508d03ffea998bc94f9de73dd719d70d551126b969e94bd3eab","lineageFingerprint":"4f53cda18c2baa0c0354bb5f9a3ecbe5ed12ab4d8e11ba873c2f11161202b945","publicMetadataFingerprint":"354d83821f20ef57dbda2796766f76e179ee666d30bae7a9b04c76762d7c3af5","record":{"version":"site-content-record-v1","logicalId":"medications:indapamide","producerClass":"dynamic_registry","domain":"medications","route":"/medications/indapamide","title":"Indapamide","body":"Medication: Indapamide\nClass: Antihypertensive\nSubclass: Thiazide-like Diuretic\nSchedule: S4\nTag: DIURETIC\nRapid Summary summary Overview Thiazide-like diuretic. Superior to hydrochlorothiazide for blood pressure lowering with slightly less metabolic disruption (glucose/lipids). Maximum Dose Max **2.5 mg/day** (IR) or **1.5 mg/day** (SR). Clinical Focus Preferred over HCTZ in patients with metabolic syndrome or diabetes due to neutrality on glucose tolerance. Bottom Line Indapamide is a thiazide-like diuretic with superior stroke prevention evidence (ADVANCE, HYVET), but carries the same electrolyte disturbance risks as conventional thiazides. Risk Profile risk Electrolytes CRITICAL — Severe hyponatremia and hypokalemia. Metabolic LOW — Mildly increases uric acid (gout risk), but largely neutral regarding blood glucose and lipids (unlike HCTZ). Dermatological MODERATE — Sulfa-allergy cross-reactivity, rare rash. Dosing & Administration dose Hypertension **PO** 1.25-2.5 mg (IR) **OD** morning. OR **PO** 1.5 mg (SR) **OD** morning. Elderly / Frail Prefer 1.5 mg SR **OD** to minimise rapid electrolyte shifts. Formulation & Access form Brand Names Natrilix, Natrilix SR, Coversyl Plus (combo) Oral Routes Tablets IR (2.5 mg), Tablets SR (1.5 mg). Prescribing & PBS Unrestricted General Benefit (S4). Indications & Use ind Primary Hypertension. Clinical Place First-line diuretic option for hypertension. Supported by strong evidence in the elderly (HYVET trial). Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — Severe renal failure/anuria, hepatic coma/encephalopathy or severe hepatic impairment, severe sulfa allergy, hypokalaemia. Renal Impairment HIGH — Loses diuretic efficacy at **eGFR** < 30. Pregnancy CAUTION — **Pregnancy** Category C. Key Interactions inter Pharmacodynamic HIGH — Lithium (decreases clearance, high toxicity risk). Triple Whammy CRITICAL — NSAIDs + ACEi + Indapamide. Monitoring & Cautions mon Laboratory MANDATORY — Check **U&E** within 1-2 weeks of initiation. Elderly women are at massive risk of silent hyponatremia. Bedside MANDATORY — Monitor **BP**. Clinical Pearls pearl Metabolic Advantage If a hypertensive patient has pre-diabetes or hyperlipidemia, switch them from HCTZ to Indapamide to protect their metabolic profile. The Elderly Trap Hyponatremia on indapamide can present as confusion, lethargy, or frequent falls in the elderly, mimicking dementia. Always check a U&E first. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Inhibits Na+/Cl- cotransporter in distal tubule. Also has direct smooth muscle relaxant properties, acting as a direct vasodilator. Onset **PO** 1-2 hours. Duration 24 h (SR). Half-life 14-24 hours (Highly lipophilic, concentrates in vascular smooth muscle). Sources src Source Review Source-backed review 2026-05-11: NATRILIX/NATRILIX SR indapamide Australian PI. Entry checked for dosing, access, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and app structure.\nClass & Role Thiazide-like Diuretic — Thiazide-like diuretic. Route / Formulation Tablets IR (2.5 mg), Tablets SR (1.5 mg). (Natrilix, Natrilix SR, Coversyl Plus (combo)) Usual Dose & Max **PO** 1.25-2.5 mg (IR) **OD** morning. OR **PO** 1.5 mg (SR) **OD** morning. Max **2.5 mg/day** (IR) or **1.5 mg/day** (SR). Key Indication Doses Hypertension: **PO** 1.25-2.5 mg (IR) **OD** morning. OR **PO** 1.5 mg (SR) **OD** morning. Elderly / Frail: Prefer 1.5 mg SR **OD** to minimise rapid electrolyte shifts. Best Uses Indapamide is a thiazide-like diuretic with superior stroke prevention evidence (ADVANCE, HYVET), but carries the same electrolyte disturbance risks as conventional thiazides. Avoid / Cautions Severe hepatic failure (can precipitate hepatic encephalopathy), anuria, severe sulfa allergy. **Pregnancy** Category C. Key Risks Electrolytes: Severe hyponatremia and hypokalemia. Metabolic: Mildly increases uric acid (gout risk), but largely neutral regarding blood glucose and lipids (unlike HCTZ). Dermatological: Sulfa-allergy cross-reactivity, rare rash. Key Interactions Lithium (decreases clearance, high toxicity risk). NSAIDs + ACEi + Indapamide. Monitoring Check **U&E** within 1-2 weeks of initiation. Elderly women are at massive risk of silent hyponatremia. Monitor **BP**. Clinical Pearl If a hypertensive patient has pre-diabetes or hyperlipidemia, switch them from HCTZ to Indapamide to protect their metabolic profile.","sourceRole":"clinical_reference","access":"public","validationStatus":"unverified","sourceStatus":"current","sourceLineage":[],"publicationVersion":"fd1ff212dc46ca3417fdf5edab2f57a1251e452a03363b7f4fccd548dde1e22b","contentHash":"37f9f52015641f70518c3110c755f30a62ee786c1863a4bf7aa22d8d2e1fa07f"},"renderPayload":{"slug":"indapamide","name":"Indapamide","class":"Antihypertensive","subclass":"Thiazide-like Diuretic","category":"Cardiovascular - Diuretics","accent":"#0284c7","tag":"DIURETIC","schedule":"S4","stats":[{"label":"Max Dose","value":"2.5 mg/day","cls":"hi","flag":"hi"},{"label":"Half-life","value":"14–24 h","cls":"","flag":""},{"label":"Hyponatremia","value":"RISK","cls":"hi","flag":""},{"label":"BP Efficacy","value":"HIGH","cls":"good","flag":""}],"sections":[{"title":"Rapid Summary","type":"summary","rows":[{"key":"Overview","val":"Thiazide-like diuretic. Superior to hydrochlorothiazide for blood pressure lowering with slightly less metabolic disruption (glucose/lipids).","tags":[]},{"key":"Maximum Dose","val":"Max **2.5 mg/day** (IR) or **1.5 mg/day** (SR).","tags":[]},{"key":"Clinical Focus","val":"Preferred over HCTZ in patients with metabolic syndrome or diabetes due to neutrality on glucose tolerance.","tags":[]},{"key":"Bottom Line","val":"Indapamide is a thiazide-like diuretic with superior stroke prevention evidence (ADVANCE, HYVET), but carries the same electrolyte disturbance risks as conventional thiazides.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Risk Profile","type":"risk","rows":[{"key":"Electrolytes","val":"CRITICAL — Severe hyponatremia and hypokalemia.","tags":[]},{"key":"Metabolic","val":"LOW — Mildly increases uric acid (gout risk), but largely neutral regarding blood glucose and lipids (unlike HCTZ).","tags":[]},{"key":"Dermatological","val":"MODERATE — Sulfa-allergy cross-reactivity, rare rash.","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["allergy-sulfa"],"action":"caution","severity":"caution","note":"Indapamide is a sulfonamide derivative; sulfonamide allergy should prompt caution/review."}}]},{"title":"Dosing & Administration","type":"dose","rows":[{"key":"Hypertension","val":"**PO** 1.25-2.5 mg (IR) **OD** morning. OR **PO** 1.5 mg (SR) **OD** morning.","tags":[]},{"key":"Elderly / Frail","val":"Prefer 1.5 mg SR **OD** to minimise rapid electrolyte shifts.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Formulation & Access","type":"form","rows":[{"key":"Brand Names","val":"Natrilix, Natrilix SR, Coversyl Plus (combo)","tags":[]},{"key":"Oral Routes","val":"Tablets IR (2.5 mg), Tablets SR (1.5 mg).","tags":[]},{"key":"Prescribing & PBS","val":"Unrestricted General Benefit (S4).","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Indications & Use","type":"ind","rows":[{"key":"Primary","val":"Hypertension.","tags":["PBS","TGA"]},{"key":"Clinical Place","val":"First-line diuretic option for hypertension. Supported by strong evidence in the elderly (HYVET trial).","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Contraindications & Populations","type":"contra","rows":[{"key":"Absolute","val":"CRITICAL — Severe renal failure/anuria, hepatic coma/encephalopathy or severe hepatic impairment, severe sulfa allergy, hypokalaemia.","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["renal","hepatic","allergy-sulfa"],"action":"contraindication","severity":"danger","match":{"hepatic":["severe"]},"note":"Indapamide is contraindicated in severe renal failure/anuria, severe hepatic impairment/encephalopathy, and sulfonamide hypersensitivity."}},{"key":"Renal Impairment","val":"HIGH — Loses diuretic efficacy at **eGFR** < 30.","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["renal"],"action":"caution","severity":"caution","match":{},"note":"Indapamide loses diuretic efficacy in severe renal impairment; review therapy and consider alternatives."}},{"key":"Pregnancy","val":"CAUTION — **Pregnancy** Category C.","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["pregnancy"],"action":"caution","severity":"caution","note":"Indapamide pregnancy row is a source-backed caution and should not trigger an automatic contraindication alert."}}]},{"title":"Key Interactions","type":"inter","rows":[{"key":"Pharmacodynamic","val":"HIGH — Lithium (decreases clearance, high toxicity risk).","tags":[]},{"key":"Triple Whammy","val":"CRITICAL — NSAIDs + ACEi + Indapamide.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Monitoring & Cautions","type":"mon","rows":[{"key":"Laboratory","val":"MANDATORY — Check **U&E** within 1-2 weeks of initiation. Elderly women are at massive risk of silent hyponatremia.","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["elderly"],"action":"dose-adjust","severity":"caution","match":{"age":{}},"note":"Inferred from existing decision-section wording; review before clinical use."}},{"key":"Bedside","val":"MANDATORY — Monitor **BP**.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Clinical Pearls","type":"pearl","rows":[{"key":"Metabolic Advantage","val":"If a hypertensive patient has pre-diabetes or hyperlipidemia, switch them from HCTZ to Indapamide to protect their metabolic profile.","tags":[]},{"key":"The Elderly Trap","val":"Hyponatremia on indapamide can present as confusion, lethargy, or frequent falls in the elderly, mimicking dementia. Always check a U&E first.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics","type":"evid","rows":[{"key":"Mechanism","val":"Inhibits Na+/Cl- cotransporter in distal tubule. Also has direct smooth muscle relaxant properties, acting as a direct vasodilator.","tags":[]},{"key":"Onset","val":"**PO** 1-2 hours.","tags":[]},{"key":"Duration","val":"24 h (SR).","tags":[]},{"key":"Half-life","val":"14-24 hours (Highly lipophilic, concentrates in vascular smooth muscle).","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Sources","type":"src","rows":[{"key":"Source Review","val":"Source-backed review 2026-05-11: NATRILIX/NATRILIX SR indapamide Australian PI. Entry checked for dosing, access, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and app structure."}]}],"quick":[{"label":"Class & Role","value":"Thiazide-like Diuretic — Thiazide-like diuretic."},{"label":"Route / Formulation","value":"Tablets IR (2.5 mg), Tablets SR (1.5 mg). (Natrilix, Natrilix SR, Coversyl Plus (combo))"},{"label":"Usual Dose & Max","value":"**PO** 1.25-2.5 mg (IR) **OD** morning. OR **PO** 1.5 mg (SR) **OD** morning. Max **2.5 mg/day** (IR) or **1.5 mg/day** (SR)."},{"label":"Key Indication Doses","value":"Hypertension: **PO** 1.25-2.5 mg (IR) **OD** morning. OR **PO** 1.5 mg (SR) **OD** morning.  Elderly / Frail: Prefer 1.5 mg SR **OD** to minimise rapid electrolyte shifts."},{"label":"Best Uses","value":"Indapamide is a thiazide-like diuretic with superior stroke prevention evidence (ADVANCE, HYVET), but carries the same electrolyte disturbance risks as conventional thiazides."},{"label":"Avoid / Cautions","value":"Severe hepatic failure (can precipitate hepatic encephalopathy), anuria, severe sulfa allergy. **Pregnancy** Category C."},{"label":"Key Risks","value":"Electrolytes: Severe hyponatremia and hypokalemia.  Metabolic: Mildly increases uric acid (gout risk), but largely neutral regarding blood glucose and lipids (unlike HCTZ).  Dermatological: Sulfa-allergy cross-reactivity, rare rash."},{"label":"Key Interactions","value":"Lithium (decreases clearance, high toxicity risk).  NSAIDs + ACEi + Indapamide."},{"label":"Monitoring","value":"Check **U&E** within 1-2 weeks of initiation. Elderly women are at massive risk of silent hyponatremia.  Monitor **BP**."},{"label":"Clinical Pearl","value":"If a hypertensive patient has pre-diabetes or hyperlipidemia, switch them from HCTZ to Indapamide to protect their metabolic profile."}]}},{"logicalId":"medications:insulin-aspart","logicalDocumentId":"286ae305-02e8-57c1-8a1f-55d7e7dcd305","logicalChunkId":"002061e5-d21b-5d99-aa7e-f5a6ee928203","normalizedText":"Medication: Insulin aspart\nClass: Antidiabetic\nSubclass: Prandial Insulin\nSchedule: S4\nTag: INSULIN\nRapid Summary summary Overview Ultra-rapid-acting mealtime (prandial) insulin. Designed to perfectly mimic the physiological burst of insulin a healthy pancreas releases when food hits the stomach. Maximum Dose Titrated dynamically based on meal carbohydrate content or sliding scales. Clinical Focus Must be given immediately before eating. If the patient is injected and then the meal is delayed, they will suffer a severe hypoglycemic crash. Bottom Line Insulin aspart is a rapid-acting mealtime insulin for prandial glucose control and insulin pump therapy, but requires carbohydrate counting skill and carries significant hypoglycaemia risk if mistimed. Risk Profile risk Metabolic CRITICAL — Rapid, severe hypoglycemia if meals are skipped, delayed, or lack carbohydrates. HIGH — Weight gain. Dermatological MODERATE — Lipohypertrophy at injection sites. Dosing & Administration dose Type 1 / Type 2 Diabetes **SC** Administered 5-10 minutes prior to a meal. Dose varies (e.g., 5-15 units per meal). Hyperkalemia / DKA **IV** Regular insulin (Actrapid) is preferred for IV infusions in ICU/ED, but rapid analogues can be used SC for correction doses. Formulation & Access form Brand Names NovoRapid, Fiasp (ultra-fast) Parenteral Pre-filled pens or cartridges for **SC** injection (100 U/mL). Clear solution. Prescribing & PBS Authority Required (PBS). Indications & Use ind Primary Glycemic control at mealtimes in Type 1 and Type 2 DM. Correction of acute hyperglycemia. Clinical Place The 'Bolus' in the basal-bolus regimen. Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — During active hypoglycemia. Patient refusing to eat. Pregnancy SAFE — **Pregnancy** Category A. Widely used in gestational diabetes. Key Interactions inter Pharmacodynamic HIGH — Beta-blockers (masks hypo symptoms). Alcohol (blocks hepatic gluconeogenesis, exacerbating hypos). Monitoring & Cautions mon Bedside MANDATORY — **BSL** checks pre-meals. Ensure the food tray is physically on the table before the nurse administers the injection. Laboratory Routine **HbA1c**. Clinical Pearls pearl The Fiasp Difference Fiasp is Insulin Aspart with added Niacinamide, which acts as a vasodilator. It absorbs even faster than NovoRapid. It can literally be injected *as* the patient takes their first bite of food. The Correction Factor 1 unit of rapid-acting insulin typically drops the blood sugar by ~2 to 3 mmol/L in an average adult. Do not 'stack' correction doses; wait 3 hours for the first dose to finish acting before correcting again. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism A single amino acid substitution (proline replaced by aspartic acid) prevents the insulin molecules from clumping together as hexamers. They remain as single monomers, allowing them to instantly diffuse into the bloodstream. Onset **SC** 10-20 mins. Peak 1-3 hours. Duration 3-5 hours (Matches the digestion time of a standard meal). Sources src Source Review Source Check - NOVORAPID ARTG/PI and PBS search checked 2026-05-13 for high-risk opioid/general batch appraisal: identity/access, indication, dosing, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, pregnancy/lactation, patient-context metadata, and source-row status.\nClass & Role Prandial Insulin — Ultra-rapid-acting mealtime (prandial) insulin. Route / Formulation Pre-filled pens or cartridges for **SC** injection (100 U/mL). Clear solution. (NovoRapid, Fiasp (ultra-fast)) Usual Dose & Max **SC** Administered 5-10 minutes prior to a meal. Dose varies (e.g., 5-15 units per meal). Titrated dynamically based on meal carbohydrate content or sliding scales. Key Indication Doses Type 1 / Type 2 Diabetes: **SC** Administered 5-10 minutes prior to a meal. Dose varies (e.g., 5-15 units per meal). Hyperkalemia / DKA: **IV** Regular insulin (Actrapid) is preferred for IV infusions in ICU/ED, but rapid analogues can be used SC for correction doses. Best Uses Insulin aspart is a rapid-acting mealtime insulin for prandial glucose control and insulin pump therapy, but requires carbohydrate counting skill and carries significant hypoglycaemia risk if mistimed. Avoid / Cautions During active hypoglycemia. Patient refusing to eat. **Pregnancy** Category A. Widely used in gestational diabetes. Key Risks Metabolic: Rapid, severe hypoglycemia if meals are skipped, delayed, or lack carbohydrates. HIGH — Weight gain. Dermatological: Lipohypertrophy at injection sites. Key Interactions Beta-blockers (masks hypo symptoms). Alcohol (blocks hepatic gluconeogenesis, exacerbating hypos). Monitoring **BSL** checks pre-meals. Ensure the food tray is physically on the table before the nurse administers the injection. Routine **HbA1c**. Clinical Pearl Fiasp is Insulin Aspart with added Niacinamide, which acts as a vasodilator. It absorbs even faster than NovoRapid. It can literally be injected *as* the patient takes their first bite of food.","contentHash":"a7563739c698ec8fc21eca7cbcbae4ebe4947e4d34d940453309c54d5da082c9","publicationFingerprint":"e307e90957118f24ea495f3824a0919d0e6221dece95c7db9193edec3ad60d80","governanceFingerprint":"6473895da16d5508d03ffea998bc94f9de73dd719d70d551126b969e94bd3eab","lineageFingerprint":"4f53cda18c2baa0c0354bb5f9a3ecbe5ed12ab4d8e11ba873c2f11161202b945","publicMetadataFingerprint":"eb969a339c5d058c76655ae1678f202ac0b230c946b891ea9860fc4ec1cfb575","record":{"version":"site-content-record-v1","logicalId":"medications:insulin-aspart","producerClass":"dynamic_registry","domain":"medications","route":"/medications/insulin-aspart","title":"Insulin aspart","body":"Medication: Insulin aspart\nClass: Antidiabetic\nSubclass: Prandial Insulin\nSchedule: S4\nTag: INSULIN\nRapid Summary summary Overview Ultra-rapid-acting mealtime (prandial) insulin. Designed to perfectly mimic the physiological burst of insulin a healthy pancreas releases when food hits the stomach. Maximum Dose Titrated dynamically based on meal carbohydrate content or sliding scales. Clinical Focus Must be given immediately before eating. If the patient is injected and then the meal is delayed, they will suffer a severe hypoglycemic crash. Bottom Line Insulin aspart is a rapid-acting mealtime insulin for prandial glucose control and insulin pump therapy, but requires carbohydrate counting skill and carries significant hypoglycaemia risk if mistimed. Risk Profile risk Metabolic CRITICAL — Rapid, severe hypoglycemia if meals are skipped, delayed, or lack carbohydrates. HIGH — Weight gain. Dermatological MODERATE — Lipohypertrophy at injection sites. Dosing & Administration dose Type 1 / Type 2 Diabetes **SC** Administered 5-10 minutes prior to a meal. Dose varies (e.g., 5-15 units per meal). Hyperkalemia / DKA **IV** Regular insulin (Actrapid) is preferred for IV infusions in ICU/ED, but rapid analogues can be used SC for correction doses. Formulation & Access form Brand Names NovoRapid, Fiasp (ultra-fast) Parenteral Pre-filled pens or cartridges for **SC** injection (100 U/mL). Clear solution. Prescribing & PBS Authority Required (PBS). Indications & Use ind Primary Glycemic control at mealtimes in Type 1 and Type 2 DM. Correction of acute hyperglycemia. Clinical Place The 'Bolus' in the basal-bolus regimen. Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — During active hypoglycemia. Patient refusing to eat. Pregnancy SAFE — **Pregnancy** Category A. Widely used in gestational diabetes. Key Interactions inter Pharmacodynamic HIGH — Beta-blockers (masks hypo symptoms). Alcohol (blocks hepatic gluconeogenesis, exacerbating hypos). Monitoring & Cautions mon Bedside MANDATORY — **BSL** checks pre-meals. Ensure the food tray is physically on the table before the nurse administers the injection. Laboratory Routine **HbA1c**. Clinical Pearls pearl The Fiasp Difference Fiasp is Insulin Aspart with added Niacinamide, which acts as a vasodilator. It absorbs even faster than NovoRapid. It can literally be injected *as* the patient takes their first bite of food. The Correction Factor 1 unit of rapid-acting insulin typically drops the blood sugar by ~2 to 3 mmol/L in an average adult. Do not 'stack' correction doses; wait 3 hours for the first dose to finish acting before correcting again. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism A single amino acid substitution (proline replaced by aspartic acid) prevents the insulin molecules from clumping together as hexamers. They remain as single monomers, allowing them to instantly diffuse into the bloodstream. Onset **SC** 10-20 mins. Peak 1-3 hours. Duration 3-5 hours (Matches the digestion time of a standard meal). Sources src Source Review Source Check - NOVORAPID ARTG/PI and PBS search checked 2026-05-13 for high-risk opioid/general batch appraisal: identity/access, indication, dosing, contraindications/popu