 Diabetes. Clinical Place The most advanced basal insulin. The absolute best choice for shift workers or teenagers with chaotic schedules who cannot take their insulin at the exact same time every day. Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — Active hypoglycemia. Pregnancy CAUTION — **Pregnancy** Category B3. Key Interactions inter Pharmacodynamic HIGH — Corticosteroids (spike BSL), Beta-blockers (mask hypos). Monitoring & Cautions mon Bedside MANDATORY — Monitor fasting **BSL**. Laboratory Routine **HbA1c**. Clinical Pearls pearl The Shift Worker's Insulin With Glargine, a 4-hour delay in injection causes a massive BSL spike, and taking it 4 hours early causes a severe overlap hypo. With Degludec, the 42-hour duration means the patient has a massive buffer. They can inject it whenever it is convenient that day. The DKA Buffer If a Type 1 diabetic forgets their basal insulin completely for a day, Glargine washes out and they go into DKA. Degludec's massive tail provides an extra 24 hours of background coverage, heavily protecting against missed-dose DKA. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Upon injection, it forms massive, stable multi-hexamer structures in the subcutaneous tissue. Zinc slowly diffuses away, allowing single insulin monomers to break off at a glacial, completely steady rate into the blood. Onset **SC** 1-2 hours. Duration 42 hours. Half-life 25 hours (Steady state takes 3-4 days). Sources src Source Review Source Check - TRESIBA ARTG/AusPAR PI and PBS search checked 2026-05-13 for high-risk opioid/general batch appraisal: identity/access, indication, dosing, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, pregnancy/lactation, patient-context metadata, and source-row status.\nClass & Role Ultra-Long Basal Insulin — Ultra-long-acting basal insulin. Route / Formulation Pre-filled pens for **SC** injection (100 U/mL). Clear solution. (Tresiba) Usual Dose & Max **SC** Administered ONCE daily at any time of day. Ensure at least 8 hours between doses. Titrated to fasting BSL. Key Indication Doses Type 1 / Type 2 Diabetes: **SC** Administered ONCE daily at any time of day. Ensure at least 8 hours between doses. Dose Adjustments: Because it takes 3-4 days to reach steady state, do NOT adjust the dose more frequently than every 3-4 days, or you will cause a stacking overdose. Best Uses Insulin degludec is an ultra-long-acting basal insulin with the lowest hypoglycaemia risk and flexible dosing, but is more expensive and offers marginal benefit over glargine for most patients. Avoid / Cautions Active hypoglycemia. **Pregnancy** Category B3. Key Risks Metabolic: Hypoglycemia. However, Degludec has a statistically significantly lower risk of severe nocturnal hypoglycemia compared to Glargine. Dermatological: Lipohypertrophy. Key Interactions Corticosteroids (spike BSL), Beta-blockers (mask hypos). Monitoring Monitor fasting **BSL**. Routine **HbA1c**. Clinical Pearl With Glargine, a 4-hour delay in injection causes a massive BSL spike, and taking it 4 hours early causes a severe overlap hypo. With Degludec, the 42-hour duration means the patient has a massive buffer. They can inject it whenever it is convenient that day.","sourceRole":"clinical_reference","access":"public","validationStatus":"unverified","sourceStatus":"current","sourceLineage":[],"publicationVersion":"0ed19a5320c4f3ddf0f2b81dafe78132e003781fa2095dc1721967256694ea14","contentHash":"eda7f5e0fe8bd639fc48e263638d1f5937a6f4f4f173a970ff3d8062d310fbed"},"renderPayload":{"slug":"insulin-degludec","name":"Insulin degludec","class":"Antidiabetic","subclass":"Ultra-Long Basal Insulin","category":"Endocrinology - Insulins & Injectables","accent":"#16a34a","tag":"INSULIN","schedule":"S4","stats":[{"label":"Max Dose","value":"Titrated","cls":"hi","flag":"hi"},{"label":"Duration","value":"42 HOURS","cls":"good","flag":"good"},{"label":"Timing","value":"FLEXIBLE","cls":"good","flag":""},{"label":"Hypos","value":"LOWER RISK","cls":"good","flag":""}],"sections":[{"title":"Rapid Summary","type":"summary","rows":[{"key":"Overview","val":"Ultra-long-acting basal insulin. Forms multi-hexamer chains under the skin, taking days to absorb. Provides the flattest, most consistent 24-hour profile with the lowest risk of nocturnal hypos.","tags":[]},{"key":"Maximum Dose","val":"Titrated to fasting BSL.","tags":[]},{"key":"Clinical Focus","val":"Because it lasts 42 hours, the injection timing is incredibly flexible. A patient can take it at 8 AM one day and 8 PM the next day without risking gaps or overlap.","tags":[]},{"key":"Bottom Line","val":"Insulin degludec is an ultra-long-acting basal insulin with the lowest hypoglycaemia risk and flexible dosing, but is more expensive and offers marginal benefit over glargine for most patients.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Risk Profile","type":"risk","rows":[{"key":"Metabolic","val":"CRITICAL — Hypoglycemia. However, Degludec has a statistically significantly lower risk of severe nocturnal hypoglycemia compared to Glargine.","tags":[]},{"key":"Dermatological","val":"MODERATE — Lipohypertrophy.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Dosing & Administration","type":"dose","rows":[{"key":"Type 1 / Type 2 Diabetes","val":"**SC** Administered ONCE daily at any time of day. Ensure at least 8 hours between doses.","tags":[]},{"key":"Dose Adjustments","val":"MANDATORY — Because it takes 3-4 days to reach steady state, do NOT adjust the dose more frequently than every 3-4 days, or you will cause a stacking overdose.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Formulation & Access","type":"form","rows":[{"key":"Brand Names","val":"Tresiba","tags":[]},{"key":"Parenteral","val":"Pre-filled pens for **SC** injection (100 U/mL). Clear solution.","tags":[]},{"key":"Prescribing & PBS","val":"Authority Required (PBS).","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Indications & Use","type":"ind","rows":[{"key":"Primary","val":"Type 1 and Type 2 Diabetes.","tags":["PBS","TGA"]},{"key":"Clinical Place","val":"The most advanced basal insulin. The absolute best choice for shift workers or teenagers with chaotic schedules who cannot take their insulin at the exact same time every day.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Contraindications & Populations","type":"contra","rows":[{"key":"Absolute","val":"CRITICAL — Active hypoglycemia.","tags":[]},{"key":"Pregnancy","val":"CAUTION — **Pregnancy** Category B3.","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["pregnancy"],"action":"caution","severity":"caution","note":"Insulin degludec pregnancy row is Category B3/caution, not an automatic contraindication alert."}}]},{"title":"Key Interactions","type":"inter","rows":[{"key":"Pharmacodynamic","val":"HIGH — Corticosteroids (spike BSL), Beta-blockers (mask hypos).","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Monitoring & Cautions","type":"mon","rows":[{"key":"Bedside","val":"MANDATORY — Monitor fasting **BSL**.","tags":[]},{"key":"Laboratory","val":"Routine **HbA1c**.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Clinical Pearls","type":"pearl","rows":[{"key":"The Shift Worker's Insulin","val":"With Glargine, a 4-hour delay in injection causes a massive BSL spike, and taking it 4 hours early causes a severe overlap hypo. With Degludec, the 42-hour duration means the patient has a massive buffer. They can inject it whenever it is convenient that day.","tags":[]},{"key":"The DKA Buffer","val":"If a Type 1 diabetic forgets their basal insulin completely for a day, Glargine washes out and they go into DKA. Degludec's massive tail provides an extra 24 hours of background coverage, heavily protecting against missed-dose DKA.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics","type":"evid","rows":[{"key":"Mechanism","val":"Upon injection, it forms massive, stable multi-hexamer structures in the subcutaneous tissue. Zinc slowly diffuses away, allowing single insulin monomers to break off at a glacial, completely steady rate into the blood.","tags":[]},{"key":"Onset","val":"**SC** 1-2 hours.","tags":[]},{"key":"Duration","val":"42 hours.","tags":[]},{"key":"Half-life","val":"25 hours (Steady state takes 3-4 days).","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Sources","type":"src","rows":[{"key":"Source Review","val":"Source Check - TRESIBA ARTG/AusPAR PI and PBS search checked 2026-05-13 for high-risk opioid/general batch appraisal: identity/access, indication, dosing, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, pregnancy/lactation, patient-context metadata, and source-row status."}]}],"quick":[{"label":"Class & Role","value":"Ultra-Long Basal Insulin — Ultra-long-acting basal insulin."},{"label":"Route / Formulation","value":"Pre-filled pens for **SC** injection (100 U/mL). Clear solution. (Tresiba)"},{"label":"Usual Dose & Max","value":"**SC** Administered ONCE daily at any time of day. Ensure at least 8 hours between doses. Titrated to fasting BSL."},{"label":"Key Indication Doses","value":"Type 1 / Type 2 Diabetes: **SC** Administered ONCE daily at any time of day. Ensure at least 8 hours between doses.  Dose Adjustments: Because it takes 3-4 days to reach steady state, do NOT adjust the dose more frequently than every 3-4 days, or you will cause a stacking overdose."},{"label":"Best Uses","value":"Insulin degludec is an ultra-long-acting basal insulin with the lowest hypoglycaemia risk and flexible dosing, but is more expensive and offers marginal benefit over glargine for most patients."},{"label":"Avoid / Cautions","value":"Active hypoglycemia. **Pregnancy** Category B3."},{"label":"Key Risks","value":"Metabolic: Hypoglycemia. However, Degludec has a statistically significantly lower risk of severe nocturnal hypoglycemia compared to Glargine.  Dermatological: Lipohypertrophy."},{"label":"Key Interactions","value":"Corticosteroids (spike BSL), Beta-blockers (mask hypos)."},{"label":"Monitoring","value":"Monitor fasting **BSL**.  Routine **HbA1c**."},{"label":"Clinical Pearl","value":"With Glargine, a 4-hour delay in injection causes a massive BSL spike, and taking it 4 hours early causes a severe overlap hypo. With Degludec, the 42-hour duration means the patient has a massive buffer. They can inject it whenever it is convenient that day."}]}},{"logicalId":"medications:insulin-detemir","logicalDocumentId":"b3349db7-8ba9-51d8-80ae-31b671dd2f70","logicalChunkId":"9ce68337-cc15-5fbf-ac40-62e10aaa0377","normalizedText":"Medication: Insulin detemir\nClass: Antidiabetic\nSubclass: Basal Insulin\nSchedule: S4\nTag: INSULIN\nRapid Summary summary Overview Long-acting basal insulin. Binds to albumin in the blood to delay its action. Often lasts less than 24 hours, frequently requiring twice-daily dosing to prevent gap hyperglycemia. Maximum Dose Titrated strictly to fasting BSL. Clinical Focus Uniquely associated with slightly LESS weight gain than Glargine or old NPH insulins. Bottom Line Insulin detemir is a long-acting basal insulin with less weight gain than glargine, but may require twice-daily dosing and has largely been superseded by insulin degludec. Risk Profile risk Metabolic CRITICAL — Hypoglycemia. HIGH — Hypokalemia (insulin drives K+ into cells). Dermatological MODERATE — Lipohypertrophy (lumps) at injection sites. Dosing & Administration dose Type 1 / Type 2 Diabetes **SC** Administered once or twice daily. If given OD, usually given at dinner or bedtime. If coverage fails at 18 hours, must be split into **BD** dosing. Renal Impairment MANDATORY — Reduce dose in severe renal failure to prevent severe hypos. Formulation & Access form Brand Names Levemir Parenteral Pre-filled pens or cartridges for **SC** injection (100 U/mL). Clear solution. Prescribing & PBS Authority Required (PBS). Indications & Use ind Primary Type 1 and Type 2 Diabetes. Clinical Place A solid basal insulin, but increasingly superseded by Glargine or Degludec due to its annoying tendency to require BD injections in many patients. Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — Active hypoglycemia. Pregnancy SAFE — **Pregnancy** Category A. Highly studied and very safe for basal coverage in gestational/Type 1 pregnancy. Key Interactions inter Pharmacodynamic HIGH — Beta-blockers mask hypoglycemia. Corticosteroids massively increase insulin requirements. Monitoring & Cautions mon Bedside MANDATORY — Check fasting **BSL**. If fasting BSL is good, but pre-dinner BSL is 18, the Detemir is wearing off at 16 hours. Split the dose to BD. Laboratory Routine **HbA1c**. Clinical Pearls pearl The Weight Advantage For reasons not entirely understood (potentially related to BBB penetration and satiety signals), Detemir causes statistically less weight gain than Glargine. A good choice for obese T2DM patients requiring basal insulin. Never IV Detemir must NEVER be given IV. The massive albumin binding would cause entirely unpredictable and dangerous kinetics. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism A fatty acid (myristic acid) is chemically bound to the insulin molecule. Once injected, it binds tightly to albumin in the subcutaneous tissue and in the blood, creating a slow, steady release of free insulin. Onset **SC** 1-2 hours. Duration 16-24 hours (Dose-dependent. Low doses wear off very fast). Peak Relatively flat, but has a slight peak at 6-8 hours compared to Glargine. Sources src Source Review Source Check - LEVEMIR ARTG, AusPAR PI, and PBS search checked 2026-05-13 for high-risk opioid/general batch appraisal: identity/access, indication, dosing, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, pregnancy/lactation, patient-context metadata, and source-row status.\nClass & Role Basal Insulin — Long-acting basal insulin. Route / Formulation Pre-filled pens or cartridges for **SC** injection (100 U/mL). Clear solution. (Levemir) Usual Dose & Max **SC** Administered once or twice daily. If given OD, usually given at dinner or bedtime. If coverage fails at 18 hours, must be split into **BD** dosing. Titrated strictly to fasting BSL. Key Indication Doses Type 1 / Type 2 Diabetes: **SC** Administered once or twice daily. If given OD, usually given at dinner or bedtime. If coverage fails at 18 hours, must be split into **BD** dosing. Renal Impairment: Reduce dose in severe renal failure to prevent severe hypos. Best Uses Insulin detemir is a long-acting basal insulin with less weight gain than glargine, but may require twice-daily dosing and has largely been superseded by insulin degludec. Avoid / Cautions Active hypoglycemia. **Pregnancy** Category A. Highly studied and very safe for basal coverage in gestational/Type 1 pregnancy. Key Risks Metabolic: Hypoglycemia. HIGH — Hypokalemia (insulin drives K+ into cells). Dermatological: Lipohypertrophy (lumps) at injection sites. Key Interactions Beta-blockers mask hypoglycemia. Corticosteroids massively increase insulin requirements. Monitoring Check fasting **BSL**. If fasting BSL is good, but pre-dinner BSL is 18, the Detemir is wearing off at 16 hours. Split the dose to BD. Routine **HbA1c**. Clinical Pearl For reasons not entirely understood (potentially related to BBB penetration and satiety signals), Detemir causes statistically less weight gain than Glargine. A good choice for obese T2DM patients requiring basal insulin.","contentHash":"51673b987a0d7f0ee6fa6daa5f5ca2002987221dc762f490e9546536d9a7dd28","publicationFingerprint":"173c117958e792d77e542d36e5a4407d2f8268e1ed793b358543291d888e343c","governanceFingerprint":"6473895da16d5508d03ffea998bc94f9de73dd719d70d551126b969e94bd3eab","lineageFingerprint":"4f53cda18c2baa0c0354bb5f9a3ecbe5ed12ab4d8e11ba873c2f11161202b945","publicMetadataFingerprint":"439c19637b3ebde0a3fe2c0299c57c7f6e4391d16783a0ae378bbb82f7b5b90b","record":{"version":"site-content-record-v1","logicalId":"medications:insulin-detemir","producerClass":"dynamic_registry","domain":"medications","route":"/medications/insulin-detemir","title":"Insulin detemir","body":"Medication: Insulin detemir\nClass: Antidiabetic\nSubclass: Basal Insulin\nSchedule: S4\nTag: INSULIN\nRapid Summary summary Overview Long-acting basal insulin. Binds to albumin in the blood to delay its action. Often lasts less than 24 