neys). Sources src Source Review Source Check - TRAJENTA ARTG/PI and PBS search checked 2026-05-13 for high-risk opioid/general batch appraisal: identity/access, indication, dosing, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, pregnancy/lactation, patient-context metadata, and source-row status.\nClass & Role DPP-4 Inhibitor — DPP-4 Inhibitor (Gliptin). Route / Formulation Tablets (5 mg). (Trajenta, Trajentamet (combo with metformin)) Usual Dose & Max **PO** 5 mg **OD** (with or without food). Max **5 mg/day** (Flat dosing). Key Indication Doses Type 2 Diabetes: **PO** 5 mg **OD** (with or without food). Renal / Hepatic Impairment: No dose adjustments required for any degree of renal or hepatic impairment. Best Uses Linagliptin is the only DPP-4 inhibitor requiring no renal dose adjustment (hepatic elimination), but provides modest HbA1c reduction and lacks cardiovascular outcome benefits. Avoid / Cautions History of pancreatitis. Type 1 Diabetes. **Pregnancy** Category B3. Key Risks Gastrointestinal: Acute pancreatitis (rare but severe). LOW — Nausea, diarrhea. Musculoskeletal: Severe, disabling arthralgia (joint pain) has been linked to the entire DPP-4 class. Resolves on cessation. Endocrine: Zero hypoglycemia risk when used as monotherapy. Weight neutral. Key Interactions Strong CYP3A4/P-gp inducers (Rifampicin) significantly drop linagliptin blood levels, reducing efficacy. Monitoring Warn patient to report severe, radiating upper abdominal pain immediately (pancreatitis). Routine **HbA1c**. Clinical Pearl Every other oral diabetes drug (Metformin, Gliclazide, Sitagliptin, Empagliflozin) either stops working or becomes highly toxic in severe renal failure. Linagliptin is the only oral drug you can safely give at full dose to a dialysis patient.","sourceRole":"clinical_reference","access":"public","validationStatus":"unverified","sourceStatus":"current","sourceLineage":[],"publicationVersion":"480cfea06cc17d86b19698d9222e9d338d9c77edae1954c7a92f0a4e8