rapid heart rates. First-line for post-MI cardioprotection. Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — Severe asthma, 2nd/3rd degree heart block, sick sinus syndrome, uncompensated acute decompensated heart failure. Pregnancy CAUTION — **Pregnancy** Category C (risk of neonatal bradycardia). Hepatic Impairment CAUTION — Highly metabolised by the liver. **Hepatic** impairment increases bioavailability. Key Interactions inter Pharmacodynamic CRITICAL — Verapamil and Diltiazem. Concurrent use risks fatal asystole. Pharmacokinetic HIGH — **CYP2D6** inhibitors (e.g., fluoxetine, paroxetine) will significantly increase metoprolol levels and bradycardia risk. Monitoring & Cautions mon Bedside MANDATORY — Hold dose if **HR** < 50 bpm or systolic **BP** < 90 mmHg. Check before every administration. Cardiac MANDATORY — Baseline **ECG** to rule out existing heart block before initiation. Clinical Pearls pearl Salt Matters Metoprolol Tartrate is immediate release (dosed BD/TDS). Metoprolol Succinate is controlled release (dosed OD). Succinate is the ONLY form proven to reduce mortality in Heart Failure. The Asthma Myth While 'cardioselective', Metoprolol will still trigger life-threatening bronchospasm in susceptible asthmatics. Use alternative rate-control (like Diltiazem) if asthma is severe. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Selective Beta-1 receptor antagonist. Decreases heart rate, contractility, and AV nodal conduction. Onset **PO** 1-2 hours. **IV** 5 mins. Duration 12 hours (Tartrate), 24 hours (Succinate CR). Half-life 3-7 hours (Tartrate undergoes extensive hepatic first-pass metabolism). Sources src Source Review Source-backed review 2026-05-11: BETALOC/metoprolol Australian PI and TOPROL-XL Australian CMI source pack. Entry checked for dosing, access, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and app structure.\nClass & Role Beta Blocker — Cardioselective (Beta-1) adrenergic antagonist. Route / Formulation Tartrate Tablets (50 mg, 100 mg). Succinate CR Tablets (23.5 mg, 47 mg, 95 mg, 190 mg). (Betaloc (Tartrate IR), Betaloc CR, Minax) Usual Dose & Max **PO** 25-50 mg (Tartrate) **BD** or **TDS**. May use **IV** 2.5-5 mg slowly (Specialist). Max **200 mg/day** (often divided **BD** for tartrate form). Key Indication Doses AF / Rate Control (Acute): **PO** 25-50 mg (Tartrate) **BD** or **TDS**. May use **IV** 2.5-5 mg slowly (Specialist). Heart Failure (HFrEF): **PO** 11.75 mg or 23.5 mg (Succinate CR) **OD**. Titrate slowly to Max **190 mg OD**. Post-MI / Angina: **PO** 50 mg (Tartrate) **BD**. Max **200 mg/day**. Best Uses Metoprolol is the first-line beta-blocker for heart failure and acute MI with both oral and IV formulations, but requires careful dose titration and must not be stopped abruptly. Avoid / Cautions Severe asthma, 2nd/3rd degree heart block, sick sinus syndrome, uncompensated acute decompensated heart failure. **Pregnancy** Category C (risk of neonatal bradycardia). Key Risks Cardiovascular: Bradycardia, AV block, cardiogenic shock in acute overdose. Respiratory: Bronchospasm (despite being 'cardioselective', selectivity is lost at high doses). Endocrine: Can mask the physiological symptoms of hypoglycemia (tachycardia, tremor) in diabetics. Neurological: Fatigue, vivid dreams, cold extremities. Key Interactions Verapamil and Diltiazem. Concurrent use risks fatal asystole. **CYP2D6** inhibitors (e.g., fluoxetine, paroxetine) will significantly increase metoprolol levels and bradycardia risk. Monitoring Hold dose if **HR** < 50 bpm or systolic **BP** < 90 mmHg. Check before every administration. Baseline **ECG** to rule out existing heart block before initiation. Clinical Pearl Metoprolol Tartrate is immediate release (dosed BD/TDS). Metoprolol Succinate is controlled release (dosed OD). Succinate is the ONLY form proven to reduce mortality in Heart Failure.","sourceRole":"clinical_reference","access":"public","validationStatus":"unverified","sourceStatus":"current","sourceLineage":[],"publicationVersion":"76f93741323d4dc561068c5d4d669c6182f1efa7e74567e96715108aacfaa401","contentHash":"a930c2c67837bec8d64786ff52453e334fd10a26cb658077da8cb3f66330bf71"},"renderPayload":{"slug":"metoprolol","name":"Metoprolol","class":"Antihypertensive","subclass":"Beta Blocker","category":"Cardiovascular - Beta Blockers","accent":"#0284c7","tag":"BETA BLOCKER","schedule":"S4","stats":[{"label":"Max Dose","value":"200 mg/day","cls":"hi","flag":"hi"},{"label":"Half-life","value":"3–7 h","cls":"","flag":""},{"label":"Asthma","value":"CONTRAIND.","cls":"hi","flag":""},{"label":"Bradycardia","value":"RISK","cls":"warn","flag":""}],"sections":[{"title":"Rapid Summary","type":"summary","rows":[{"key":"Overview","val":"Cardioselective (Beta-1) adrenergic antagonist. A staple of ward medicine for rate control, angina, and post-MI management.","tags":[]},{"key":"Maximum Dose","val":"Max **200 mg/day** (often divided **BD** for tartrate form).","tags":[]},{"key":"Clinical Focus","val":"Tartrate (IR) is used for acute rate control. Succinate (CR) is used for chronic heart failure. Do not mix them up.","tags":[]},{"key":"Bottom Line","val":"Metoprolol is the first-line beta-blocker for heart failure and acute MI with both oral and IV formulations, but requires careful dose titration and must not be stopped abruptly.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Risk Profile","type":"risk","rows":[{"key":"Cardiovascular","val":"CRITICAL — Bradycardia, AV block, cardiogenic shock in acute overdose.","tags":[]},{"key":"Respiratory","val":"HIGH — Bronchospasm (despite being 'cardioselective', selectivity is lost at high doses).","tags":[]},{"key":"Endocrine","val":"MODERATE — Can mask the physiological symptoms of hypoglycemia (tachycardia, tremor) in diabetics.","tags":[]},{"key":"Neurological","val":"LOW — Fatigue, vivid dreams, cold extremities.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Dosing & Administration","type":"dose","rows":[{"key":"AF / Rate Control (Acute)","val":"**PO** 25-50 mg (Tartrate) **BD** or **TDS**. May use **IV** 2.5-5 mg slowly (Specialist).","tags":[]},{"key":"Heart Failure (HFrEF)","val":"**PO** 11.75 mg or 23.5 mg (Succinate CR) **OD**. Titrate slowly to Max **190 mg OD**.","tags":[]},{"key":"Post-MI / Angina","val":"**PO** 50 mg (Tartrate) **BD**. Max **200 mg/day**.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Formulation & Access","type":"form","rows":[{"key":"Brand Names","val":"Betaloc (Tartrate IR), Betaloc CR, Minax","tags":[]},{"key":"Oral Routes","val":"Tartrate Tablets (50 mg, 100 mg). Succinate CR Tablets (23.5 mg, 47 mg, 95 mg, 190 mg).","tags":[]},{"key":"Parenteral","val":"Ampoule (5 mg/5 mL) for **IV** administration (high risk of bradycardia/arrest).","tags":[]},{"key":"Prescribing & PBS","val":"Unrestricted General Benefit (S4).","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Indications & Use","type":"ind","rows":[{"key":"Primary","val":"AF rate control, angina, post-MI, HFrEF (succinate only).","tags":["PBS","TGA"]},{"key":"Clinical Place","val":"Workhorse beta-blocker on the ward for rapid heart rates. First-line for post-MI cardioprotection.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Contraindications & Populations","type":"contra","rows":[{"key":"Absolute","val":"CRITICAL — Severe asthma, 2nd/3rd degree heart block, sick sinus syndrome, uncompensated acute decompensated heart failure.","tags":[]},{"key":"Pregnancy","val":"CAUTION — **Pregnancy** Category C (risk of neonatal bradycardia).","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["pregnancy"],"action":"caution","severity":"caution","note":"Metoprolol pregnancy row is a source-backed caution and should not trigger an automatic contraindication alert."}},{"key":"Hepatic Impairment","val":"CAUTION — Highly metabolised by the liver. **Hepatic** impairment increases bioavailability.","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["hepatic"],"action":"dose-adjust","severity":"caution","match":{"hepatic":["mild","moderate","severe"]},"note":"Metoprolol hepatic impairment row should prompt dose review/monitoring rather than automatic contraindication."}}]},{"title":"Key Interactions","type":"inter","rows":[{"key":"Pharmacodynamic","val":"CRITICAL — Verapamil and Diltiazem. Concurrent use risks fatal asystole.","tags":[]},{"key":"Pharmacokinetic","val":"HIGH — **CYP2D6** inhibitors (e.g., fluoxetine, paroxetine) will significantly increase metoprolol levels and bradycardia risk.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Monitoring & Cautions","type":"mon","rows":[{"key":"Bedside","val":"MANDATORY — Hold dose if **HR** < 50 bpm or systolic **BP** < 90 mmHg. Check before every administration.","tags":[]},{"key":"Cardiac","val":"MANDATORY — Baseline **ECG** to rule out existing heart block before initiation.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Clinical Pearls","type":"pearl","rows":[{"key":"Salt Matters","val":"Metoprolol Tartrate is immediate release (dosed BD/TDS). Metoprolol Succinate is controlled release (dosed OD). Succinate is the ONLY form proven to reduce mortality in Heart Failure.","tags":[]},{"key":"The Asthma Myth","val":"While 'cardioselective', Metoprolol will still trigger life-threatening bronchospasm in susceptible asthmatics. Use alternative rate-control (like Diltiazem) if asthma is severe.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics","type":"evid","rows":[{"key":"Mechanism","val":"Selective Beta-1 receptor antagonist. Decreases heart rate, contractility, and AV nodal conduction.","tags":[]},{"key":"Onset","val":"**PO** 1-2 hours. **IV** 5 mins.","tags":[]},{"key":"Duration","val":"12 hours (Tartrate), 24 hours (Succinate CR).","tags":[]},{"key":"Half-life","val":"3-7 hours (Tartrate undergoes extensive hepatic first-pass metabolism).","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Sources","type":"src","rows":[{"key":"Source Review","val":"Source-backed review 2026-05-11: BETALOC/metoprolol Australian PI and TOPROL-XL Australian CMI source pack. Entry checked for dosing, access, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and app structure."}]}],"quick":[{"label":"Class & Role","value":"Beta Blocker — Cardioselective (Beta-1) adrenergic antagonist."},{"label":"Route / Formulation","value":"Tartrate Tablets (50 mg, 100 mg). Succinate CR Tablets (23.5 mg, 47 mg, 95 mg, 190 mg). (Betaloc (Tartrate IR), Betaloc CR, Minax)"},{"label":"Usual Dose & Max","value":"**PO** 25-50 mg (Tartrate) **BD** or **TDS**. May use **IV** 2.5-5 mg slowly (Specialist). Max **200 mg/day** (often divided **BD** for tartrate form)."},{"label":"Key Indication Doses","value":"AF / Rate Control (Acute): **PO** 25-50 mg (Tartrate) **BD** or **TDS**. May use **IV** 2.5-5 mg slowly (Specialist).  Heart Failure (HFrEF): **PO** 11.75 mg or 23.5 mg (Succinate CR) **OD**. Titrate slowly to Max **190 mg OD**.  Post-MI / Angina: **PO** 50 mg (Tartrate) **BD**. Max **200 mg/day**."},{"label":"Best Uses","value":"Metoprolol is the first-line beta-blocker for heart failure and acute MI with both oral and IV formulations, but requires careful dose titration and must not be stopped abruptly."},{"label":"Avoid / Cautions","value":"Severe asthma, 2nd/3rd degree heart block, sick sinus syndrome, uncompensated acute decompensated heart failure. **Pregnancy** Category C (risk of neonatal bradycardia)."},{"label":"Key Risks","value":"Cardiovascular: Bradycardia, AV block, cardiogenic shock in acute overdose.  Respiratory: Bronchospasm (despite being 'cardioselective', selectivity is lost at high doses).  Endocrine: Can mask the physiological symptoms of hypoglycemia (tachycardia, tremor) in diabetics.  Neurological: Fatigue, vivid dreams, cold extremities."},{"label":"Key Interactions","value":"Verapamil and Diltiazem. Concurrent use risks fatal asystole.  **CYP2D6** inhibitors (e.g., fluoxetine, paroxetine) will significantly increase metoprolol levels and bradycardia risk."},{"label":"Monitoring","value":"Hold dose if **HR** < 50 bpm or systolic **BP** < 90 mmHg. Check before every administration.  Baseline **ECG** to rule out existing heart block before initiation."},{"label":"Clinical Pearl","value":"Metoprolol Tartrate is immediate release (dosed BD/TDS). Metoprolol Succinate is controlled release (dosed OD). Succinate is the ONLY form proven to reduce mortality in Heart Failure."}]}},{"logicalId":"medications:metronidazole","logicalDocumentId":"6eb9ba22-d01a-568a-abfc-5539c967c148","logicalChunkId":"ba0d68ae-d07d-5c25-a20c-50811f6993d2","normalizedText":"Medication: Metronidazole\nClass: Antibiotic\nSubclass: Nitroimidazole\nSchedule: S4\nTag: NITROIMIDAZOLE\nRapid Summary summary Overview Highly potent bactericidal agent against strict anaerobes and protozoa. Penetrates abscesses, bone, and the blood-brain barrier beautifully. Maximum Dose Max **2 g/day**. Clinical Focus Interacts violently with alcohol. Prolonged courses cause peripheral neuropathy. Bottom Line Metronidazole is the first-line treatment for anaerobic infections and C. difficile, but causes a severe disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol and peripheral neuropathy with prolonged use. Risk Profile risk Neurological HIGH — Peripheral neuropathy (usually with courses > 14 days), optic neuropathy. CRITICAL — Seizures, encephalopathy at massive doses. Gastrointestinal HIGH — Severe nausea, metallic taste in mouth, anorexia. Haematological LOW — Mild, reversible neutropenia. Dosing & Administration dose Anaerobic Sepsis / Abscess **IV** 500 mg q12h (or **PO** 400 mg q12h). C. Difficile (Mild) **PO** 400 mg **TDS** for 10-14 days. Trichomoniasis / Giardia **PO** 2 g STAT single dose. Hepatic Impairment MANDATORY — Reduce dose in severe liver disease (metabolised hepatically). Formulation & Access form Brand Names Flagyl, Metrogyl Oral Routes Tablets (200 mg, 400 mg). Parenteral Pre-mixed infusion bags (500 mg/100 mL) for **IV** use. Suppositories for **PR**. Prescribing & PBS Unrestricted General Benefit (S4). Indications & Use ind Primary Intra-abdominal sepsis, pelvic inflammatory disease, brain abscess, C. difficile, Trichomonas, Giardia. Clinical Place The foundational drug for any infection 'below the diaphragm' to cover gut anaerobes (Bacteroides). Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — Active CNS disease (seizures). Pregnancy CAUTION — **Pregnancy** Category B2. Often avoided in 1st trimester but used if required. Key Interactions inter Pharmacodynamic CRITICAL — Alcohol. Causes a severe 'Disulfiram-like' reaction (accumulation of acetaldehyde) resulting in violent vomiting, flushing, tachycardia, and hypotension. Must avoid alcohol during and for 48h after course. Pharmacokinetic HIGH — Warfarin. Metronidazole inhibits CYP2C9, drastically spiking INR. Halve the Warfarin dose proactively. Monitoring & Cautions mon Bedside Warn patients about metallic taste and strict zero-alcohol rule. Laboratory Monitor **LFTs** if prolonged course. Clinical Pearls pearl The Bioavailability Win Oral metronidazole has 100% bioavailability. If the patient can swallow, switch from IV to PO instantly to save money and protect their veins. The Neuropathy Trap If an intra-abdominal abscess requires 6 weeks of metronidazole, actively ask the patient every week about tingling in their fingers or toes. Cease the drug if neuropathy starts, as it can become permanent. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Prodrug activated strictly by anaerobic bacteria. Forms toxic free radicals that catastrophically shatter bacterial DNA. Onset **PO** 1-2 hours. Half-life 8 hours. Exceptional bioavailability (near 100%). Sources src Source Review Source Check - FLAGYL/metronidazole ARTG/CMI and PBS search checked 2026-05-13 for high-risk opioid/general batch appraisal: identity/access, indication, dosing, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, pregnancy/lactation, patient-context metadata, and source-row status.\nClass & Role Nitroimidazole — Highly potent bactericidal agent against strict anaerobes and protozoa. Route / Formulation Tablets (200 mg, 400 mg). (Flagyl, Metrogyl) Usual Dose & Max **IV** 500 mg q12h (or **PO** 400 mg q12h). Max **2 g/day**. Key Indication Doses Anaerobic Sepsis / Abscess: **IV** 500 mg q12h (or **PO** 400 mg q12h). C. Difficile (Mild): **PO** 400 mg **TDS** for 10-14 days. Trichomoniasis / Giardia: **PO** 2 g STAT single dose. Hepatic Impairment: Reduce dose in severe liver disease (metabolised hepatically). Best Uses Metronidazole is the first-line treatment for anaerobic infections and C. difficile, but causes a severe disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol and peripheral neuropathy with prolonged use. Avoid / Cautions Active CNS disease (seizures). **Pregnancy** Category B2. Often avoided in 1st trimester but used if required. Key Risks Neurological: Peripheral neuropathy (usually with courses > 14 days), optic neuropathy. CRITICAL — Seizures, encephalopathy at massive doses. Gastrointestinal: Severe nausea, metallic taste in mouth, anorexia. Haematological: Mild, reversible neutropenia. Key Interactions Alcohol. Causes a severe 'Disulfiram-like' reaction (accumulation of acetaldehyde) resulting in violent vomiting, flushing, tachycardia, and hypotension. Must avoid alcohol during and for 48h after course. Warfarin. Metronidazole inhibits CYP2C9, drastically spiking INR. Halve the Warfarin dose proactively. Monitoring Warn patients about metallic taste and strict zero-alcohol rule. Monitor **LFTs** if prolonged course. Clinical Pearl Oral metronidazole has 100% bioavailability. If the patient can swallow, switch from IV to PO instantly to save money and protect their veins.","contentHash":"001071773106305f05f099e98b4adec9002205f333574e948870d18069ae3c16","publicationFingerprint":"d0753fcb59890674a1b6d16925b7bf3c42e54be1f4994bd97e3e7ad0a0450e4d","governanceFingerprint":"6473895da16d5508d03ffea998bc94f9de73dd719d70d551126b969e94bd3eab","lineageFingerprint":"4f53cda18c2baa0c0354bb5f9a3ecbe5ed12ab4d8e11ba873c2f11161202b945","publicMetadataFingerprint":"98d13a9b0457c35b26062e7bc43e0ec7517a7464712858b786dadfb5d2ef4182","record":{"version":"site-content-record-v1","logicalId":"medica