pulations contra Absolute CRITICAL — Not for acute bronchospasm. Psychiatric CAUTION — History of severe depression or suicidal ideation. Pregnancy SAFE — **Pregnancy** Category B1. Key Interactions inter Pharmacokinetic LOW — Phenytoin and phenobarbital can induce CYP enzymes and reduce montelukast levels. Monitoring & Cautions mon Bedside MANDATORY — Warn parents/patients strictly to monitor for mood changes, horrific nightmares, or behavioral shifts within the first weeks of therapy. Laboratory No specific routine laboratory tests required. Clinical Pearls pearl Nighttime Dosing Should ideally be taken in the evening (NOCTE) to match the circadian rhythm of leukotriene release, which naturally peaks overnight and causes early-morning asthma symptoms. The NSAID Asthma Link Aspirin-Exacerbated Respiratory Disease (AERD) is caused by COX blockade shunting arachidonic acid entirely down the leukotriene pathway. Montelukast specifically blocks this, making it highly effective for these patients. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Highly specific antagonist of the cysteinyl leukotriene receptor (CysLT1). Blocks the action of leukotrienes released by mast cells/eosinophils, reducing airway oedema, smooth muscle contraction, and inflammation. Onset **PO** Within hours to days. Duration 24 h. Half-life 3-5 hours. Extensive hepatic metabolism (CYP3A4, CYP2C8). Sources src Source Review Source-backed review 2026-05-11: SINGULAIR/montelukast Australian PI source pack. Entry checked for dosing, access, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and app structure.\nClass & Role Leukotriene Receptor Antagonist — Oral leukotriene receptor antagonist. Route / Formulation Tablets (10 mg). Chewable tablets (4 mg, 5 mg). (Singulair, Lukair) Usual Dose & Max **PO** 10 mg **NOCTE**. Max **10 mg/day**. Key Indication Doses Asthma (Adults): **PO** 10 mg **NOCTE**. Asthma (Children 6-14): **PO** 5 mg chewable tablet **NOCTE**. Asthma (Children 2-5): **PO** 4 mg chewable tablet **NOCTE**. Best Uses Montelukast is a useful add-on for exercise-induced and allergic asthma, but carries an FDA black box warning for neuropsychiatric events including suicidality. Avoid / Cautions Not for acute bronchospasm. **Pregnancy** Category B1. Key Risks Neurological: Neuropsychiatric events: agitation, aggressive behavior, dream abnormalities, hallucinations, depression, suicidal ideation. Gastrointestinal: Mild GI upset. Immunological: RARE — Churg-Strauss syndrome (eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis). Key Interactions Phenytoin and phenobarbital can induce CYP enzymes and reduce montelukast levels. Monitoring Warn parents/patients strictly to monitor for mood changes, horrific nightmares, or behavioral shifts within the first weeks of therapy. No specific routine laboratory tests required. Clinical Pearl Should ideally be taken in the evening (NOCTE) to match the circadian rhythm of leukotriene release, which naturally peaks overnight and causes early-morning asthma symptoms.","sourceRole":"clinical_reference","access":"public","validationStatus":"unverified","sourceStatus":"current","sourceLineage":[],"publicationVersion":"7d4215c664475e8558a85b4d064759ecd700f66fb243ae762b54e00e86a9c42e","contentHash":"fc0a653488c2c26039cee098a3a346a35c8ccfa5fcc8dece5ca2f896a0444632"},"renderPayload":{"slug":"montelukast","name":"Montelukast","class":"Respiratory","subclass":"Leukotriene Receptor Antagonist","category":"Respiratory - Asthma & COPD","accent":"#475569","tag":"LTRA","schedule":"S4","stats":[{"label":"Max Dose","value":"10 mg/day","cls":"hi","flag":"hi"},{"label":"Half-life","value":"3-5 h","cls":"","flag":""},{"label":"Psych Risk","value":"BLACK BOX","cls":"hi","flag":"warn"},{"label":"Freq","value":"NOCTE","cls":"good","flag":""}],"sections":[{"title":"Rapid Summary","type":"summary","rows":[{"key":"Overview","val":"Oral leukotriene receptor antagonist. An add-on therapy for asthma and allergic rhinitis, p