cg (Micro-dosing). Repeat every 2 mins. (Goal is to restore breathing without causing agonizing withdrawal pain). Community Overdose: **Intranasal** (Nyxoid) 1.8 mg spray into one nostril. Best Uses Naloxone is the essential opioid antagonist for reversal of life-threatening respiratory depression, but has a shorter half-life than most opioids requiring repeated dosing and observation. Avoid / Cautions NONE — No absolute contraindications in life-threatening respiratory depression. Key Risks Neurological: Acute, violent opioid withdrawal (agitation, aggression, vomiting, extreme pain, seizures). Cardiovascular: Tachycardia, severe hypertension, acute pulmonary oedema (from massive sympathetic surge upon waking). Key Interactions Buprenorphine (Suboxone). Buprenorphine binds so tightly to the receptor that standard doses of Naloxone cannot displace it. Massive doses (or continuous infusions) are required. Monitoring Continuous **SpO2** and **RR**. The patient must be observed for at least 2-4 hours post-administration (or longer for sustained-release opioids) to catch re-sedation. Clinical Pearl Methadone lasts for 40 hours. Naloxone lasts for 1 hour. If you reverse a methadone overdose, you MUST set up a continuous IV Naloxone infusion (e.g., 2/3 of the wake-up dose per hour), or they will die when you turn your back.","sourceRole":"clinical_reference","access":"public","validationStatus":"unverified","sourceStatus":"current","sourceLineage":[],"publicationVersion":"8c8325b98b0c13c13d2ac1d412b82d44a637b3d1ce9843ff7b405def6b55cbc1","contentHash":"1e85d069e44ab8f50b0afac35a22f640d1b44dc53af4528f51544870c2241409"},"renderPayload":{"slug":"naloxone","name":"Naloxone","class":"Antidote","subclass":"Opioid Antagonist","category":"Toxicology & Antidotes","accent":"#e11d48","tag":"REVERSAL","schedule":"S4","stats":[{"label":"Max Dose","value":"10 mg (STAT)","cls":"hi","flag":"hi"},{"label":"Half-life","value":"1 h (Short!)","cls":"warn","flag":"warn"},{"label":"Resedation",