opulations contra Absolute CONTRAINDICATED - Do not use nicotine gum in non-tobacco users, children under 12 years, or patients with hypersensitivity to nicotine or product components. Medical review Use medical review for unstable cardiovascular disease, diabetes, active GI disease, severe renal/hepatic impairment, uncontrolled hyperthyroidism, phaeochromocytoma, or seizure history. Pregnancy CAUTION - Use during pregnancy or lactation only after risk-benefit review. Intermittent NRT may help reduce nicotine exposure timing compared with continuous forms, but smoking cessation support remains first-line. Key Interactions inter Pharmacokinetic CAUTION - Acidic drinks such as coffee, juice and soft drinks reduce buccal nicotine absorption; avoid them for 15 minutes before and during gum use. Smoking cessation can also increase levels of some CYP1A2 substrates such as clozapine or olanzapine. Monitoring & Cautions mon Bedside MANDATORY — Teach the 'Park and Chew' method. Chew slowly until a peppery taste appears. Stop chewing. 'Park' the gum between the cheek and gum until the taste fades. Chew again. Repeat for 30 minutes. Clinical Pearls pearl The Swallowing Trap If swallowed, Nicotine enters the highly acidic stomach, becomes ionized, and cannot be absorbed into the blood. It then passes to the liver and is 100% destroyed. Swallowed nicotine provides zero craving relief and just makes the patient vomit. The Dose Confusion Patients often buy the 2mg gum because it's cheaper, even if they smoke 40 a day. The 2mg gum will fail miserably in a heavy smoker. Prescribe the 4mg. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Absorbed directly across the buccal mucosa into the systemic circulation, avoiding hepatic first-pass metabolism. Onset **Buccal** 5-10 mins (Rapidly hits the brain). Duration 30-60 mins. Sources src Source Review Source Check - Nicorette Chewing Gum Australian PI checked 2026-05-10 for indication, dosing limits, contraindications, pregnancy/lactation cautions, technique, acidic-drink interaction, and source-reviewed NRT safety wording.\nClass & Role Nicotinic Agonist — Short-acting, fast-relief Nicotine Replacement Therapy. Route / Formulation Chewing Gum (2 mg, 4 mg). (Nicorette, Nicabate) Usual Dose & Max Use 2 mg or 4 mg gum when cravings occur. Most smokers use 8-12 pieces/day of 2 mg or 4-6 pieces/day of 4 mg. Max 20 pieces/day of 2 mg or 10 pieces/day of 4 mg. Key Indication Doses Acute Cravings (> 20 cigs/day): **Buccal** Chew 4 mg piece PRN for cravings. Max 20 pieces/day. Acute Cravings (< 20 cigs/day): **Buccal** Chew 2 mg piece PRN for cravings. Max 30 pieces/day. Combination Therapy: Use PRN heavily alongside a daily 24h Nicotine Patch for maximum success rates. Best Uses Nicotine gum is a flexible on-demand NRT for smoking cessation allowing dose titration, but requires correct chewing technique (chew-and-park) and can cause jaw pain and GI upset. Avoid / Cautions CONTRAINDICATED - Do not use nicotine gum in non-tobacco users, children under 12 years, or patients with hypersensitivity to nicotine or product components. Use medical review for pregnancy/lactation, unstable cardiovascular disease, diabetes, active GI disease, severe renal/hepatic impairment, uncontrolled hyperthyroidism, phaeochromocytoma, or seizure history. Key Risks Gastrointestinal: Hiccups, severe nausea, heartburn, sore throat. (Almost exclusively caused by incorrect chewing technique leading to swallowing the nicotine). Dental: Jaw ache, damage to dental work/fillings. Key Interactions Coffee, Juice, Soft Drinks. Acidic drinks lower the pH of the mouth. Nicotine requires an alkaline pH to absorb across the cheek. If the patient drinks coffee while chewing, the absorption drops to zero. Must wait 15 mins after acidic drinks. Monitoring Teach the 'Park and Chew' method. Chew slowly until a peppery taste appears. Stop chewing. 'Park' the gum between the cheek and gum until the taste fades. Chew again. Repeat for 30 minutes. Clinical Pearl If swallowed, Nicotine enters the highly acidic stomach, becomes ionized, and cannot be absorbed into the blood. It then passes to the liver and is 100% destroyed. Swallowed nicotine provides zero craving relief and just makes the patient vomit.","sourceRole":"clinical_reference","access":"public","validationStatus":"unverified","sourceStatus":"current","sourceLineage":[],"publicationVersion":"529a6fcd2aedfb7dd41e104a2d907704694575753a4fe492fcbf012adf7d1dfa","contentHash":"21c5199e2a5158b1e4ed7978a3953383f413f34c8b540f94d4d6e1e3cfe56e98"},"renderPayload":{"slug":"nicotine-gum","name":"Nicotine gum","class":"SUD","subclass":"Nicotinic Agonist","category":"Addiction Medicine","accent":"#e11d48","tag":"NRT","schedule":"UNSCHEDULED","stats":[{"label":"Max Dose","value":"2 mg: max 20 pieces/day; 4 mg: max 10 pieces/day","cls":"hi","flag":"hi"},{"label":"Onset","value":"Minutes","cls":"","flag":""},{"label":"Technique","value":"PARK & CHEW","cls":"warn","flag":"warn"},{"label":"Combination","value":"RECOMMENDED","cls":"good","flag":""}],"sections":[{"title":"Rapid Summary","type":"summary","rows":[{"key":"Overview","val":"Short-acting, fast-relief Nicotine Replacement Therapy. Used to combat acute, breakthrough cravings. Absorbed through the buccal mucosa, NOT the stomach.","tags":[]},{"key":"Maximum Dose","val":"Max 20 pieces/day of 2 mg gum or 10 pieces/day of 4 mg gum.","tags":[]},{"key":"Clinical Focus","val":"Must be used with the 'Park and Chew' technique. Chewing it like normal chewing gum causes it to be swallowed, destroying the drug and causing severe hiccups and nausea.","tags":[]},{"key":"Bottom Line","val":"Nicotine gum is a flexible on-demand NRT for smoking cessation allowing dose titration, but requires correct chewing technique (chew-and-park) and can cause jaw pain and GI upset.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Risk Profile","type":"risk","rows":[{"key":"Gastrointestinal","val":"HIGH — Hiccups, severe nausea, heartburn, sore throat. (Almost exclusively caused by incorrect chewing technique leading to swallowing the nicotine).","tags":[]},{"key":"Dental","val":"MODERATE — Jaw ache, damage to dental work/fillings.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Dosing & Administration","type":"dose","rows":[{"key":"Acute Cravings (> 20 cigs/day)","val":"**Buccal** 4 mg gum when cravings occur. Chew slowly until taste/tingle, then park between cheek and gum; repeat for about 30 minutes. Max 10 pieces/day.","tags":[]},{"key":"Acute Cravings (< 20 cigs/day)","val":"**Buccal** 2 mg gum when cravings occur. Chew-and-park for about 30 minutes. Max 20 pieces/day.","tags":[]},{"key":"Combination Therapy","val":"Use PRN heavily alongside a daily 24h Nicotine Patch for maximum success rates.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Formulation & Access","type":"form","rows":[{"key":"Brand Names","val":"Nicorette, Nicabate","tags":[]},{"key":"Oral Routes","val":"Chewing Gum (2 mg, 4 mg).","tags":[]},{"key":"Prescribing & PBS","val":"Over The Counter (OTC).","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Indications & Use","type":"ind","rows":[{"key":"Primary","val":"Relief of acute nicotine cravings, smoking cessation adjunct.","tags":["TGA"]},{"key":"Clinical Place","val":"Provides the acute 'hit' to address sudden psychological triggers (e.g., finishing a meal, feeling stressed).","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Contraindications & Populations","type":"contra","rows":[{"key":"Absolute","val":"CONTRAINDICATED - Do not use nicotine gum in non-tobacco users, children under 12 years, or patients with hypersensitivity to nicotine or product components.","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["paediatric"],"action":"contraindication","severity":"danger","match":{"age":{"lt":12}},"note":"Existing row contraindicates this NRT product in children under 12 years; nicotine/product hypersensitivity is not modeled in the patient panel."}},{"key":"Medical review","val":"Use medical review for unstable cardiovascular disease, diabetes, active GI disease, severe renal/hepatic impairment, uncontrolled hyperthyroidism, phaeochromocytoma, or seizure history.","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["renal","hepatic"],"action":"caution","severity":"caution","match":{"hepatic":["severe"]},"note":"Existing row asks for medical review in severe renal/hepatic impairment; cardiovascular, diabetes, GI, endocrine, seizure and skin-site risks remain in row text because this panel does not model them."}},{"key":"Pregnancy","val":"CAUTION - Use during pregnancy or lactation only after risk-benefit review. Intermittent NRT may help reduce nicotine exposure timing compared with continuous forms, but smoking cessation support remains first-line.","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["pregnancy","lactation"],"action":"caution","severity":"caution","note":"NRT in pregnancy/lactation requires individual risk-benefit review; intermittent forms can reduce infant exposure timing compared with continuous dosing."}}]},{"title":"Key Interactions","type":"inter","rows":[{"key":"Pharmacokinetic","val":"CAUTION - Acidic drinks such as coffee, juice and soft drinks reduce buccal nicotine absorption; avoid them for 15 minutes before and during gum use. Smoking cessation can also increase levels of some CYP1A2 substrates such as clozapine or olanzapine.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Monitoring & Cautions","type":"mon","rows":[{"key":"Bedside","val":"MANDATORY — Teach the 'Park and Chew' method. Chew slowly until a peppery taste appears. Stop chewing. 'Park' the gum between the cheek and gum until the taste fades. Chew again. Repeat for 30 minutes.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Clinical Pearls","type":"pearl","rows":[{"key":"The Swallowing Trap","val":"If swallowed, Nicotine enters the highly acidic stomach, becomes ionized, and cannot be absorbed into the blood. It then passes to the liver and is 100% destroyed. Swallowed nicotine provides zero craving relief and just makes the patient vomit.","tags":[]},{"key":"The Dose Confusion","val":"Patients often buy the 2mg gum because it's cheaper, even if they smoke 40 a day. The 2mg gum will fail miserably in a heavy smoker. Prescribe the 4mg.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics","type":"evid","rows":[{"key":"Mechanism","val":"Absorbed directly across the buccal mucosa into the systemic circulation, avoiding hepatic first-pass metabolism.","tags":[]},{"key":"Onset","val":"**Buccal** 5-10 mins (Rapidly hits the brain).","tags":[]},{"key":"Duration","val":"30-60 mins.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Sources","type":"src","rows":[{"key":"Source Review","val":"Source Check - Nicorette Chewing Gum Australian PI checked 2026-05-10 for indication, dosing limits, contraindications, pregnancy/lactation cautions, technique, acidic-drink interaction, and source-reviewed NRT safety wording."}]}],"quick":[{"label":"Class & Role","value":"Nicotinic Agonist — Short-acting, fast-relief Nicotine Replacement Therapy."},{"label":"Route / Formulation","value":"Chewing Gum (2 mg, 4 mg). (Nicorette, Nicabate)"},{"label":"Usual Dose & Max","value":"Use 2 mg or 4 mg gum when cravings occur. Most smokers use 8-12 pieces/day of 2 mg or 4-6 pieces/day of 4 mg. Max 20 pieces/day of 2 mg or 10 pieces/day of 4 mg."},{"label":"Key Indication Doses","value":"Acute Cravings (> 20 cigs/day): **Buccal** Chew 4 mg piece PRN for cravings. Max 20 pieces/day.  Acute Cravings (< 20 cigs/day): **Buccal** Chew 2 mg piece PRN for cravings. Max 30 pieces/day.  Combination Therapy: Use PRN heavily alongside a daily 24h Nicotine Patch for maximum success rates."},{"label":"Best Uses","value":"Nicotine gum is a flexible on-demand NRT for smoking cessation allowing dose titration, but requires correct chewing technique (chew-and-park) and can cause jaw pain and GI upset."},{"label":"Avoid / Cautions","value":"CONTRAINDICATED - Do not use nicotine gum in non-tobacco users, children under 12 years, or patients with hypersensitivity to nicotine or product components. Use medical review for pregnancy/lactation, unstable cardiovascular disease, diabetes, active GI disease, severe renal/hepatic impairment, uncontrolled hyperthyroidism, phaeochromocytoma, or seizure history."},{"label":"Key Risks","value":"Gastrointestinal: Hiccups, severe nausea, heartburn, sore throat. (Almost exclusively caused by incorrect chewing technique leading to swallowing the nicotine).  Dental: Jaw ache, damage to dental work/fillings."},{"label":"Key Interactions","value":"Coffee, Juice, Soft Drinks. Acidic drinks lower the pH of the mouth. Nicotine requires an alkaline pH to absorb across the cheek. If the patient drinks coffee while chewing, the absorption drops to zero. Must wait 15 mins after acidic drinks."},{"label":"Monitoring","value":"Teach the 'Park and Chew' method. Chew slowly until a peppery taste appears. Stop chewing. 'Park' the gum between the cheek and gum until the taste fades. Chew again. Repeat for 30 minutes."},{"label":"Clinical Pearl","value":"If swallowed, Nicotine enters the highly acidic stomach, becomes ionized, and cannot be absorbed into the blood. It then passes to the liver and is 100% destroyed. Swallowed nicotine provides zero craving relief and just makes the patient vomit."}]}},{"logicalId":"medications:nicotine-inhalator","logicalDocumentId":"055bd003-703c-5331-8b62-7ae0ff5f71ce","logicalChunkId":"6f011eaf-72cc-552b-8f0d-debfecb9d02a","normalizedText":"Medication: Nicotine inhalator\nClass: SUD\nSubclass: Nicotinic Agonist\nSchedule: UNSCHEDULED\nTag: NRT\nRapid Summary summary Overview A plastic mouthpiece containing a nicotine cartridge. Satisfies the 'hand-to-mouth' behavioral addiction of smoking while delivering nicotine to the mouth (not the lungs). Maximum Dose Max **6 cartridges/day** (Each cartridge lasts for about 20-40 minutes of intense puffing). Clinical Focus It is NOT a vape or an asthma inhaler. The patient must take short, shallow 'cigar' puffs into the mouth. Deep lung inhalations will cause severe coughing and yield zero drug. Bottom Line Nicotine inhalator is an NRT mimicking the hand-to-mouth habit of smoking, but delivers less nicotine than cigarettes and requires frequent puffing throughout the day. Risk Profile risk Gastrointestinal HIGH — Mouth/throat irritation, cough, hiccups. Cardiovascular LOW — Palpitations. Dosing & Administration dose Acute Cravings / Behavioral Habit **Buccal** Take shallow puffs continuously for 20 mins when cravings occur. Usually 3-6 cartridges a day are required. Formulation & Access form Brand Names Nicorette Inhalator Oral Routes Plastic mouthpiece with replaceable 15 mg cartridges (delivers ~2-4 mg of absorbable nicotine). Prescribing & PBS Over The Counter (OTC). Indications & Use ind Primary Relief of acute nicotine cravings, replacement of the hand-to-mouth smoking ritual. Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CONTRAINDICATED - Do not use nicotine inhalator in non-tobacco users, children under 12 years, or patients with hypersensitivity to nicotine or product components. Medical review Use medical review for unstable cardiovascular disease, diabetes, active GI disease, severe renal/hepatic impairment, uncontrolled hyperthyroidism, phaeochromocytoma, or seizure history. Asthma CAUTION - Use cautiously in chronic throat disease or asthma; nicotine vapour can irritate the throat and airways. Pregnancy / Lactation CAUTION - Use during pregnancy or lactation only after risk-benefit review. If breastfeeding and intermittent NRT is needed, feed before dosing where practical to reduce infant exposure. Key Interactions inter Pharmacokinetic CAUTION - Acidic drinks can reduce oral nicotine absorption. Smoking cessation can increase levels of CYP1A2 substrates such as theophylline, clozapine, olanzapine, imipramine, clomipramine, fluvoxamine, ropinirole, and caffeine. Monitoring & Cautions mon Bedside MANDATORY — Teach the 'shallow puff' technique. If they try to inhale it like a cigarette, it will fail. Clinical Pearls pearl The Ritual Replacement Many smokers are addicted to the physical act of holding a cigarette and bringing it to their lips (the ritual). The inhalator is the ONLY medical NRT that addresses this physical behavioral habit. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Air drawn through the cartridge vapourises nicotine, which is absorbed in the mouth. It is a buccal product, not a lung-delivery vape or asthma inhaler. Onset **Buccal** 5-10 mins. Sources src Source Review Source Check - Nicorette Inhalator Australian PI checked 2026-05-10 for formulation, buccal absorption, contraindications, asthma/throat caution, renal/hepatic cautions, pregnancy/lactation, and CYP1A2 smoking-cessation interaction context.\nClass & Role Nicotinic Agonist — A plastic mouthpiece containing a nicotine cartridge. Route / Formulation Plastic mouthpiece with replaceable 15 mg cartridges (delivers ~2-4 mg of absorbable nicotine). (Nicorette Inhalator) Usual Dose & Max **Buccal** Take shallow puffs continuously for 20 mins when cravings occur. Usually 3-6 cartridges a day are required. Max **6 cartridges/day** (Each cartridge lasts for about 20-40 minutes of intense puffing). Best Uses Nicotine inhalator is an NRT mimicking the hand-to-mouth habit of smoking, but delivers less nicotine than cigarettes and requires frequent puffing throughout the day. Avoid / Cautions CONTRAINDICATED - Do not use nicotine inhalator in non-tobacco users, children under 12 years, or patients with hypersensitivity to nicotine or product components. Use medical review for pregnancy/lactation, unstable cardiovascular disease, diabetes, active GI disease, severe renal/hepatic impairment, uncontrolled hyperthyroidism, phaeochromocytoma, or seizure history. Key Risks Gastrointestinal: Mouth/throat irritation, cough, hiccups. Cardiovascular: Palpitations. Key Interactions Acidic drinks (Coffee/Juice) destroy oral absorption. Wait 15 mins. Monitoring Teach the 'shallow puff' technique. If they try to inhale it like a cigarette, it will fail. Clinical Pearl Many smokers are addicted to the physical act of holding a cigarette and bringing it to their lips (the ritual). The inhalator is the ONLY medical NRT that addresses this physical behavioral habit.","contentHash":"99408a29fd2dd488c6b1c87bcb77bb5bb79e2cb3483f23064e1df93443fd38db","publicationFingerprint":"592e1ae67304db268fcfe323fd86ce7e153f322f48be61d23ed66d1fc2fc60ac","governanceFingerprint":"6473895da16d5508d03ffea998bc94f9de73dd719d70d551126b969e94bd3eab","lineageFingerprint":"4f53cda18c2baa0c0354bb5f9a3ecbe5ed12ab4d8e11ba873c2f11161202b945","publicMetadataFingerprint":"c30083aa763bb9114ebf3bd0cd80ae31ff2f23eba07c66bf81e0dabf0e80d2d2","record":{"version":"site-content-record-v1","logicalId":"medications:nicotine-inhalator","producerClass":"dynamic_registry","domain":"medications","route":"/medications/nicotine-inhalator","title":"Nicotine inhalator","body":"Medication: Nicotine inhalator\nClass: SUD\nSubclass: Nicotinic Agonist\nSchedule: UNSCHEDULED\nTag: NRT\nRapid Summary summary Overview A plastic mouthpiece containing a nicotine cartridge. Satisfies the 'hand-to-mouth' behavioral addiction of smoking while delivering nicotine to the mouth (not the lungs). Maximum Dose Max **6 cartridges/day** (Each cartridge lasts for about 20-40 minutes of intense puffing). Clinical Focus It is NOT a vape or an asthma inhaler. The patient must take short, shallow 'cigar' puffs into the mouth. Deep lung inhalations will cause severe coughing and yield zero drug. Bottom Line Nicotine inhalator is an NRT mimicking the hand-to-mouth habit of smoking, but delivers less nicotine than cigarettes and requires frequent puffing throughout the day. Risk Profile risk Gastrointestinal HIGH — Mouth/throat irritation, cough, hiccups. Cardiovascular LOW — Palpitations. Dosing & Administration dose Acute Cravings / Behavioral Habit **Buccal** Take shallow puffs continuously for 20 mins when cravings occur. Usually 3-6 cartridges a day are required. Formulation & Access form Brand Names Nicorette Inhalator Oral Routes Plastic mouthpiece with replaceable 15 mg cartridges (delivers 