leans dentures thoroughly to prevent re-infection. Instruct them to NOT eat or drink for 30 mins after swishing the drops. Clinical Pearls pearl The Swish and Swallow Nystatin only works by contact. If a patient immediately gulps the liquid down, it will completely fail to cure their mouth thrush. It must be held in the mouth and swished over the plaques. The Esophageal Failure If a patient complains of pain *when swallowing* (odynophagia), the thrush has moved into their esophagus. Nystatin liquid washes past the esophagus too fast to kill it. You MUST switch to systemic oral Fluconazole. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Binds strongly to ergosterol in the fungal cell membrane, forming pores/channels. Potassium and essential cellular components leak out, causing instant fungal cell death. Onset Rapid local action. Half-life Not applicable. 100% of the oral dose is excreted unchanged in the feces. Sources src Source Review Source Check - MYCOSTATIN nystatin ARTG/PI and PBS search checked 2026-05-13 for high-risk opioid/general batch appraisal: identity/access, indication, dosing, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, pregnancy/lactation, patient-context metadata, and source-row status.\nClass & Role Polyene — Highly toxic polyene antifungal that is completely un-absorbable by the human gut. Route / Formulation Oral drops/suspension (100,000 U/mL). Capsules (500,000 U). (Nilstat, Mycostatin) Usual Dose & Max **PO** 1 mL (100,000 Units) **QID**. Swish the liquid around the mouth for as long as possible (minutes) before swallowing. Max **4 Million Units/day** (e.g., 1 mL (100,000 U) QID for oral drops). Key Indication Doses Oral Candidiasis (Thrush): **PO** 1 mL (100,000 Units) **QID**. Swish the liquid around the mouth for as long as possible (minutes) before swallowing. Intestinal Candidiasis: **PO** 500,000 to 1,000,000 Units (Tablets) **TDS** to **QID**. Cutaneous Thrush: **Topical** Apply cream/ointment to affected skin (e.g., beneath breasts, groin) **BD** to **QID**. Best Uses Nystatin is a topical/oral antifungal for mucocutaneous candidiasis with no systemic absorption, but must be held in the mouth as long as possible for oral thrush and has no systemic antifungal activity. Avoid / Cautions NONE — Extremely safe due to lack of systemic absorption. **Pregnancy** Category A. Safe for breastfeeding infants (used commonly for neonatal oral thrush). Key Risks Gastrointestinal: Mild nausea, vomiting, diarrhea (only at massive oral doses). Systemic: Because it cannot enter the bloodstream, it has zero liver, cardiac, or renal toxicity. Key Interactions NONE — No systemic drug interactions. Monitoring Ensure patient removes dentures before using oral drops, and cleans dentures thoroughly to prevent re-infection. Instruct them to NOT eat or drink for 30 mins after swishing the drops. Clinical Pearl Nystatin only works by contact. If a patient immediately gulps the liquid down, it will completely fail to cure their mouth thrush. It must be held in the mouth and swished over the plaques.","sourceRole":"clinical_reference","access":"public","validationStatus":"unverified","sourceStatus":"current","sourceLineage":[],"publicationVersion":"df2c8763e8bc0e252791cdfb7d8af27035ca09ee67518659d93d6186f94b7b2c","contentHash":"e4ab5388a759f5a50fa6e6e80d95626006901fb5aebf5a611b770466fc0529f1"},"renderPayload":{"slug":"nystatin","name":"Nystatin","class":"Antifungal","subclass":"Polyene","category":"Infectious Diseases - Antifungals","accent":"#ea580c","tag":"ANTIFUNGAL","schedule":"S4","stats":[{"label":"Max Dose","value":"4 Million U/day","cls":"hi","flag":"hi"},{"label":"Half-life","value":"Not Absorbed","cls":"","flag":""},{"label":"Route","value":"TOPICAL/PO","cls":"purple","flag":""},{"label":"Systemic Abs.","value":"ZERO","cls":"good","flag":""}],"sections":[{"title":"Rapid Summary","type":"summary","rows":[{"key":"Overview","val":"Highly toxic polyene antifungal that is completely un-absorbable by the human gut. Acts as a brilliant, safe topical/luminal agent to kill Candida (Thrush) on contact.","tags":[]},{"key":"Maximum Dose","val":"Max **4 Million Units/day** (e.g., 1 mL (100,000 U) QID for oral drops).","tags":[]},{"key":"Clinical Focus","val":"Must physically touch the fungus to kill it. Swallowing it like a normal pill does absolutely nothing for mouth ulcers.","tags":[]},{"key":"Bottom Line","val":"Nystatin is a topical/oral antifungal for mucocutaneous candidiasis with no systemic absorption, but must be held in the mouth as long as possible for oral thrush and has no systemic antifungal activity.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Risk Profile","type":"risk","rows":[{"key":"Gastrointestinal","val":"LOW — Mild nausea, vomiting, diarrhea (only at massive oral doses).","tags":[]},{"key":"Systemic","val":"SAFE — Because it cannot enter the bloodstream, it has zero liver, cardiac, or renal toxicity.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Dosing & Administration","type":"dose","rows":[{"key":"Oral Candidiasis (Thrush)","val":"**PO** 1 mL (100,000 Units) **QID**. Swish the liquid around the mouth for as long as possible (minutes) before swallowing.","tags":[]},{"key":"Intestinal Candidiasis","val":"**PO** 500,000 to 1,000,000 Units (Tablets) **TDS** to **QID**.","tags":[]},{"key":"Cutaneous Thrush","val":"**Topical** Apply cream/ointment to affected skin (e.g., beneath breasts, groin) **BD** to **QID**.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Formulation & Access","type":"form","rows":[{"key":"Brand Names","val":"Nilstat, Mycostatin","tags":[]},{"key":"Oral Routes","val":"Oral drops/suspension (100,000 U/mL). Capsules (500,000 U).","tags":[]},{"key":"Topical","val":"Cream, Ointment (100,000 U/g).","tags":[]},{"key":"Prescribing & PBS","val":"Pharmacy Medicine (S2/S3) for topical/drops. S4 for capsules.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Indications & Use","type":"ind","rows":[{"key":"Primary","val":"Treatment and prophylaxis of oral, intestinal, and cutaneous Candida albicans infections.","tags":["PBS","TGA"]},{"key":"Clinical Place","val":"First-line for simple oral thrush (e.g., secondary to steroid inhaler use). Replaced by Fluconazole if infection is severe or extends down the esophagus.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Contraindications & Populations","type":"contra","rows":[{"key":"Absolute","val":"NONE — Extremely safe due to lack of systemic absorption.","tags":[]},{"key":"Pregnancy","val":"SAFE — **Pregnancy** Category A. Minimal systemic absorption; compatible with breastfeeding and paediatric/neonatal oral thrush use when clinically indicated.","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["pregnancy","lactation","paediatric"],"action":"info","severity":"info","match":{"age":{"lt":18}},"note":"Nystatin pregnancy/lactation/paediatric row is source-reviewed safe-use context, not a contraindication alert."}}]},{"title":"Key Interactions","type":"inter","rows":[{"key":"Pharmacodynamic","val":"NONE — No systemic drug interactions.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Monitoring & Cautions","type":"mon","rows":[{"key":"Bedside","val":"MANDATORY — Ensure patient removes dentures before using oral drops, and cleans dentures thoroughly to prevent re-infection. Instruct them to NOT eat or drink for 30 mins after swishing the drops.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Clinical Pearls","type":"pearl","rows":[{"key":"The Swish and Swallow","val":"Nystatin only works by contact. If a patient immediately gulps the liquid down, it will completely fail to cure their mouth thrush. It must be held in the mouth and swished over the plaques.","tags":[]},{"key":"The Esophageal Failure","val":"If a patient complains of pain *when swallowing* (odynophagia), the thrush has moved into their esophagus. Nystatin liquid washes past the esophagus too fast to kill it. You MUST switch to systemic oral Fluconazole.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics","type":"evid","rows":[{"key":"Mechanism","val":"Binds strongly to ergosterol in the fungal cell membrane, forming pores/channels. Potassium and essential cellular components leak out, causing instant fungal cell death.","tags":[]},{"key":"Onset","val":"Rapid local action.","tags":[]},{"key":"Half-life","val":"Not applicable. 100% of the oral dose is excreted unchanged in the feces.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Sources","type":"src","rows":[{"key":"Source Review","val":"Source Check - MYCOSTATIN nystatin ARTG/PI and PBS search checked 2026-05-13 for high-risk opioid/general batch appraisal: identity/access, indication, dosing, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, pregnancy/lactation, patient-context metadata, and source-row status."}]}],"quick":[{"label":"Class & Role","value":"Polyene — Highly toxic polyene antifungal that is completely un-absorbable by the human gut."},{"label":"Route / Formulation","value":"Oral drops/suspension (100,000 U/mL). Capsules (500,000 U). (Nilstat, Mycostatin)"},{"label":"Usual Dose & Max","value":"**PO** 1 mL (100,000 Units) **QID**. Swish the liquid around the mouth for as long as possible (minutes) before swallowing. Max **4 Million Units/day** (e.g., 1 mL (100,000 U) QID for oral drops)."},{"label":"Key Indication Doses","value":"Oral Candidiasis (Thrush): **PO** 1 mL (100,000 Units) **QID**. Swish the liquid around the mouth for as long as possible (minutes) before swallowing.  Intestinal Candidiasis: **PO** 500,000 to 1,000,000 Units (Tablets) **TDS** to **QID**.  Cutaneous Thrush: **Topical** Apply cream/ointment to affected skin (e.g., beneath breasts, groin) **BD** to **QID**."},{"label":"Best Uses","value":"Nystatin is a topical/oral antifungal for mucocutaneous candidiasis with no systemic absorption, but must be held in the mouth as long as possible for oral thrush and has no systemic antifungal activity."},{"label":"Avoid / Cautions","value":"NONE — Extremely safe due to lack of systemic absorption. **Pregnancy** Category A. Safe for breastfeeding infants (used commonly for neonatal oral thrush)."},{"label":"Key Risks","value":"Gastrointestinal: Mild nausea, vomiting, diarrhea (only at massive oral doses).  Systemic: Because it cannot enter the bloodstream, it has zero liver, cardiac, or renal toxicity."},{"label":"Key Interactions","value":"NONE — No systemic drug interactions."},{"label":"Monitoring","value":"Ensure patient removes dentures before using oral drops, and cleans dentures thoroughly to prevent re-infection. Instruct them to NOT eat or drink for 30 mins after swishing the drops."},{"label":"Clinical Pearl","value":"Nystatin only works by contact. If a patient immediately gulps the liquid down, it will completely fail to cure their mouth thrush. It must be held in the mouth and swished over the plaques."}]}},{"logicalId":"medications:olanzapine-pamoate-lai","logicalDocumentId":"05fbb804-3579-5f91-8304-f2602e1cd469","logicalChunkId":"42bc7f04-069f-5d6d-ab66-7cbf93c20a48","normalizedText":"Medication: Olanzapine pamoate LAI\nClass: LAI Antipsychotic\nSubclass: Zyprexa Relprevv\nSchedule: S4\nTag: DEPOT Q2-4W\nRapid Summary summary Overview A depot formulation of Olanzapine. Exceptional efficacy, but restricted by one of the most intense and dangerous post-injection monitoring protocols in modern psychiatry. Maximum Dose Max **300 mg** every 2 weeks or **405 mg** every 4 weeks. Clinical Focus Can cause Post-Injection Delirium/Sedation Syndrome (PDSS), where the drug accidentally hits a blood vessel, inducing a sudden, profound coma. Bottom Line Olanzapine pamoate LAI is a long-acting injectable olanzapine for treatment-resistant schizophrenia, but carries unique post-injection delirium/sedation syndrome (PDSS) requiring 3-hour observation. Risk Profile risk Neurological CRITICAL — Post-Injection Delirium/Sedation Syndrome (PDSS). Presents within 1-3 hours of injection as severe ataxia, delirium, excessive sedation, and coma. Caused by accidental intravascular entry of the suspension. Metabolic CRITICAL — Massive weight gain, new-onset diabetes, extreme hyperlipidemia. Gastrointestinal HIGH — Constipation, dry mouth. Dosing & Administration dose Schizophrenia Maintenance **IM** 150-300 mg every 2 weeks, or 300-405 mg every 4 weeks. Deep gluteal injection ONLY. Initiation No oral overlap is strictly required if target depot doses are used, though 1-2 days of oral is often given to confirm tolerance. Formulation & Access form Brand Names Zyprexa Relprevv Parenteral Powder for reconstitution. Forms a thick yellow suspension. Prescribing & PBS Authority Required (PBS). Must be administered in a registered healthcare facility with resuscitation equipment. Indications & Use ind Primary Maintenance treatment of Schizophrenia. Clinical Place Reserved for severely non-compliant patients who respond brilliantly to oral olanzapine but fail on other depots. The strict monitoring limits its use in the community. Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — Unmonitored administration environments. Severe CNS depression. Acute narrow-angle glaucoma. Pregnancy CAUTION — **Pregnancy** Category C. Key Interactions inter Pharmacodynamic CRITICAL — Benzodiazepines/Opioids (Additive severe CNS depression). Pharmacokinetic HIGH — Smoking induces CYP1A2, accelerating olanzapine clearance. Monitoring & Cautions mon Bedside MANDATORY — The patient MUST remain in the clinic/ward and be physically observed by a nurse every 30 minutes for exactly 3 HOURS post-injection. They cannot leave early. They must not drive home. Laboratory Strict routine **BSL**, **HbA1c**, **Lipids**, and **Weight**. Clinical Pearls pearl The Aspiration Check When injecting, you MUST aspirate the syringe for 5-10 seconds before pushing the plunger. If ANY blood appears, you must withdraw and start again. If the pamoate salt hits a vein, it dissolves instantly, hitting the brain with 300mg of Olanzapine at once, causing a PDSS coma. The Gluteal Rule Must ONLY be injected into the gluteal muscle. Deltoid injections are strictly prohibited due to muscle size and vascularity risks. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Olanzapine pamoate salt dissolves extremely slowly in muscle tissue. Broad receptor antagonist (D2, 5-HT2A, H1, M1). Onset Days. Peak concentration occurs within 1 week. Half-life 30 Days. Sources src Source Review Source Check - ZYPREXA RELPREVV ARTG/PI and PBS search checked 2026-05-10 for antipsychotic/LAI batch appraisal: identity/access, indication, dosing, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and source-row status.\nClass & Role Zyprexa Relprevv — A depot formulation of Olanzapine. Route / Formulation Powder for reconstitution. Forms a thick yellow suspension. (Zyprexa Relprevv) Usual Dose & Max **IM** 150-300 mg every 2 weeks, or 300-405 mg every 4 weeks. Deep gluteal injection ONLY. Max **300 mg** every 2 weeks or **405 mg** every 4 weeks. Key Indication Doses Schizophrenia Maintenance: **IM** 150-300 mg every 2 weeks, or 300-405 mg every 4 weeks. Deep gluteal injection ONLY. Initiation: No oral overlap is strictly required if target depot doses are used, though 1-2 days of oral is often given to confirm tolerance. Best Uses Olanzapine pamoate LAI is a long-acting injectable olanzapine for treatment-resistant schizophrenia, but carries unique post-injection delirium/sedation syndrome (PDSS) requiring 3-hour observation. Avoid / Cautions Unmonitored administration environments. Severe CNS depression. Acute narrow-angle glaucoma. **Pregnancy** Category C. Key Risks Neurological: Post-Injection Delirium/Sedation Syndrome (PDSS). Presents within 1-3 hours of injection as severe ataxia, delirium, excessive sedation, and coma. Caused by accidental intravascular entry of the suspension. Metabolic: Massive weight gain, new-onset diabetes, extreme hyperlipidemia. Gastrointestinal: Constipation, dry mouth. Key Interactions Benzodiazepines/Opioids (Additive severe CNS depression). Smoking induces CYP1A2, accelerating olanzapine clearance. Monitoring The patient MUST remain in the clinic/ward and be physically observed by a nurse every 30 minutes for exactly 3 HOURS post-injection. They cannot leave early. They must not drive home. Strict routine **BSL**, **HbA1c**, **Lipids**, and **Weight**. Clinical Pearl When injecting, you MUST aspirate the syringe for 5-10 seconds before pushing the plunger. If ANY blood appears, you must withdraw and start again. If the pamoate salt hits a vein, it dissolves instantly, hitting the brain with 300mg of Olanzapine at once, causing a PDSS coma.","contentHash":"c47e57bf401d6d8d757e2d699b7ec1506f50860d52927d353421423078de1886","publicationFingerprint":"3503cbed54e5a3d7295d77dbe5469a2d45a4c13320b9273e90d6343e2343d248","governanceFingerprint":"6473895da16d5508d03ffea998bc94f9de73dd719d70d551126b969e94bd3eab","lineageFingerprint":"4f53cda18c2baa0c0354bb5f9a3ecbe5ed12ab4d8e11ba873c2f11161202b945","publicMetadataFingerprint":"bcb4a1d9581699a510d339a8f44165f68eaefbfb4733a2feb88c61facb4bfed1","record":{"version":"site-content-record-v1","logicalId":"medications:olanzapine-pamoate-lai","producerClass":"dynamic_registry","domain":"medications","route":"/medications/olanzapine-pamoate-lai","title":"Olanzapine pamoate LAI","body":"Medication: Olanzapine pamoate LAI\nClass: LAI Antipsychotic\nSubclass: Zyprexa Relprevv\nSchedule: S4\nTag: DEPOT Q2-4W\nRapid Summary summary Overview A depot formulation of Olanzapine. Exceptional efficacy, but restricted by one of the most intense and dangerous post-injection monitoring protocols in modern psychiatry. Maximum Dose Max **300 mg** every 2 weeks or **405 mg** every 4 weeks. Clinical Focus Can cause Post-Injection Delirium/Sedation Syndrome (PDSS), where the drug accidentally hits a blood vessel, inducing a sudden, profound coma. Bottom Line Olanzapine pamoate LAI is a long-acting injectable olanzapine for treatment-resistant schizophrenia, but carries unique post-injection delirium/sedation syndrome (PDSS) requiring 3-hour observation. Risk Profile risk Neurological CRITICAL — Post-Injection Delirium/Sedation Syndrome (PDSS). Present