 a scoop of Osmolax into their morning cup of tea is completely undetectable and guarantees they get their laxative.","sourceRole":"clinical_reference","access":"public","validationStatus":"unverified","sourceStatus":"current","sourceLineage":[],"publicationVersion":"e2ba364b0dc0ec002a8dec3b017acad9713982aea32d7323839b166543086279","contentHash":"f72b8023c4f478236195dece8291eac8c839a4390a4ea60d2740022b0ea33dcf"},"renderPayload":{"slug":"osmolax","name":"Osmolax","class":"Aperient","subclass":"Osmotic Laxative","category":"Gastrointestinal - Laxatives","accent":"#b45309","tag":"OSMOTIC","schedule":"UNSCHEDULED","stats":[{"label":"Max Dose","value":"3-4 scoops/day","cls":"hi","flag":"hi"},{"label":"Onset","value":"1-2 Days","cls":"good","flag":""},{"label":"Electrolytes","value":"NONE","cls":"warn","flag":""},{"label":"Taste","value":"TASTELESS","cls":"good","flag":""}],"sections":[{"title":"Rapid Summary","type":"summary","rows":[{"key":"Overview","val":"Macrogol 3350 (PEG) WITHOUT added electrolytes. Acts exactly like Movicol, but is completely tasteless and can be mixed into any hot or cold drink.","tags":[]},{"key":"Maximum Dose","val":"Max **3-4 scoops/day** (Adults).","tags":[]},{"key":"Clinical Focus","val":"Provides the same excellent laxative effect as Movicol, massively improving patient compliance because it doesn't taste like salty lemon water.","tags":[]},{"key":"Bottom Line","val":"Osmolax is an unflavoured macrogol osmotic laxative for constipation, but provides no electrolyte replacement unlike Movicol and requires adequate fluid intake.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Risk Profile","type":"risk","rows":[{"key":"Gastrointestinal","val":"MODERATE — Bloating, flatulence, nausea.","tags":[]},{"key":"Metabolic","val":"MODERATE — Because it lacks the protective electrolytes of Movicol, massive doses *could* theoretically cause minor electrolyte shifts, but this is clinically rare at standard doses.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Dosing & Administration","type":"dose","rows":[{"key":"Constipation (Adults)","val":"**PO** 1-2 scoops (17-34g) **OD**. Mix with 200 mL of any hot/cold liquid (water, juice, coffee).","tags":[]},{"key":"Constipation (Paediatrics)","val":"Strictly weight-based (e.g., 1 scoop daily for children > 2 years).","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Formulation & Access","type":"form","rows":[{"key":"Brand Names","val":"OsmoLax","tags":[]},{"key":"Oral Routes","val":"Tasteless, odorless white powder (Macrogol 3350 pure).","tags":[]},{"key":"Prescribing & PBS","val":"Not Scheduled; healthdirect/PBS data verify PBS availability for listed Osmolax packs.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Indications & Use","type":"ind","rows":[{"key":"Primary","val":"Treatment of chronic constipation.","tags":["TGA"]},{"key":"Clinical Place","val":"Highly favored in pediatrics or for picky adult/dementia patients who refuse to drink salty Movicol.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Contraindications & Populations","type":"contra","rows":[{"key":"Absolute","val":"CRITICAL — Bowel obstruction, perforated gut.","tags":[]},{"key":"Pregnancy","val":"SAFE — **Pregnancy** Category B1.","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["pregnancy"],"action":"contraindication","severity":"danger","note":"Inferred from existing decision-section wording; review before clinical use."}}]},{"title":"Key Interactions","type":"inter","rows":[{"key":"Pharmacokinetic","val":"LOW — May reduce transit time of other drugs.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Monitoring & Cautions","type":"mon","rows":[{"key":"Bedside","val":"Monitor bowel actions and ensure adequate fluid intake. Each level scoop is mixed with about 240 mL water; use caution where fluid intake is restricted or unsafe.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Clinical Pearls","type":"pearl","rows":[{"key":"The Compliance Winner","val":"For dementia patients who spit out Movicol, stirring a scoop of Osmolax into their morning cup of tea is completely undetectable and guarantees they get their laxative.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics","type":"evid","rows":[{"key":"Mechanism","val":"Pure PEG 3350. Osmotically holds water in the colon to soften stool and trigger peristalsis.","tags":[]},{"key":"Onset","val":"1-2 Days.","tags":[]},{"key":"Half-life","val":"Not absorbed. Excreted in feces.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Sources","type":"src","rows":[{"key":"Source Review","val":"Source-backed review 2026-05-12: OSMOLAX/macrogol 3350 healthdirect, PBS, and Australian constipation guideline source pack. Entry checked for dosing, access, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and app structure."}]}],"quick":[{"label":"Class & Role","value":"Osmotic Laxative — Macrogol 3350 (PEG) WITHOUT added electrolytes."},{"label":"Route / Formulation","value":"Tasteless, odorless white powder (Macrogol 3350 pure). (OsmoLax)"},{"label":"Usual Dose & Max","value":"**PO** 1-2 scoops (17-34g) **OD**. Mix with 200 mL of any hot/cold liquid (water, juice, coffee). Max **3-4 scoops/day** (Adults)."},{"label":"Key Indication Doses","value":"Constipation (Adults): **PO** 1-2 scoops (17-34g) **OD**. Mix with 200 mL of any hot/cold liquid (water, juice, coffee).  Constipation (Paediatrics): Strictly weight-based (e.g., 1 scoop daily for children > 2 years)."},{"label":"Best Uses","value":"Osmolax is an unflavoured macrogol osmotic laxative for constipation, but provides no electrolyte replacement unlike Movicol and requires adequate fluid intake."},{"label":"Avoid / Cautions","value":"Bowel obstruction, perforated gut. **Pregnancy** Category B1."},{"label":"Key Risks","value":"Gastrointestinal: Bloating, flatulence, nausea.  Metabolic: Because it lacks the protective electrolytes of Movicol, massive doses *could* theoretically cause minor electrolyte shifts, but this is clinically rare at standard doses."},{"label":"Key Interactions","value":"May reduce transit time of other drugs."},{"label":"Monitoring","value":"Monitor bowel actions and fluid tolerance. Each level scoop is mixed with about 240 mL water; use caution with fluid restriction, dysphagia, or thickened-fluid requirements."},{"label":"Clinical Pearl","value":"For dementia patients who spit out Movicol, stirring a scoop of Osmolax into their morning cup of tea is completely undetectable and guarantees they get their laxative."}]}},{"logicalId":"medications:oxazepam","logicalDocumentId":"7ef00932-f4e4-53b5-817c-a23e6aea13f1","logicalChunkId":"d8913e88-e54c-5f90-aac9-c87b1223abf1","normalizedText":"Medication: Oxazepam\nClass: Benzodiazepine\nSubclass: Short-acting\nSchedule: S4\nTag: BENZO\nRapid Summary summary Overview Short-acting benzodiazepine. Does not require hepatic Phase 1 metabolism. It is the safest, most predictable oral benzo for the elderly and patients with liver cirrhosis. Maximum Dose Max **120 mg/day** (Typically 15-30 mg per dose). Clinical Focus Has a very slow onset of action. It produces zero 'rush' or euphoria, making it excellent for preventing abuse but terrible for acute panic attacks. Bottom Line Oxazepam is a short-acting benzodiazepine preferred in elderly and hepatic impairment (no active metabolites), but has slower onset than other BZDs and still carries dependence risk. Risk Profile risk Neurological MODERATE — Sedation, falls risk, ataxia, confusion. (Much lower risk of multi-day 'hangover' compared to diazepam due to lack of metabolites). Respiratory MODERATE — Respiratory depression (if mixed with opioids). Dosing & Administration dose Anxiety **PO** 15-30 mg **TDS** to **QID**. Insomnia **PO** 15-30 mg **NOCTE** (Take 1 hour before bed due to slow onset). Alcohol Withdrawal (in Cirrhosis) **PO** 15-30 mg q2h PRN based on CIWA-Ar scale. Elderly / Frail Start at 7.5 mg (Half a 15mg tablet). Formulation & Access form Brand Names Serepax, Murelax Oral Routes Tablets (15 mg, 30 mg). Prescribing & PBS Unrestricted General Benefit (S4). Indications & Use ind Primary Short-term anxiety, insomnia, alcohol withdrawal in hepatic impairment. Clinical Place The workhorse anxiolytic for the geriatric ward. Displaces Diazepam entirely in patients > 65 years. Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — Severe respiratory failure, Myasthenia Gravis. Hepatic Impairment SAFE — The drug of choice for anxiety/withdrawal in severe liver failure (alongside Lorazepam). Pregnancy CAUTION — **Pregnancy** Category C. Key Interactions inter Pharmacodynamic CRITICAL — Opioids, Alcohol (Fatal respiratory arrest). Pharmacokinetic LOW — Impervious to CYP450 drug interactions. Safe to mix with Clarithromycin or Amiodarone. Monitoring & Cautions mon Bedside MANDATORY — Assess falls risk in the elderly. Laboratory No specific routine laboratory tests required. Clinical Pearls pearl The Abuse Deterrent Addicts rarely seek Oxazepam. Because it is highly water-soluble, it trickles into the brain slowly over 2 hours. It provides zero dopamine 'rush' or 'hit'. It simply smooths out the anxiety smoothly and safely. The Cirrhosis Rescue If an alcoholic with yellow eyes, ascites, and liver cirrhosis goes into DTs (Delirium Tremens), Diazepam will kill them or put them in a coma for a month. You MUST use Oxazepam. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Positive allosteric modulator of GABA-A. Highly water-soluble compared to diazepam, meaning it crosses the blood-brain barrier slowly. Onset **PO** 60-120 mins (Very slow). Duration 6-8 h. Half-life 4-15 hours. Glucuronidated directly (No active metabolites). Sources src Source Review Source Check - SEREPAX ARTG/PI and PBS search checked 2026-05-12 for sedative/acute-calming batch appraisal: identity/access, indication, dosing, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, pregnancy/lactation, patient-context metadata, and source-row status.\nClass & Role Short-acting — Short-acting benzodiazepine. Route / Formulation Tablets (15 mg, 30 mg). (Serepax, Murelax) Usual Dose & Max **PO** 15-30 mg **TDS** to **QID**. Max **120 mg/day** (Typically 15-30 mg per dose). Key Indication Doses Anxiety: **PO** 15-30 mg **TDS** to **QID**. Insomnia: **PO** 15-30 mg **NOCTE** (Take 1 hour before bed due to slow onset). Alcohol Withdrawal (in Cirrhosis): **PO** 15-30 mg q2h PRN based on CIWA-Ar scale. Elderly / Frail: Start at 7.5 mg (Half a 15mg tablet). Best Uses Oxazepam is a short-acting benzodiazepine preferred in elderly and hepatic impairment (no active metabolites), but has slower onset than other BZDs and still carries dependence risk. Avoid / Cautions Severe respiratory failure, Myasthenia Gravis. **Pregnancy** Category C. Key Risks Neurological: Sedation, falls risk, ataxia, confusion. (Much lower risk of multi-day 'hangover' compared to diazepam due to lack of metabolites). Respiratory: Respiratory depression (if mixed with opioids). Key Interactions Opioids, Alcohol (Fatal respiratory arrest). Impervious to CYP450 drug interactions. Safe to mix with Clarithromycin or Amiodarone. Monitoring Assess falls risk in the elderly. No specific routine laboratory tests required. Clinical Pearl Addicts rarely seek Oxazepam. Because it is highly water-soluble, it trickles into the brain slowly over 2 hours. It provides zero dopamine 'rush' or 'hit'. It simply smooths out the anxiety smoothly and safely.","contentHash":"8c9d5e698fabb5cd7dd3fb053077f852aecbf860373219dc15f4cc5210e44a57","publicationFingerprint":"8d4b5edd1658c63b2fe4d8c579783259cbb4cde43be2232c171fec5aa33025ca","governanceFingerprint":"6473895da16d5508d03ffea998bc94f9de73dd719d70d551126b969e94bd3eab","lineageFingerprint":"4f53cda18c2baa0c0354bb5f9a3ecbe5ed12ab4d8e11ba873c2f11161202b945","publicMetadataFingerprint":"d0f9fb1d445a436c637150e7f95bd97697c52a845a58f36db64224e027c68ebe","record":{"version":"site-content-record-v1","logicalId":"medications:oxazepam","producerClass":"dynamic_registry","domain":"medications","route":"/medications/oxazepam","title":"Oxazepam","body":"Medication: Oxazepam\nClass: Benzodiazepine\nSubclass: Short-acting\nSchedule: S4\nTag: BENZO\nRapid Summary summary Overview Short-acting