le glaucoma, severe cardiac failure.","tags":[]},{"key":"Elderly","val":"CRITICAL — High risk of falls, stroke, and mortality in dementia-related psychosis.","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["elderly"],"action":"contraindication","severity":"danger","match":{"age":{}},"note":"Inferred from existing decision-section wording; review before clinical use."}}]},{"title":"Key Interactions","type":"inter","rows":[{"key":"Pharmacodynamic","val":"CRITICAL — Additive hypotension and sedation with antihypertensives, Opioids, and Benzos.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Monitoring & Cautions","type":"mon","rows":[{"key":"Bedside","val":"MANDATORY — Monitor lying and standing **BP**. Implement falls precautions.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Clinical Pearls","type":"pearl","rows":[{"key":"The PRN Trap","val":"Because it is so sedating, doctors often write Periciazine 10mg PRN for sleep. In the elderly, this causes massive morning hypotension, anticholinergic confusion, and fractured hips. Use a safer alternative (like low-dose Mirtazapine or Temazepam) if possible.","tags":[]},{"key":"The Liquid Burn","val":"The oral drops are highly concentrated and can cause contact dermatitis if spilled on the nurse's hands. Always dilute the drops in water/juice before giving to the patient to prevent throat irritation.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics","type":"evid","rows":[{"key":"Mechanism","val":"Low-affinity D2 antagonist. Very high affinity for H1 (histamine) and Alpha-1 receptors, driving its intense sedating and hypotensive profile.","tags":[]},{"key":"Onset","val":"**PO** 30-60 mins.","tags":[]},{"key":"Half-life","val":"10-20 hours. Hepatically metabolised.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Sources","type":"src","rows":[{"key":"Source Review","val":"Source Check - NEULACTIL ARTG/PI and PBS search checked 2026-05-10 for antipsychotic/LAI batch appraisal: identity/access, indication, dosing, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and source-row status."}]}],"quick":[{"label":"Class & Role","value":"FGA / Phenothiazine — Low-potency phenothiazine."},{"label":"Route / Formulation","value":"Tablets (2.5 mg, 10 mg). Oral liquid drops (10 mg/mL). (Neulactil)"},{"label":"Usual Dose & Max","value":"**PO** 10-20 mg **BD** to **TDS**. Max 75 mg/day."},{"label":"Key Indication Doses","value":"Severe Behavioral Disturbance / Aggression: **PO** 10-20 mg **BD** to **TDS**. Max 75 mg/day.  Agitation (Elderly): Start at 2.5 mg. Increase to max 10 mg/day strictly under supervision.  Insomnia (Off-Label): **PO** 2.5-10 mg **NOCTE**."},{"label":"Best Uses","value":"Periciazine is a low-potency phenothiazine antipsychotic used for behavioural disturbance, but causes significant sedation and anticholinergic effects with limited antipsychotic efficacy."},{"label":"Avoid / Cautions","value":"Severe CNS depression, narrow-angle glaucoma, severe cardiac failure."},{"label":"Key Risks","value":"Neurological: Profound sedation, hangover, EPS (less than high-potency FGAs like Haldol, but still present).  Cardiovascular: Orthostatic hypotension, tachycardia, **QTc** prolongation.  Gastrointestinal: Dry mouth, constipation (Anticholinergic toxidrome)."},{"label":"Key Interactions","value":"Additive hypotension and sedation with antihypertensives, Opioids, and Benzos."},{"label":"Monitoring","value":"Monitor lying and standing **BP**. Implement falls precautions."},{"label":"Clinical Pearl","value":"Because it is so sedating, doctors often write Periciazine 10mg PRN for sleep. In the elderly, this causes massive morning hypotension, anticholinergic confusion, and fractured hips. Use a safer alternative (like low-dose Mirtazapine or Temazepam) if possible."}]}},{"logicalId":"medications:perindopril","logicalDocumentId":"d829d747-4afb-5bd3-bba8-0e9edcc706c1","logicalChunkId":"6e22b3ce-85af-50c1-bb8a-099de27298e3","normalizedText":"Medication: Perindopril\nClass: Antihypertensive\nSubclass: ACE Inhibitor\nSchedule: S4\nTag: ACEi\nRapid Summary summary Overview Long-acting Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE) inhibitor. The absolute gold-standard foundational therapy for hypertension, heart failure, and diabetic nephropathy. Maximum Dose Max **10 mg/day**. Reduce in Renal impairment — Max 2.5 mg/day if **eGFR** < 30. Clinical Focus Absolutely contraindicated in Pregnancy (Category D). Causes severe fetal renal agenesis and skull hypoplasia. Cease immediately on confirmed pregnancy. Bottom Line Perindopril is the preferred ACE inhibitor for most cardiovascular and renal-protective indications, but must never be used in Pregnancy and always requires **U&E** monitoring. Risk Profile risk Renal HIGH — Hyperkalemia, Acute Kidney Injury (AKI) if initiated in bilateral renal artery stenosis or hypovolemia. Respiratory HIGH — Persistent, dry, tickly cough (Occurs in ~10-15% of patients due to bradykinin accumulation). Immunological CRITICAL — Angioedema (swelling of lips/tongue/airway). Can occur years after starting. Dosing & Administration dose Hypertension / Heart Failure Start **PO** 2.5 mg **OD**. Titrate up every 2-4 weeks to target 5-10 mg **OD**. Renal Impairment MANDATORY — Halve the dose if **eGFR** 30-60. Max 2.5 mg/day if **eGFR** < 30. Monitor K+ strictly. Formulation & Access form Brand Names Coversyl, Perindo Oral Routes Tablets (2.5, 5, 10 mg). Often combined with Amlodipine (Reaptan) or Indapamide (Coversyl Plus). Prescribing & PBS Unrestricted General Benefit (S4). Indications & Use ind Primary Hypertension, Heart Failure (HFrEF), prevention of progressive diabetic nephropathy, post-MI remodeling prevention. Clinical Place First-line agent for almost all cardiovascular and renal-protective indications. Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — History of ACEi-induced angioedema. Bilateral renal artery stenosis. Pregnancy ABSOLUTE — **Pregnancy** Category D. Must be ceased immediately upon conception. Key Interactions inter Triple Whammy CRITICAL — ACEi + Diuretic + NSAID (Ibuprofen/Naproxen). This combination guarantees acute renal failure by destroying both afferent and efferent glomerular blood flow. Pharmacodynamic HIGH — Potassium-sparing diuretics (Spironolactone), K+ supplements (Severe hyperkalemia). Monitoring & Cautions mon Laboratory MANDATORY — Check **U&E** (Creatinine, Potassium) 1-2 weeks after starting or changing the dose. A mild creatinine bump (<30%) is normal and protective; a large bump indicates AKI. Bedside Check lying and standing **BP**. Clinical Pearls pearl The Cough Switch If a patient develops the ACEi dry cough, DO NOT prescribe cough medicine. It will not work. Stop the Perindopril and switch them to an ARB (like Candesartan or Irbesartan), which does not affect bradykinin. The First-Dose Drop Patients taking high-dose diuretics are deeply reliant on the RAAS system to maintain blood pressure. Giving them their first dose of an ACEi can cause a sudden, massive syncopal crash (First-Dose Hypotension). The 30% Rule It is normal for creatinine to rise 15-30% after starting an ACEi — this is the intended effect of reducing glomerular hyperfiltration. Junior staff often panic and stop the drug unnecessarily. Only stop if creatinine rises > 30% from baseline, or potassium climbs above 5.5 mmol/L. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Competitively inhibits ACE, preventing the conversion of Angiotensin I to Angiotensin II (a potent vasoconstrictor). Also prevents the breakdown of bradykinin (leading to the classic dry cough). Onset **PO** 1-2 hours. Half-life Prodrug. Active metabolite (perindoprilat) has a half-life of ~17 hours. Renally cleared. Best Uses & Selection sel Ideal Patient Patient with hypertension plus any of: diabetic nephropathy, Heart Failure (HFrEF), post-MI remodelling, or proteinuria. Particularly valuable when a single agent needs to address both BP and renal protection simultaneously. Over ARBs (Candesartan) Choose Perindopril over ARBs when: cost is a significant concern (cheaper on PBS), or the patient has not previously experienced ACEi-related cough. ARBs are preferred when: cough develops, angioedema history, or patient preference. Over Other ACEis Longer half-life than Captopril (once-daily dosing improves adherence). Equivalent efficacy to Ramipril. Strong evidence specifically in stable coronary artery disease (EUROPA trial). Avoid When Avoid in: any patient planning or currently pregnant, bilateral renal artery stenosis, prior ACEi angioedema, hyperkalaemia > 5.5 mmol/L, severe aortic stenosis, or volume-depleted patients without ability to monitor **U&E** within 1-2 weeks. Pros & Cons comp Advantages Once-daily dosing (excellent adherence). Dual benefit: antihypertensive + renal-protective in diabetic nephropathy. Strong post-MI evidence (EUROPA trial). Cheap on PBS. Can be combined with almost all antihypertensive classes. Disadvantages Class-specific dry cough in 10-15% (often leads to switching). Requires baseline and ongoing **U&E** monitoring. Absolute contraindication in Pregnancy limits use in women of reproductive age. Risk of first-dose hypotension in volume-depleted patients. Monitoring Burden **U&E** at baseline, 1-2 weeks after starting and each dose change, then 6-12 monthly. Moderate burden vs calcium channel blockers (no monitoring needed), similar to ARBs. Adherence Fit Once daily, no food restrictions, widely available. Good adherence profile. Cough is the primary driver of non-adherence and switching. Overdose Profile Severe hypotension is the primary concern in overdose. Supportive care with **IV** fluids and vasopressors if needed. Not lethal in isolation but dangerous in combination with other antihypertensives. Not removed by dialysis. Sources src Source Review Source-backed review 2026-05-11: COVERSYL/perindopril Australian PI. Entry checked for dosing, access, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and app structure.\nClass & Role ACE Inhibitor — first-line antihypertensive, heart failure, renal protection Route / Formulation **PO** tablets: 2.5 mg, 5 mg, 10 mg (Coversyl, Perindo) Usual Dose & Max Start 2.5 mg **OD**, titrate to 5-10 mg **OD**. Max 10 mg/day. Key Indication Doses HF: start 2.5 mg **OD**, target 10 mg **OD**. Renal: halve if **eGFR** 30-60. Administration Take same time daily. Swallow whole. No food restriction. Missed dose: skip if < 4 h to next. Best Uses Hypertension + diabetic nephropathy, HFrEF, post-MI. Cheap on PBS. Once-daily. Avoid / Cautions Pregnancy (Category D), bilateral renal artery stenosis, prior ACEi angioedema, K+ > 5.5 mmol/L. Key Risks Dry cough 10-15%, first-dose hypotension, angioedema (rare but fatal), hyperkalaemia. Key Interactions Triple Whammy (ACEi + diuretic + NSAID = AKI). K+-sparing diuretics. Aliskiren in diabetes. Monitoring **U&E** at baseline, 1-2 weeks post-start, each dose change, then 6-12 monthly. **BP** at each visit. Clinical Pearl Creatinine rise up to 30% is expected and protective — only stop if > 30% or K+ > 5.5 mmol/L.","contentHash":"ff0bf068783e7a7c45cab23b4e077ecee4dcd34168b8aafe43dc6eef5bc2df7e","publicationFingerprint":"b07a612889a5c393837f1843bd58cbdc1b92c0ffd0c1e7bfefb680c82c4ec4a2","governanceFingerprint":"6473895da16d5508d03ffea998bc94f9de73dd719d70d551126b969e94bd3eab","lineageFingerprint":"4f53cda18c2baa0c0354bb5f9a3ecbe5ed12ab4d8e11ba873c2f11161202b945","publicMetadataFingerprint":"ce0c69ead0d6bf95eb4a0cb609563cf87bf9377cb66d10cbb310d0451b2b114c","record":{"version":"site-content-record-v1","logicalId":"medications:perindopril","producerClass":"dynamic_registry","domain":"medications","route":"/medications/perindopril","title":"Perindopril","body":"Medication: Perindopril\nClass: Antihypertensive\nSubclass: ACE Inhibitor\nSchedule: S4\nTag: ACEi\nRapid Summary summary Overview Long-acting Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE) inhibitor. The absolute gold-standard foundational therapy for hypertension, heart failure, and diabetic nephropathy. Maximum Dose Max **10 mg/day**. Reduce in Renal impairment — Max 2.5 mg/day if **eGFR** < 30. Clinical Focus Absolutely contraindicated in Pregnancy (Category D). Causes severe fetal renal agenesis and skull hypoplasia. Cease immediately on confirmed pregnancy. Bottom Line Perindopril is the preferred ACE inhibitor for most cardiovascular and renal-protective indications, but must never be used in Pregnancy and always requires **U&E** monitoring. Risk Profile risk Renal HIGH — Hyperkalemia, Acute Kidney Injury (AKI) if initiated in bilateral renal artery stenosis or hypovolemia. Respiratory HIGH — Persistent, dry, tickly cough (Occurs in ~10-15% of patients due to bradykinin accumulation). Immunological CRITICAL — Angioedema (swelling of lips/tongue/airway). Can occur years after starting. Dosing & Administration dose Hypertension / Heart Failure Start **PO** 2.5 mg **OD**. Titrate up every 2-4 weeks to target 5-10 mg **OD**. Renal Impairment MANDATORY — Halve the dose if **eGFR** 30-60. Max 2.5 mg/day if **eGFR** < 30. Monitor K+ strictly. Formulation & Access form Brand Names Coversyl, Perindo Oral Routes Tablets (2.5, 5, 10 mg). Often combined with Amlodipine (Reaptan) or Indapamide (Coversyl Plus). Prescribing & PBS Unrestricted General Benefit (S4). Indications & Use ind Primary Hypertension, Heart Failure (HFrEF), prevention of progressive diabetic nephropathy, post-MI remodeling prevention. Clinical Place First-line agent for almost all cardiovascular and renal-protective indications. Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — History of ACEi-induced angioedema. Bilateral renal artery stenosis. Pregnancy ABSOLUTE — **Pregnancy** Category D. Must be ceased immediately upon conception. Key Interactions inter Triple Whammy CRITICAL — ACEi + Diuretic + NSAID (Ibuprofen/Naproxen). This combination guarantees acute renal failure by destroying both afferent and efferent glomerular blood flow. Pharmacodynamic HIGH — Potassium-sparing diuretics (Spironolactone), K+ supplements (Severe hyperkalemia). Monitoring & Cautions mon Laboratory MANDATORY — Check **U&E** (Creatinine, Potassium) 1-2 weeks after starting or changing the dose. A mild creatinine bump (<30%) is normal and protective; a large bump indicates AKI. Bedside Check lying and standing **BP**. Clinical Pearls pearl The Cough Switch If a patient develops the ACEi dry cough, DO NOT prescribe cough medicine. It will not work. Stop the Perindopril and switch them to an ARB (like Candesartan or Irbesartan), which does not affect bradykinin. The First-Dose Drop Patients taking high-dose diuretics are deeply reliant on the RAAS system to maintain blood pressure. Giving them their first dose of an ACEi can cause a sudden, massive syncopal crash (First-Dose Hypotension). The 30% Rule It is normal for creatinine to rise 15-30% after starting an ACEi — this is the intended effect of reducing glomerular hyperfiltration. Junior staff often panic and stop the drug unnecessarily. Only stop if creatinine rises > 30% from baseline, or potassium climbs above 5.5 mmol/L. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Competitively inhibits ACE, preventing the conversion of Angiotensin I to Angiotensin II (a potent vasoconstrictor). Also prevents the breakdown of bradykinin (leading to the classic dry cough). Onset **PO** 1-2 hours. Half-life Prodrug. Active metabolite (perindoprilat) has a half-life of ~17 hours. Renally cleared. Best Uses & Selection sel Ideal Patient Patient with hypertension plus any of: diabetic nephropathy, Heart Failure (HFrEF), post-MI remodelling, or proteinuria. Particularly valuable when a single agent needs to address both BP and renal protection simultaneously. Over ARBs (Candesartan) Choose Perindopril over ARBs when: cost is a significant concern (cheaper on PBS), or the patient has not previously experienced ACEi-related cough. ARBs are preferred when: cough develops, angioedema history, or patient preference. Over Other ACEis Longer half-life than Captopril (once-daily dosing improves adherence). Equivalent efficacy to Ramipril. Strong evidence specifically in stable coronary artery disease (EUROPA trial). Avoid When Avoid in: any patient planning or currently pregnant, bilateral renal artery stenosis, prior ACEi angioedema, hyperkalaemia > 5.5 mmol/L, severe aortic stenosis, or volume-depleted patients without ability to monitor **U&E** within 1-2 weeks. Pros & Cons comp Advantages Once-daily dosing (excellent adherence). Dual benefit: antihypertensive + renal-protective in diabetic nephropathy. Strong post-MI evidence (EUROPA trial). Cheap on PBS. Can be combined with almost all antihypertensive classes. Disadvantages Class-specific dry cough in 10-15% (often leads to switching). Requires baseline and ongoing **U&E** monitoring. Absolute contraindication in Pregnancy limits use in women of reproductive age. Risk of first-dose hypotension in volume-depleted patients. Monitoring Burden **U&E** at baseline, 1-2 weeks after starting and each dose change, then 6-12 monthly. Moderate burden vs calcium channel blockers (no monitoring needed), similar to ARBs. Adherence Fit Once daily, no food restrictions, widely available. Good adherence profile. Cough is the primary driver of non-adherence and switching. Overdose Profile Severe hypotension is the primary concern in overdose. Supportive care with **IV** fluids and vasopressors if needed. Not lethal in isolation but dangerous in combination with other antihypertensives. Not removed by dialysis. Sources src Source Review Source-backed review 2026-05-11: COVERSYL/perindopril Australian PI. Entry checked for dosing, access, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and app structure.\nClass & Role ACE Inhibitor — first-line antihypertensive, heart failure, renal protection Route / Formulation **PO** tablets: 2.5 mg, 5 mg, 10 mg (Coversyl, Perindo) Usual Dose & Max Start 2.5 mg **OD**, titrate to 5-10 mg **OD**. Max 10 mg/day. Key Indication Doses HF: start 2.5 mg **OD**, target 10 mg **OD**. Renal: halve if **eGFR** 30-60. Administration Take same time daily. Swallow whole. No food restriction. Missed dose: skip if < 4 h to next. Best Uses Hypertension + diabetic nephropathy, HFrEF, post-MI. Cheap on PBS. Once-daily. Avoid / Cautions Pregnancy (Category D), bilateral renal artery stenosis, prior ACEi angioedema, K+ > 5.5 mmol/L. Key Risks Dry cough 10-15%, first-dose hypotension, angioedema (rare but fatal), hyperkalaemia. Key Interactions Triple Whammy (ACEi + diuretic + NSAID = AKI). K+-sparing diuretics. Aliskiren in diabetes. Monitoring **U&E** at baseline, 1-2 weeks post-start, each dose change, then 6-12 monthly. **BP** at each visit. Clinical Pearl Creatinine rise up to 30% is expected and protective — only stop if > 30% or K+ > 5.5 mmol/L.","sourceRole":"clinical_reference","access":"public","validationStatus":"unverified","sourceStatus":"current","sourceLineage":[],"publicationVersion":"b07a612889a5c393837f1843bd58cbdc1b92c0ffd0c1e7bfefb680c82c4ec4a2","contentHash":"ff0bf068783e7a7c45cab23b4e077ecee4dcd34168b8aafe43dc6eef5bc2df7e"},"renderPayload":{"slug":"perindopril","name":"Perindopril","class":"Antihypertensive","subclass":"ACE Inhibitor","category":"Cardiovascular - Antihypertensives","accent":"#0284c7","tag":"ACEi","schedule":"S4","stats":[{"label":"Max Dose","value":"10 mg/day","cls":"hi","flag":"hi"},{"label":"Half-life","value":"17 h (Active)","cls":"","flag":""},{"label":"Dry Cough","value":"COMMON","cls":"warn","flag":""},{"label":"Teratogenic","value":"CRITICAL RISK","cls":"hi","flag":"warn"}],"sections":[{"title":"Rapid Summary","type":"summary","rows":[{"key":"Overview","val":"Long-acting Angiotensin-Converting Enzyme (ACE) inhibitor. The absolute gold-standard foundational therapy for hypertension, heart failure, and diabetic nephropathy.","tags":[]},{"key":"Maximum Dose","val":"Max **10 mg/day**. Reduce in Renal impairment — Max 2.5 mg/day if **eGFR** < 30.","tags":[]},{"ke