ho cannot tolerate serotonergic side effects (sexual dysfunction, weight gain). Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — Concurrent MAOI use. Cardiovascular CAUTION — Uncontrolled hypertension, recent MI, severe BPH, glaucoma. Pregnancy CAUTION — **Pregnancy** Category B1. Key Interactions inter Pharmacodynamic CRITICAL — MAOIs (Fatal hypertensive crisis). Pharmacokinetic HIGH — Strong **CYP3A4** inhibitors (Clarithromycin, Ketoconazole) spike Reboxetine levels, worsening tachycardia and urinary retention. Monitoring & Cautions mon Bedside MANDATORY — Check **BP** and **HR** at baseline and follow-up. Monitor bladder output in elderly males. Laboratory No specific routine laboratory tests required. Clinical Pearls pearl The Sweating Trap Patients will frequently complain of profuse, drenching sweats on Reboxetine. This is a direct physiological result of excess noradrenaline on the sweat glands, not a fever or infection. Alpha-blockers (like Clonidine) can reduce this if the drug is otherwise working perfectly. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Selectively inhibits the reuptake of Noradrenaline (NET), increasing noradrenergic tone in the prefrontal cortex. Zero affinity for muscarinic, histaminic, or alpha-1 receptors. Onset 2-4 WEEKS. Half-life 13 hours. Metabolised by CYP3A4. Sources src Source Review Source Check - EDRONAX Australian PI/CMI, ARTG 521130, and PBS search checked 2026-05-10 for antidepressant batch appraisal: identity/access, indication, dosing, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and source-row status.\nClass & Role NRI — Highly selective Noradrenaline Reuptake Inhibitor (NRI). Route / Formulation Tablets (4 mg). Usually scored to halve. (Edronax) Usual Dose & Max Start **PO** 4 mg **BD** (morning and midday). Max 12 mg/day. Key Indication Doses Major Depressive Disorder: Start **PO** 4 mg **BD** (morning and midday). Max 12 mg/day. Elderly / Renal / Hepatic: Start at **PO** 2 mg **BD**. Max 4 mg/day in severe CKD/Cirrhosis. Best Uses Reboxetine is a selective noradrenaline reuptake inhibitor for depression, but has questionable efficacy compared to SSRIs and causes urinary retention, insomnia, and dry mouth. Avoid / Cautions Concurrent MAOI use. **Pregnancy** Category B1. Key Risks Genitourinary: Urinary retention/hesitancy (especially in men with BPH), testicular pain. Neurological: Insomnia (do not take at night), agitation, anxiety, intense hyperhidrosis (sweating). Cardiovascular: Tachycardia, palpitations, mild hypertension. Metabolic: Weight neutral, zero sexual dysfunction. Key Interactions MAOIs (Fatal hypertensive crisis). Strong **CYP3A4** inhibitors (Clarithromycin, Ketoconazole) spike Reboxetine levels, worsening tachycardia and urinary retention. Monitoring Check **BP** and **HR** at baseline and follow-up. Monitor bladder output in elderly males. No specific routine laboratory tests required. Clinical Pearl Patients will frequently complain of profuse, drenching sweats on Reboxetine. This is a direct physiological result of excess noradrenaline on the sweat glands, not a fever or infection. Alpha-blockers (like Clonidine) can reduce this if the drug is otherwise working perfectly.","sourceRole":"clinical_reference","access":"public","validationStatus":"unverified","sourceStatus":"current","sourceLineage":[],"publicationVersion":"020b9d845a633996ab6a850e4280f17709feeeba40c2239e08366a8ffdad8642","contentHash":"8b196d63e07917d604f216386d6e9cae1d67a8f2798b244bb5a2526e0dfd9eaa"},"renderPayload":{"slug":"reboxetine","name":"Reboxetine","class":"Antidepressant","subclass":"NRI","category":"Psychiatry - Antidepressants","accent":"#7c3aed","tag":"NRI","schedule":"S4","stats":[{"label":"Max Dose","value":"12 mg/day","cls":"hi","flag":"hi"},{"label":"Half-life","value":"13 h","cls":"","flag":""},{"label":"Activation","value":"HIGH","cls":"warn","flag":""},{"label":"Sweating","value":"SEVERE","cls":"warn","flag":""}],"sections":[{"t