ions of GABA (inhibitory neurotransmitter). Blocks voltage-gated sodium channels and T-type calcium channels. Onset **PO** Days for mania. **IV** Minutes for seizures. Half-life 14 hours. Highly protein bound. Extensively metabolised by the liver. Special Populations spec Pregnancy CRITICAL — **Pregnancy** Category X (effectively). The most teratogenic AED in common use. Risk of neural tube defects (~5%), fetal valproate syndrome (facial anomalies, limb defects, autism). NEVER initiate in a woman of childbearing potential without specialist discussion, documented counselling, and dual contraception under the Australian REMS-equivalent guidelines. Elderly MANDATORY — Hepatic enzyme activity declines with age. Valproate clearance is reduced. Start at 50% of standard dose and titrate slowly. High risk of confusion, tremor, and falls at standard doses. Renal Impairment CAUTION — Protein binding of Valproate is reduced in renal impairment (hypoalbuminaemia). Total plasma levels underestimate the free (active) fraction — a 'normal' total level may represent relative toxicity. Use free Valproate levels if available. Lactation CAUTION — Low transfer into breast milk (~1-10%). Generally considered compatible with breastfeeding by most guidelines; however, monitor infant for sedation. Sources src Source Review Source Check - EPILIM oral/VALPROATE-AFT IV ARTG/PI and PBS search checked 2026-05-10 for mood-stabiliser/anticonvulsant batch appraisal: identity/access, indication, dosing, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, pregnancy/lactation, patient-context metadata, and source-row status.\nClass & Role Fatty acid derivative — Broad-spectrum anticonvulsant and highly effective mood stabiliser for Bipolar Mania. Route / Formulation Enteric-coated tablets (100, 200, 500 mg). Crushable tablets (100 mg). CR Tablets (200, 500 mg). Oral liquid. (Epilim, Epilim CR, Valpro) Usual Dose & Max Start **PO** 500 mg **BD** (enteric-coated or CR). Titrate rapidly to target 