rior mortality benefit (PLATO trial), provided bleeding risk is acceptable. Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — Active bleeding, history of intracranial haemorrhage, severe hepatic impairment. Surgery MANDATORY — Cease 5 days prior to major elective surgery. Pregnancy CAUTION — **Pregnancy** Category B1. Key Interactions inter Pharmacokinetic HIGH — Metabolised by **CYP3A4**. Strong inhibitors (ketoconazole, clarithromycin) drastically increase bleeding risk. Pharmacodynamic CRITICAL — High-dose Aspirin (>150 mg/day) reduces the efficacy of Ticagrelor. Aspirin dose MUST be strictly 100 mg/day. Monitoring & Cautions mon Bedside MANDATORY — Monitor for bleeding. Reassure patients who experience mild shortness of breath without hypoxia. Laboratory Baseline **FBC**. Clinical Pearls pearl The Dyspnoea Panic Ticagrelor causes a unique sensation of breathlessness in ~15% of patients. If their SpO2, chest auscultation, and CXR are completely normal, this is a benign side effect. Do not blindly treat for APO. The BD Danger Because it is reversible and has a short half-life, missing a single dose of Ticagrelor leaves a fresh stent completely unprotected within 12 hours. Adherence is paramount. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Direct-acting, reversible P2Y12 receptor antagonist. Does not require hepatic activation. Onset **PO** 30 mins. Duration 3-4 Days (Platelet function recovers faster than with Clopidogrel due to reversibility). Half-life 7 hours (Requires strict **BD** dosing). Sources src Source Review Source Check - BRILINTA ARTG/PI and PBS search checked 2026-05-13 for high-risk opioid/general batch appraisal: identity/access, indication, dosing, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, pregnancy/lactation, patient-context metadata, and source-row status.\nClass & Role Antiplatelet — Highly potent, *reversible* P2Y12 ADP receptor antagonist. Route / Formulation Tablets (60 mg, 90 mg). (Brilinta) Usual Dose & Max **PO** 180 mg STAT (Loading dose), then 90 mg **BD** for 12 months. Max **90 mg BD** (Maintenance). Key Indication Doses Acute ACS / STEMI: **PO** 180 mg STAT (Loading dose), then 90 mg **BD** for 12 months. Extended Prevention: **PO** 60 mg **BD** (Can be used beyond 12 months post-MI in high-risk patients). Best Uses Ticagrelor is the preferred P2Y12 inhibitor in ACS with faster onset and superior outcomes to Clopidogrel, but causes dyspnoea and requires twice-daily dosing affecting adherence. Avoid / Cautions Active bleeding, history of intracranial haemorrhage, severe hepatic impairment. **Pregnancy** Category B1. Key Risks Haematological: Major bleeding (higher risk than Clopidogrel). Respiratory: Dyspnoea (shortness of breath). Very common, usually benign and transient, caused by adenosine accumulation in the lungs. Cardiovascular: Bradycardia, ventricular pauses (usually in the first week of therapy). Key Interactions Metabolised by **CYP3A4**. Strong inhibitors (ketoconazole, clarithromycin) drastically increase bleeding risk. High-dose Aspirin (>150 mg/day) reduces the efficacy of Ticagrelor. Aspirin dose MUST be strictly 100 mg/day. Monitoring Monitor for bleeding. Reassure patients who experience mild shortness of breath without hypoxia. Baseline **FBC**. Clinical Pearl Ticagrelor causes a unique sensation of breathlessness in ~15% of patients. If their SpO2, chest auscultation, and CXR are completely normal, this is a benign side effect. Do not blindly treat for APO.","sourceRole":"clinical_reference","access":"public","validationStatus":"unverified","sourceStatus":"current","sourceLineage":[],"publicationVersion":"024bfcbd376c66d2c6e4017e6582ab102812a30dc42d93d07834d301d816a555","contentHash":"cdb0e63eb6eb36c1a1240bcb4dae4efc226869289096b78308f85f0e0fb5cdf1"},"renderPayload":{"slug":"ticagrelor","name":"Ticagrelor","class":"Anticoagulant","subclass":"Antiplatelet","category":"Haematology - Antiplatelets","accent":"#0284c7","tag":"ANTIPLATELET","schedule":"S4","stats":[{"label":"Max Dose","value":"90 mg BD","cls":"hi","flag":"hi"},{"label":"Half-life","value":"7 h","cls":"","flag":""},{"label":"Dyspnoea","value":"COMMON","cls":"warn","flag":""},{"label":"Bleeding Risk","value":"HIGH","cls":"warn","flag":"warn"}],"sections":[{"title":"Rapid Summary","type":"summary","rows":[{"key":"Overview","val":"Highly potent, *reversible* P2Y12 ADP receptor antagonist. Faster and stronger than Clopidogrel, but carries a higher bleeding risk and requires twice-daily dosing.","tags":[]},{"key":"Maximum Dose","val":"Max **90 mg BD** (Maintenance).","tags":[]},{"key":"Clinical Focus","val":"Unlike Clopidogrel, it is NOT a prodrug. It works instantly and reliably in all genetic profiles.","tags":[]},{"key":"Bottom Line","val":"Ticagrelor is the preferred P2Y12 inhibitor in ACS with faster onset and superior outcomes to Clopidogrel, but causes dyspnoea and requires twice-daily dosing affecting adherence.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Risk Profile","type":"risk","rows":[{"key":"Haematological","val":"CRITICAL — Major bleeding (higher risk than Clopidogrel).","tags":[]},{"key":"Respiratory","val":"HIGH — Dyspnoea (shortness of breath). Very common, usually benign and transient, caused by adenosine accumulation in the lungs.","tags":[]},{"key":"Cardiovascular","val":"MODERATE — Bradycardia, ventricular pauses (usually in the first week of therapy).","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Dosing & Administration","type":"dose","rows":[{"key":"Acute ACS / STEMI","val":"**PO** 180 mg STAT (Loading dose), then 90 mg **BD** for 12 months.","tags":[]},{"key":"Extended Prevention","val":"**PO** 60 mg **BD** (Can be used beyond 12 months post-MI in high-risk patients).","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Formulation & Access","type":"form","rows":[{"key":"Brand Names","val":"Brilinta","tags":[]},{"key":"Oral Routes","val":"Tablets (60 mg, 90 mg).","tags":[]},{"key":"Prescribing & PBS","val":"Authority Required (PBS).","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Indications & Use","type":"ind","rows":[{"key":"Primary","val":"Acute coronary syndromes (STEMI, NSTEMI, Unstable Angina) alongside Aspirin.","tags":["PBS","TGA"]},{"key":"Clinical Place","val":"Preferred over Clopidogrel for ACS in WA Health due to superior mortality benefit (PLATO trial), provided bleeding risk is acceptable.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Contraindications & Populations","type":"contra","rows":[{"key":"Absolute","val":"CRITICAL — Active bleeding, history of intracranial haemorrhage, severe hepatic impairment.","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["hepatic"],"action":"contraindication","severity":"danger","note":"Inferred from existing decision-section wording; review before clinical use."}},{"key":"Surgery","val":"MANDATORY — Cease 5 days prior to major elective surgery.","tags":[]},{"key":"Pregnancy","val":"CAUTION — **Pregnancy** Category B1.","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["pregnancy"],"action":"caution","severity":"caution","note":"Ticagrelor pregnancy row is a benefit-risk caution, not an automatic contraindication."}}]},{"title":"Key Interactions","type":"inter","rows":[{"key":"Pharmacokinetic","val":"HIGH — Metabolised by **CYP3A4**. Strong inhibitors (ketoconazole, clarithromycin) drastically increase bleeding risk.","tags":[]},{"key":"Pharmacodynamic","val":"CRITICAL — High-dose Aspirin (>150 mg/day) reduces the efficacy of Ticagrelor. Aspirin dose MUST be strictly 100 mg/day.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Monitoring & Cautions","type":"mon","rows":[{"key":"Bedside","val":"MANDATORY — Monitor for bleeding. Reassure patients who experience mild shortness of breath without hypoxia.","tags":[]},{"key":"Laboratory","val":"Baseline **FBC**.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Clinical Pearls","type":"pearl","rows":[{"key":"The Dyspnoea Panic","val":"Ticagrelor causes a unique sensation of breathlessness in ~15% of patients. If their SpO2, chest auscultation, and CXR are completely normal, this is a benign side effect. Do not blindly treat for APO.","tags":[]},{"key":"The BD Danger","val":"Because it is reversible and has a short half-life, missing a single dose of Ticagrelor leaves a fresh stent completely unprotected within 12 hours. Adherence is paramount.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics","type":"evid","rows":[{"key":"Mechanism","val":"Direct-acting, reversible P2Y12 receptor antagonist. Does not require hepatic activation.","tags":[]},{"key":"Onset","val":"**PO** 30 mins.","tags":[]},{"key":"Duration","val":"3-4 Days (Platelet function recovers faster than with Clopidogrel due to reversibility).","tags":[]},{"key":"Half-life","val":"7 hours (Requires strict **BD** dosing).","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Sources","type":"src","rows":[{"key":"Source Review","val":"Source Check - BRILINTA ARTG/PI and PBS search checked 2026-05-13 for high-risk opioid/general batch appraisal: identity/access, indication, dosing, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, pregnancy/lactation, patient-context metadata, and source-row status."}]}],"quick":[{"label":"Class & Role","value":"Antiplatelet — Highly potent, *reversible* P2Y12 ADP receptor antagonist."},{"label":"Route / Formulation","value":"Tablets (60 mg, 90 mg). (Brilinta)"},{"label":"Usual Dose & Max","value":"**PO** 180 mg STAT (Loading dose), then 90 mg **BD** for 12 months. Max **90 mg BD** (Maintenance)."},{"label":"Key Indication Doses","value":"Acute ACS / STEMI: **PO** 180 mg STAT (Loading dose), then 90 mg **BD** for 12 months.  Extended Prevention: **PO** 60 mg **BD** (Can be used beyond 12 months post-MI in high-risk patients)."},{"label":"Best Uses","value":"Ticagrelor is the preferred P2Y12 inhibitor in ACS with faster onset and superior outcomes to Clopidogrel, but causes dyspnoea and requires twice-daily dosing affecting adherence."},{"label":"Avoid / Cautions","value":"Active bleeding, history of intracranial haemorrhage, severe hepatic impairment. **Pregnancy** Category B1."},{"label":"Key Risks","value":"Haematological: Major bleeding (higher risk than Clopidogrel).  Respiratory: Dyspnoea (shortness of breath). Very common, usually benign and transient, caused by adenosine accumulation in the lungs.  Cardiovascular: Bradycardia, ventricular pauses (usually in the first week of therapy)."},{"label":"Key Interactions","value":"Metabolised by **CYP3A4**. Strong inhibitors (ketoconazole, clarithromycin) drastically increase bleeding risk.  High-dose Aspirin (>150 mg/day) reduces the efficacy of Ticagrelor. Aspirin dose MUST be strictly 100 mg/day."},{"label":"Monitoring","value":"Monitor for bleeding. Reassure patients who experience mild shortness of breath without hypoxia.  Baseline **FBC**."},{"label":"Clinical Pearl","value":"Ticagrelor causes a unique sensation of breathlessness in ~15% of patients. If their SpO2, chest auscultation, and CXR are completely normal, this is a benign side effect. Do not blindly treat for APO."}]}},{"logicalId":"medications:tiotropium","logicalDocumentId":"915ac299-6438-50e0-a473-ed4bdabd4521","logicalChunkId":"46dfe5b8-d587-5460-81b8-f40c5221cf8e","normalizedText":"Medication: Tiotropium\nClass: Respiratory\nSubclass: LAMA\nSchedule: S4\nTag: LAMA\nRapid Summary summary Overview Long-Acting Muscarinic Antagonist (LAMA). The gold-standard baseline maintenance bronchodilator for COPD. Maximum Dose Max **18 mcg/day** (HandiHaler) or **5 mcg/day** (Respimat). Clinical Focus Never use for acute bronchospasm. Prevent patients from accidentally swallowing the HandiHaler capsules. Bottom Line Tiotropium is the gold-standard long-acting anticholinergic for COPD maintenance and severe asthma add-on, but provides no acute relief and must not be used as a rescue inhaler. Risk Profile risk Gastrointestinal HIGH — Dry mouth (usually mild but persistent), constipation. Genitourinary MODERATE — Urinary retention in susceptible patients (BPH). Cardiovascular LOW — Mild tachycardia, rare arrhythmias. Dosing & Administration dose COPD Maintenance **Inhaled** 18 mcg **OD** (inhale contents of 1 capsule via HandiHaler) OR 2 puffs (2.5 mcg/puff) **OD** via Respimat. Asthma Maintenance **Inhaled** 2 puffs (2.5 mcg/puff) **OD** via Respimat (add-on to ICS/LABA). Formulation & Access form Brand Names Spiriva HandiHaler, Spiriva Respimat Oral Routes Dry powder capsules for inhalation (18 mcg). Soft mist inhaler (2.5 mcg/puff). Prescribing & PBS Authority Required (PBS) for COPD. Indications & Use ind Primary Maintenance treatment of COPD, add-on maintenance for severe asthma. Clinical Place First-line monotherapy for symptomatic COPD. Significantly reduces COPD exacerbations and hospitalisations. Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — Do not use for acute bronchospasm relief. Renal Impairment CAUTION — Predominantly renally excreted; monitor closely in moderate-to-severe renal impairment (**CrCl** ≤ 50). Pregnancy CAUTION — **Pregnancy** Category B1. Key Interactions inter Pharmacodynamic MODERATE — Additive effects with other anticholinergics (e.g., Oxybutynin, Amitriptyline). Avoid concurrent use with Ipratropium. Monitoring & Cautions mon Bedside Check inhaler technique regularly. Monitor for severe dry mouth leading to dental caries. Laboratory No specific routine laboratory tests required. Clinical Pearls pearl The Swallowing Trap Ward patients routinely mistake the HandiHaler capsules for oral medications and swallow them. They must be pierced and inhaled. This is a massive cause of treatment failure. LAMA over LABA In COPD, LAMAs (like Tiotropium) generally outperform LABAs in reducing exacerbation rates. They are the backbone of therapy. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Binds to M3 muscarinic receptors in the airways, causing prolonged bronchodilation. Dissociates extremely slowly from M3, providing 24-hour coverage. Onset **Inhaled** 30 mins. Max effect takes up to 4 weeks of daily use. Duration > 24 h. Half-life 5-6 Days (Allows true once-daily dosing). Sources src Source Review Source-backed review 2026-05-11: SPIRIVA/tiotropium Australian PI source pack. Entry checked for dosing, access, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and app structure.\nClass & Role LAMA — Long-Acting Muscarinic Antagonist (LAMA). Route / Formulation Dry powder capsules for inhalation (18 mcg). Soft mist inhaler (2.5 mcg/puff). (Spiriva HandiHaler, Spiriva Respimat) Usual Dose & Max **Inhaled** 18 mcg **OD** (inhale contents of 1 capsule via HandiHaler) OR 2 puffs (2.5 mcg/puff) **OD** via Respimat. Max **18 mcg/day** (HandiHaler) or **5 mcg/day** (Respimat). Key Indication Doses COPD Maintenance: **Inhaled** 18 mcg **OD** (inhale contents of 1 capsule via HandiHaler) OR 2 puffs (2.5 mcg/puff) **OD** via Respimat. Asthma Maintenance: **Inhaled** 2 puffs (2.5 mcg/puff) **OD** via Respimat (add-on to ICS/LABA). Best Uses Tiotropium is the gold-standard long-acting anticholinergic for COPD maintenance and severe asthma add-on, but provides no acute relief and must not be used as a rescue inhaler. Avoid / Cautions Do not use for acute bronchospasm relief. **Pregnancy** Category B1. Key Risks Gastrointestinal: Dry mouth (usually mild but persistent), constipation. Genitourinary: Urinary retention in susceptible patients (BPH). Cardiovascular: Mild tachycardia, rare arrhythmias. Key Interactions Additive effects with other anticholinergics (e.g., Oxybutynin, Amitriptyline). Avoid concurrent use with Ipratropium. Monitoring Check inhaler technique regularly. Monitor for severe dry mouth leading to dental caries. No specific routine laboratory tests required. Clinical Pearl Ward patients routinely mistake the HandiHaler capsules for oral medications and swallow them. They must be pierced and inhaled. This is a massive cause of treatment failure.","contentHash":"3f555752bd9d2153cd18da228f6877d3f448caef6b5ff4a760f00ce1f3f29473","publicationFingerprint":"5b24c6ad12b81405ba21a04ff8c31328685d86bc5acb86600d7bd3d4a5d0074a","governanceFingerprint":"6473895da16d5508d03ffea998bc94f9de73dd719d70d551126b969e94bd3eab","line