nc for > 3 months, you MUST check Serum Copper levels to prevent irreversible neuropathy. Clinical Pearls pearl The Iron Clash Zinc and Iron compete for the exact same transporter in the gut. If you give a patient Zincaps and Ferro-gradumet at the same time, neither will absorb properly. Take them at different times of the day. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Cofactor for collagenase and DNA/RNA polymerases. In the gut, it induces metallothionein in enterocytes, which binds copper tightly, preventing copper from entering the blood (exploited in Wilson's disease). Onset Days to weeks. Half-life Excreted primarily in feces. Sources src Source Review Source-backed review 2026-05-12: NHMRC/Eat For Health zinc nutrient reference source pack. Entry checked for dosing, access, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and app structure.\nClass & Role Trace Element — Essential trace element required for over 300 metalloenzymes, DNA synthesis, and immune function. Route / Formulation Capsules/Tablets. Effervescent tablets. (Zincaps) Usual Dose & Max **PO** 220 mg (50 mg elemental) **OD** or **BD**. Take with food to prevent severe nausea. Max **220 mg/day** (equivalent to 50 mg elemental zinc). Key Indication Doses Zinc Deficiency / Wound Healing: **PO** 220 mg (50 mg elemental) **OD** or **BD**. Take with food to prevent severe nausea. Wilson's Disease (Maintenance): **PO** 50 mg elemental **TDS** (Acts to block copper absorption). Best Uses Zinc sulfate is used for zinc deficiency, wound healing support, and acute diarrhoea in children, but causes significant nausea on an empty stomach and copper deficiency with chronic use. Avoid / Cautions NONE — In acute deficiency. Essential nutrient. Key Risks Gastrointestinal: Intense nausea, vomiting, metallic taste (almost guaranteed if taken on an empty stomach). Neurological: Copper deficiency myeloneuropathy (mimics B12 deficiency paralysis) if used chronically at high doses. Key Interactions Binds and destroys the absorption of Tetracyclines (Doxycycline) and Fluoroquinolones (Ciprofloxacin). Separate by 4 hours. Monitoring If keeping a patient on Zinc for > 3 months, you MUST check Serum Copper levels to prevent irreversible neuropathy. Clinical Pearl Zinc and Iron compete for the exact same transporter in the gut. If you give a patient Zincaps and Ferro-gradumet at the same time, neither will absorb properly. Take them at different times of the day.","sourceRole":"clinical_reference","access":"public","validationStatus":"unverified","sourceStatus":"current","sourceLineage":[],"publicationVersion":"d38834843cdf9ceea8ef09d6f77a26229eeb80294afac6b2b2847a7dad9b1e4e","contentHash":"291a73e62812dc34fcf0805dd57b9d8d8e1eef802cb03fa527da529cbd37d1cf"},"renderPayload":{"slug":"zinc-sulfate","name":"Zinc sulfate","class":"Mineral","subclass":"Trace Element","category":"Vitamins & Minerals","accent":"#0891b2","tag":"MINERAL","schedule":"UNSCHEDULED","stats":[{"label":"Max Dose","value":"220 mg/day","cls":"hi","flag":"hi"},{"label":"Copper Defic.","value":"CHRONIC RISK","cls":"warn","flag":"warn"},{"label":"Nausea","value":"SEVERE (EMPTY STOMACH)","cls":"warn","flag":""},{"label":"Wound Healing","value":"INDICATED","cls":"good","flag":""}],"sections":[{"title":"Rapid Summary","type":"summary","rows":[{"key":"Overview","val":"Essential trace element required for over 300 metalloenzymes, DNA synthesis, and immune function. Critical for severe wound healing.","tags":[]},{"key":"Maximum Dose","val":"Max **220 mg/day** (equivalent to 50 mg elemental zinc).","tags":[]},{"key":"Clinical Focus","val":"Long-term high-dose zinc severely blocks Copper absorption, causing irreversible neurological damage from copper deficiency.","tags":[]},{"key":"Bottom Line","val":"Zinc sulfate is used for zinc deficiency, wound healing support, and acute diarrhoea in children, but causes significant nausea on an empty stomach and copper deficiency with chronic use.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Risk Profile","type":"risk","rows":[{"key":"Gastrointestinal","val":"HIGH — Intense nausea, vomiting, metallic taste (almost guaranteed if taken on an empty stomach).","tags":[]},{"key":"Neurological","val":"CRITICAL — Copper deficiency myeloneuropathy (mimics B12 deficiency paralysis) if used chronically at high doses.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Dosing & Administration","type":"dose","rows":[{"key":"Zinc Deficiency / Wound Healing","val":"**PO** 220 mg (50 mg elemental) **OD** or **BD**. Take with food to prevent severe nausea.","tags":[]},{"key":"Wilson's Disease (Maintenance)","val":"**PO** 50 mg elemental **TDS** (Acts to block copper absorption).","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Formulation & Access","type":"form","rows":[{"key":"Brand Names","val":"Zincaps","tags":[]},{"key":"Oral Routes","val":"Capsules/Tablets. Effervescent tablets.","tags":[]},{"key":"Prescribing & PBS","val":"Over The Counter (OTC).","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Indications & Use","type":"ind","rows":[{"key":"Primary","val":"Zinc deficiency, severe burns, massive pressure ulcers, Wilson's disease.","tags":["TGA"]},{"key":"Clinical Place","val":"Routinely added to the drug chart of ICU or geriatric patients with massive, non-healing bedsores to fuel collagen synthesis.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Contraindications & Populations","type":"contra","rows":[{"key":"Absolute","val":"NONE — In acute deficiency.","tags":[]},{"key":"Pregnancy","val":"SAFE — Essential nutrient at recommended intake/replacement doses; reserve prolonged high-dose zinc for documented deficiency and monitor copper status.","tags":[],"patient":{"factors":["pregnancy"],"action":"info","severity":"info","note":"Zinc is pregnancy-compatible at recommended doses; prolonged high-dose therapy needs copper monitoring."}}]},{"title":"Key Interactions","type":"inter","rows":[{"key":"Pharmacokinetic","val":"CRITICAL — Binds and destroys the absorption of Tetracyclines (Doxycycline) and Fluoroquinolones (Ciprofloxacin). Separate by 4 hours.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Monitoring & Cautions","type":"mon","rows":[{"key":"Laboratory","val":"MANDATORY — If keeping a patient on Zinc for > 3 months, you MUST check Serum Copper levels to prevent irreversible neuropathy.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Clinical Pearls","type":"pearl","rows":[{"key":"The Iron Clash","val":"Zinc and Iron compete for the exact same transporter in the gut. If you give a patient Zincaps and Ferro-gradumet at the same time, neither will absorb properly. Take them at different times of the day.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics","type":"evid","rows":[{"key":"Mechanism","val":"Cofactor for collagenase and DNA/RNA polymerases. In the gut, it induces metallothionein in enterocytes, which binds copper tightly, preventing copper from entering the blood (exploited in Wilson's disease).","tags":[]},{"key":"Onset","val":"Days to weeks.","tags":[]},{"key":"Half-life","val":"Excreted primarily in feces.","tags":[]}]},{"title":"Sources","type":"src","rows":[{"key":"Source Review","val":"Source-backed review 2026-05-12: NHMRC/Eat For Health zinc nutrient reference source pack. Entry checked for dosing, access, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and app structure."}]}],"quick":[{"label":"Class & Role","value":"Trace Element — Essential trace element required for over 300 metalloenzymes, DNA synthesis, and immune function."},{"label":"Route / Formulation","value":"Capsules/Tablets. Effervescent tablets. (Zincaps)"},{"label":"Usual Dose & Max","value":"**PO** 220 mg (50 mg elemental) **OD** or **BD**. Take with food to prevent severe nausea. Max **220 mg/day** (equivalent to 50 mg elemental zinc)."},{"label":"Key Indication Doses","value":"Zinc Deficiency / Wound Healing: **PO** 220 mg (50 mg elemental) **OD** or **BD**. Take with food to prevent severe nausea.  Wilson's Disease (Maintenance): **PO** 50 mg elemental **TDS** (Acts to block copper absorption)."},{"label":"Best Uses","value":"Zinc sulfate is used for zinc deficiency, wound healing support, and acute diarrhoea in children, but causes significant nausea on an empty stomach and copper deficiency with chronic use."},{"label":"Avoid / Cautions","value":"NONE — In acute deficiency. Essential nutrient."},{"label":"Key Risks","value":"Gastrointestinal: Intense nausea, vomiting, metallic taste (almost guaranteed if taken on an empty stomach).  Neurological: Copper deficiency myeloneuropathy (mimics B12 deficiency paralysis) if used chronically at high doses."},{"label":"Key Interactions","value":"Binds and destroys the absorption of Tetracyclines (Doxycycline) and Fluoroquinolones (Ciprofloxacin). Separate by 4 hours."},{"label":"Monitoring","value":"If keeping a patient on Zinc for > 3 months, you MUST check Serum Copper levels to prevent irreversible neuropathy."},{"label":"Clinical Pearl","value":"Zinc and Iron compete for the exact same transporter in the gut. If you give a patient Zincaps and Ferro-gradumet at the same time, neither will absorb properly. Take them at different times of the day."}]}},{"logicalId":"medications:ziprasidone","logicalDocumentId":"a8b0a109-260f-5c75-a6cf-b756090fe7c3","logicalChunkId":"ec3b09c9-2634-5e4e-b0fc-8824ab74aca8","normalizedText":"Medication: Ziprasidone\nClass: Antipsychotic\nSubclass: SGA / Benzisothiazolyl\nSchedule: S4\nTag: SGA\nRapid Summary summary Overview Second-Generation Antipsychotic (SGA). Highly weight-neutral and non-sedating, but structurally infamous for dose-dependent QTc prolongation and strict caloric absorption requirements. Maximum Dose Max **160 mg/day** (e.g., 80 mg BD). Clinical Focus Must be taken with a substantial meal (> 500 calories). If taken on an empty stomach, absorption drops by 50% and the patient will relapse into psychosis. Bottom Line Ziprasidone is an atypical antipsychotic with low metabolic risk, but carries the highest QTc prolongation risk of the atypicals and must be taken with food for adequate absorption. Risk Profile risk Cardiovascular CRITICAL — **QTc** prolongation. Carries one of the highest baseline QTc prolongation risks of the oral SGAs. Torsades de Pointes risk. Neurological MODERATE — Akathisia, extrapyramidal side effects (EPS), insomnia, dizziness. Metabolic SAFE — Excellent metabolic profile. Weight-neutral, lipid-neutral. Dosing & Administration dose Schizophrenia / Bipolar Mania Start **PO** 40 mg **BD** WITH FOOD. Titrate to 80 mg **BD**. Max 160 mg/day. Renal / Hepatic Impairment SAFE — No dose adjustment required in renal failure. Hepatic clearance is robust. Formulation & Access form Brand Names Zeldox Oral Routes Capsules (20, 40, 60, 80 mg). Prescribing & PBS Authority Required (PBS). Indications & Use ind Primary Schizophrenia, Bipolar I mania or mixed episodes. Clinical Place Preferred for patients who suffer severe weight gain or dyslipidemia on Olanzapine/Quetiapine, provided they have a healthy heart. Contraindications & Populations contra Absolute CRITICAL — Recent myocardial infarction, uncompensated heart failure, baseline **QTc** > 500 ms, concurrent use of other QTc-prolonging drugs (e.g., Sotalol, Amiodarone, Macrolides). Pregnancy CAUTION — **Pregnancy** Category C. Key Interactions inter Pharmacodynamic CRITICAL — Additive QTc prolongation with SSRIs, TCAs, and antiarrhythmics. Pharmacokinetic HIGH — **CYP3A4** inhibitors (Ketoconazole) increase levels; inducers (Carbamazepine) drastically reduce levels. Monitoring & Cautions mon Cardiac MANDATORY — Baseline and routine **ECG** to measure QTc interval. Monitor **U&E** (potassium/magnesium must be normal). Bedside MANDATORY — Ensure the patient is actually eating a heavy meal with the dose. Clinical Pearls pearl The Calorie Counter Ziprasidone is highly lipophilic. If a patient takes it with a piece of toast or a black coffee, it will pass through unabsorbed. It physically requires a ~500 kcal, fat-containing meal to dissolve and enter the blood. The Activating Edge Because it blocks noradrenaline/serotonin reuptake, it can be activating. Avoid giving the second dose right before bed if it causes insomnia; shift it to dinner time. Mechanism & Pharmacokinetics evid Mechanism Antagonist at D2 and 5-HT2A receptors. Also acts as a 5-HT1A agonist and blocks serotonin/noradrenaline reuptake (mild antidepressant effect). Onset **PO** 1-3 hours. Half-life 7 hours (Requires strictly **BD** dosing). Metabolised by aldehyde oxidase and CYP3A4. Sources src Source Review Source Check - ZELDOX ARTG/PI and PBS search checked 2026-05-10 for antipsychotic/LAI batch appraisal: identity/access, indication, dosing, contraindications/populations, interactions, monitoring, patient-context metadata, and source-row status.\nClass & Role SGA / Benzisothiazolyl — Second-Generation Antipsychotic (SGA). Route / Formulation Capsules (20, 40, 60, 80 mg). (Zeldox) Usual Dose & Max Start **PO** 40 mg **BD** WITH FOOD. Titrate to 80 mg **BD**. Max 160 mg/day. Key Indication Doses Schizophrenia / Bipolar Mania: Start **PO** 40 mg **BD** WITH FOOD. Titrate to 80 mg **BD**. Max 160 mg/day. Renal / Hepatic Impairment: No dose adjustment required in renal failure. Hepatic clearance is robust. Best Uses Ziprasidone is an atypical antipsychotic with low metabolic risk, but carries the highest QTc prolongation risk of the atypicals and must be taken with food for adequate absorption. Avoid / Cautions Recent myocardial infarction, uncompensated heart failure, baseline **QTc** > 500 ms, concurrent use of other QTc-prolonging drugs (e.g., Sotalol, Amiodarone, Macrolides). **Pregnancy** Category C. Key Risks Cardiovascular: **QTc** prolongation. Carries one of the highest baseline QTc prolongation risks of the oral SGAs. Torsades de Pointes risk. Neurological: Akathisia, extrapyramidal side effects (EPS), insomnia, dizziness. Metabolic: Excellent metabolic profile. Weight-neutral, lipid-neutral. Key Interactions Additive QTc prolongation with SSRIs, TCAs, and antiarrhythmics. **CYP3A4** inhibitors (Ketoconazole) increase levels; inducers (Carbamazepine) drastically reduce levels. Monitoring Baseline and routine **ECG** to measure QTc interval. Monitor **U&E** (potassium/magnesium must be normal). Ensure the patient is actually eating a heavy meal with the dose. Clinical Pearl Ziprasidone is highly lipophilic. If a patient takes it with a piece of toast or a black coffee, it will pass through unabsorbed. It physically requires a ~500 kcal, fat-containing meal to dissolve and enter the blood.","contentHash":"2879cf6d5919e5cd18564b510b48a4cd6c2f718ee6e78c9bce25bd9cb345be7b","publicationFingerprint":"6ec8b13c82a808cb5a892190e0975be5711aad69202fd7a0d9d50ba40ec6b2f2","governanceFingerprint":"6473895da16d5508d03ffea998bc94f9de73dd719d70d551126b969e94bd3eab","lineageFingerprint":"4f53cda18c2baa0c0354bb5f9a3ecbe5ed12ab4d8e11ba873c2f11161202b945","publicMetadataFingerprint":"d1111bf55164dd75a49f3f0aea84fb28ecdef4c4bef2732554949becf5a757eb","record":{"version":"site-content-record-v1","logicalId":"medications:ziprasidone","producerClass":"dynamic_registry","domain":"medications","route":"/medications/ziprasidone","title":"Ziprasidone","body":"Medication: Ziprasidone\nClass: Antipsychotic\nSubclass: SGA / Benzisothiazolyl\nSchedule: S4\nTag: SGA\nRapid Summary summary Overview Second-Generation Antipsychotic (SGA). Highly weight-neutral and non-sedating, but structurally infamous for dose-dependent QTc prolongation and strict caloric absorption requirements. Maximum Dose Max **160 mg/day** (e.g., 80 mg BD). Clinical Focus Must be taken with a substantial meal (> 500 calories). If taken on an empty stomach, absorption drops by 50% and the patient will relapse into psychosis. Bottom Line Ziprasidone is an atypical antipsychotic with low metabolic risk, but carries the highest QTc prolongation risk of the atypicals and must be taken with food for adequate absorption. Risk Profile risk Cardiovascular CRITICAL — **QTc** prolongation. Carries one of the highest baseline